window.WIKI_INDEX = [{"p":"","t":"Playing the game","u":"playing.html","x":""},{"p":"Playing the game","t":"Screens","u":"screens.html","x":""},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"Main menu","u":"screens--main-menu.html","x":"- First Single Player or Multiplayer, then Shop and Inventory (and Quit). Each has its own 3 save slots, each its own file, with Back to the choice. The Multiplayer page hosts its saves (see Multiplayer) and has Join a Game. Coming back from New Company returns to the same page. A slot with a save shows its company name (in the company color) and its company type, then its home port, money and game date (\"Rome · $2,500,000 · Sat 1 Jan 2000, 00:00\"), then its storms, seasonal closures and fastest game speed (\"Moderate storms · Seasonal closures · up to 8x\"); click it to continue that company, which then autosaves to the same slot. An empty slot says New Game. Each saved slot has a Delete button (with a confirmation). Saves that can't be loaded show greyed out with the reason and can only be deleted. - Loading screens: a dark sea with ships of every kind sailing across it, in from off one side and out off another and back on somewhere else, \"Maritime Empire\" and \"Loading\" with its dots, over everything. Shown when the game opens, till the title screen's scenes are loaded and the world's art is built (about 3 s), and when a save is continued, a company founded or a game joined, till the game is up and its map warm; at least 0.9 s, then fading out over 0.45 s. The scene changes behind it once it has been drawn, so the stall of building the map happens out of sight. - New Game → \"Choose your company type\" (five cards, see Company types; Next: Headquarters), then \"Found your company\" (its card says \"Company type:...\"; Back returns to the types): enter a company name, choose its headquarters (its home port: \"Choose your headquarters: click a port on the map. Your company starts there and new ships are delivered there...\", the pick shown as \"Headquarters: Rome, Italy\") and a company color, pick the fastest game speed the company may run at, pick its storms (None, Moderate or Harsh, default Moderate; see Weather), how many AI rivals and how well they play (see AI rivals) and whether to have Seasonal closures (on by default), choose whether to have the Tutorial (on by default; see Tutorial), then \"Next: Your CEO\": make the CEO, drawn in a clean cartoon style (bold dark outlines, flat colours with one soft shade, a big round head with big eyes) standing head to shoes beside a name box (with Random name), Male or Female buttons, Height and Build sliders, then the face parts in one column and the outfit in another, each stepped through with < and >: 10 skin colors, 8 eye shapes and 9 eye colors (separate), 10 noses, 10 mouths, 13 hair styles, 14 hair colors, 10 facial hair options (none included; a female body never gets a random beard), 18 tops leaning to the sea (captain's jacket, sailor stripes, pea coat, yellow oilskin, fisherman's jumper, dress whites, deck tee, life vest, wetsuit, officer's shirt, tropical shirt, pirate coat, then tees, hoodie, suit, flannel, hi-vis), 12 bottoms (navy trousers, sailor bell-bottoms, oilskin trousers, deck shorts, wetsuit legs, jeans, khakis, cargo, skirts, denim shorts), 10 shoes (deck shoes, sea boots, wellies, barefoot, boots, trainers, dress shoes, sandals), 10 glasses (none included) and 13 hats (none included: captain's cap, sailor cap, sou'wester, tricorne, bandana, watch cap, straw hat, bucket, baseball, top hat, hard hat, cowboy), with Randomize looks, then Back or Found Company. Random sliders are the sum of two rolls (mostly middling). The CEO is saved with the company. Storms and seasonal closures are saved with the company. The home port is picked on a globe filling the left of the screen (opening on the whole globe round Rome, the default), beside the form: only the 79 starting ports can be picked, named and colored by kind: shining bright green for the 5 \"Excellent starts\", the best in 5 different market regions (Conakry, Dublin, Shanghai, Cabo San Lucas, Rome); green for the next 20 \"Great starts\" (West Africa, the China coast and Taiwan, Korea, the Irish Sea and Biscay, the western Mediterranean, Manzanillo); yellow for the 20 \"Fair starts\" after them (Tunis, Busan, Praia, Marseille, Tonga and Samoa, the US East Coast, southern Italy and Sardinia, Barcelona, Monrovia, Durres, Misrata, Lazaro Cardenas, Madeira and the Azores, Kaohsiung); red for 20 \"Hard starts\", well-known ports where a Scooter still clears a thin profit (Jebel Ali, Hamad, Colombo, Santos, Rio, Tokyo, Yokohama, Dalian, Tianjin, Honolulu, Odesa, St Petersburg, Copenhagen, Tema, Balboa, King George Island, Constanta, Port Klang, Maputo; Singapore in place of Darwin, whose only Scooter run is to Dili in another region); and blue for the 14 Great Lakes ports, whose note, in blue, warns that they're a real challenge, far harder than any coast: every winter (with seasonal closures) the Seaway, Welland Canal and Soo Locks shut, Dec 31 to late March, cutting the lakes off from the sea, and from year 2 the lake ports freeze in too, mid-January to late February, unless you station an icebreaker (from $8M, level 8). Each recommended port's note gives its best Scooter run and about what each Scooter makes a minute. The form's legend explains the colors; every other port is a faint dot that can't be clicked. The picked port has a gold ring, and its name and its note (in its kind's color, up to six lines; all of it in its tooltip) show in the form. The ports not offered are still normal ports. The color is used for the company name in the top bar and the rings around the HQ and hubs. - Autosave on quit and every 30 s."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"Top bar (on World, Ships, Finances, Shop screens)","u":"screens--top-bar-on-world-ships-finances-shop-screens.html","x":"- Left: company level with an XP bar, and money. Middle: the company name (in the company color) above the calendar date and time (\"Sat 1 Jan 2000, 14:30\"). Right: the game speed buttons. - Calendar: a new company starts on 1 January 2000 at midnight. The clock runs 20 calendar minutes per second of play at 1x, matched to real ship speeds (a Scooter's 5.44 nm a second is about 16 knots), so voyages take realistic calendar times: Rome to Tunis about 20 hours, Shanghai to Rotterdam about a month; a calendar day passes in about 72 seconds. It follows the game speed (stops when paused). The route screen shows each leg's calendar time too (\"1 min 1 s (about 20 h)\"), a ship at sea shows when it arrives (\"arrives 3 Jan 09:10\"), and activity log lines are stamped with the calendar time. - Game speed: a button for each speed, Pause, 1x, 2x, 4x and 8x, up to the save's fastest game speed (chosen when founding it; a 2x save shows only Pause, 1x and 2x), with the current one highlighted; the F key steps through them in turn (back to Pause after the fastest), and Space pauses, then carries on at the speed it was paused from (neither while typing in a text box; in multiplayer only the host). Paused freezes the world; faster speeds run it that much faster (ships, breakdowns, XP, finances, prices); each session starts at 1x. - Esc (once nothing else wants it: an open popup or the Route Assignment screen close first) opens a menu over the dimmed game: Continue, Save (\"Saved at...\"), Settings, Exit to Menu and Exit to Desktop (both save first). In single player the game pauses while it's open and carries on at its speed after Continue (or Esc again); in multiplayer it keeps running. A joined player can't save (the host saves every company) and leaving keeps their company going; a host leaving ends the game for everyone, as Leave Game does. - Settings: a page over the dimmed menu in two tabs (Esc, X or a click outside closes it). General: \"Ships seen from afar\", one of Small dots (default: ships drawn their true size in the world, a 2 px dot once too small to make out), Bigger dots (5 px) or Ships grow as you zoom out; the Routes switch; Only my routes (off by default; on, the Routes switch draws only this player's own routes, not the other companies', players' or AI rivals', though anchored ships still keep off theirs); Day and night (on by default; off, it's always day: no dark side and no lights, the sun held still over the view's north-west so shadows fall south-east); Antialiasing: the Compatibility renderer has neither 2D MSAA nor FXAA, so it is one pass of our own over the screen under the map's labels, ships and birds: each pixel compares itself with its four neighbours and most stop there; on an edge the 3x3 round it gives the edge's direction and the pixel is blended, tent-weighted, with 3, 5 or 9 pixels along it, turning stair-steps on coasts, lakes, lanes, marinas and region shading into ramps (names in the map art soften a little at Medium and High). Off, the pass is hidden and costs nothing. Birds (on by default; off, no bird is drawn or can be clicked, though their calls still play); and the Tutorial switch in saves started with the tutorial. Audio: sliders 0-100% for Master, Sea and weather (the waves, wind and rain), Wildlife (the animals' calls) and Ship horns, each its own audio bus under Master (made by Settings). Routes and the tutorial are the company's, kept in its save. - Clicking the level (or its bar) opens a popup below it (click again to close): XP into the level and XP needed for the next, the company's XP per minute over the last 10 minutes, and \"Coming up\": the next level and the next few levels that unlock something (models, \"+1 slot:...\", \"a new hub\"), each with an estimate of how long until you reach it at that rate. Refreshes every second. - Clicking the money opens the Finances screen."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"Bottom navigation bar","u":"screens--bottom-navigation-bar.html","x":"- World Ships Finances Shop. Markets, Hubs, Farms and Makers are pages over the map (see 1. World (map) screen). Hidden on the Route Assignment screen. Esc opens the Esc menu from every tab. - The Ships tab shows a black dot (every alert dot on a button is black with a thin pale ring, to show on the red buttons) while any ship needs the player: lost with no recovery boat on the way, held in port, waiting at a canal for toll money, docked and paused, a docked cargo ship with no route, or upgrade points to spend. Its tooltip counts each reason and names the ships (\"1 held in port (Tundra Godwit)\", up to 3 names then \"and 2 more\"). The Ships screen filter \"Needs attention\" shows just those ships. - The Shop tab shows a black dot while any ship can be bought (unlocked, a free slot, and affordable); its tooltip lists them."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"1. World (map) screen","u":"screens--1-world-map-screen.html","x":"- Turn the globe (drag) and zoom (mouse wheel) supported (see World). - Pages over the map (2026-10-15; they were bottom-bar tabs): square buttons down the map's top left open Markets (a price chart), Hubs (a quayside crane), Farms (a wheat sheaf, Agriculture companies only), Makers (a factory, Vehicles companies only) and Industry (a mine cart heaped with ore, Cargo & Ore companies only), like the field guide, achievements and ratings buttons at its top right. Each page dims the map and fills most of it with a card holding the screen as it was, with an X in its corner; Esc, X or a click outside closes it, as does going to another tab. The port popup's Farms and Makers buttons open their pages. The bird call card at the map's top left sits beside the buttons. The Hubs page shows two hub cards across where they fit, else one. - Ports are clickable circles (sized by the port's size); ships are clickable top-down ship shapes moving along their routes. - The map's top-right corner holds the Prices picker (and Unlock a region over it while one can be unlocked), with no panel round them; the Routes and Tutorial switches are on the Settings page (below). The \"Routes\" switch (on by default, saved with the game) draws every sea lane the fleet uses as a faint line in the color of a ship using it: each cargo ship's route loop, any new route waiting to replace it, and the leg it's sailing now. A lane shared by several ships is drawn once. Where a port's marina shows, each line leaves the lane and curves round to the harbour mouth just as the ships do, keeping to the right-hand side of the entrance, and runs on in through the entrance, stopping where ships part for their berths; a lane sailed both ways shows both ways' curves (in on one side, out on the other) joining one line at sea. Ore carriers' lanes are whitened (their gray colors 65% towards white) and drawn stronger (70% opaque, others 45%), so they show on the dark deep sea. In multiplayer, other players' lanes are drawn the same way underneath, each company's in its own color (dark ones lifted toward white so they show), 25% opaque. Lanes are drawn, and the ships on them placed, just as the lanes are laid. Until 2026-10-13 each lane was bent out round the harbours of the ports it passed but wasn't sailing to (a keep-out zone 1.8 times each port's harbour mouth's distance from it, the lane pushed smoothly out of it, no further than the sea went), to keep the lines and ships out of other marinas and to leave quiet water for ships at anchor; that made the routes swerve and kink round every port on their way, and the ships lie in port now (see Long-term moorings), so it was taken out. - Only one popup open at a time; opening a new one closes the old one."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"2. Ships screen","u":"screens--2-ships-screen.html","x":"- Tabs over the list like the Shop's (2026-10-15): All, then a tab per company type (Cargo & Ore, Vehicles, Agriculture, Tankers, Seafood) and Service (recovery boats, tugs, icebreakers), each with how many of its ships the Show filter lets through (\"Tankers (17)\"); a company type's tab is left out while the company has no ships in it and doesn't have the type. The list opens on All. - Scrolling list grouped by model in the Shop's order (each type's lines, then the service ships, each line smallest first) under a heading per ship line in its color (\"Container ships\", \"Ore carriers\"...; hidden with nothing under it), each group on a faint tint of its ship line's color (the same colors as the Prices menu and Finances) under a picture of the model (bow to the right) and its name in that color, 8 compact tiles wide, one tile per owned ship. Groups with no ships (or none matching the filter) are hidden. - Each tile has its captain's face (a small round picture) beside three lines: the ship name (cut short with \"...\" if it doesn't fit), then its cargo and level in smaller text (\"Coffee · Lv 7\", in green with \"+2\" while it has upgrade points to spend; recovery boats say \"Recovery\"). A ship that needs the player for anything but upgrade points says why instead, in red (\"Held in port\", \"Paused\", \"No route\", \"Lost at sea\", \"Waiting for toll money\"). Then \"Capt. \" and its captain's name. Hovering a tile shows the full name and its status line (e.g. \"Docked at Korsakov — not enough fuel for Hong Kong; turn on refuel\"). - Each tile shows the ship name, a status dot, and thin maintenance / fuel / cargo bars. Green = running (has route, not paused, not held); amber = waiting in port for money for a full load; red = docked (paused at a port, no route, or held in port). - Clicking a tile opens the ship popup. - Sort (within each model, left to right) by earnings, name, status (lost, then at risk, then held, then stopped, then running) or profit all time; show all ships, running, stopped, lost, or recovery boats. A count shows how many are shown. Tiles re-sort when the fleet or the sort changes, not live. - An \"Upgrade all (N): $X\" button (N unspent points, $X what they'd all cost) buys the fleet's upgrades cheapest first, as far as the money goes without dipping into the bank reserve. Each ship's points go round-robin to its lowest skill path, ties in the order speed, efficiency, durability (so speed 1, efficiency 1, durability 1, speed 2,... up to all 30), which is also its cheapest. Red dot while at least the cheapest is affordable; greyed out (tooltip: the cheapest's price) when there's nothing to buy or it can't be afforded. Each model's header also has its own \"Upgrade N: $X\" button, left of its Mute, for just that model's ships (hidden while they have none to spend; greyed while the cheapest would dip into the bank reserve). - Mega upgrade: once the company owns all 8 of a model and every one is at level 30, a \"Mega upgrade: $1,000,000\" button appears to the right of the model's name (red dot when affordable; greyed, with the reason, when not). It costs 80x the model's price and can be bought once per model. Every ship of that model, including later ones, then gets +50% top speed and +50% profit on each profitable trade (on top of other bonuses); recovery boats get +50% top speed and burn 50% less fuel instead (Buffalo $100,000,000, Mammoth $800,000,000). The name then shows a gold \"★ Mega: +50% speed and profit\" (or \"+50% speed, -50% fuel\") badge, and the activity log announces it. Mega upgrades are a line in the Finances totals and are saved."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"Hubs page","u":"screens--hubs-page.html","x":"- A card for the HQ, then each hub: port name, Headquarters/Hub, level (of 40) with an XP bar and XP to the next level, the upgrade tree (points to spend, the four paths with pips, their bonuses and + buttons), its Industry section (see Headquarters and hubs), and traffic (ships docked there now, deliveries unloaded there, pay received there), with a \"Show on map\" button that centers the World map on it. - Then a \"New hub available\" card (how to found one, with a button to the map) while a hub can be founded, and a \"Next hub\" card with the company level that unlocks the next one."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"Finances screen","u":"screens--finances-screen.html","x":"- Tabs at the top (they wrap onto a second row on a narrow screen): Company (the whole company: all below), one for each of the company's types (2026-10-15: Cargo & Ore, Vehicles, Agriculture, Tankers, Seafood, Cruises, Research, as it has them), and Compare companies. A type's page shows only that type: its totals are its ships' own money (sales, cargo bought, fuel, repairs, tolls and port fees, wages, from their ledgers all time and their share of the last 10 minutes) and their operating profit, then the type's own money (Cargo & Ore industry, Vehicles factories, Agriculture farms, Tankers rigs and refineries, Cruises supplies, Research contracts and gear) and the type's total; its Delivered card only its goods (the plain Cars, Trucks and Boats of older saves under Vehicles); its ship table only its lines (\"Profit by ship: Vehicles\"). The bank, loan, logo and canal rows are the Company page's only. Compare companies: a table of every company in the game, ranked and numbered by operating profit all time, the most first (that column marked ▼; this player's marked \"(you)\", names in their colors): company type (icon and name; see Company types), level, money, ships, hubs, operating profit over the last 10 minutes and all time, and units delivered. - On the Your company page, at the top, \"Keep in the bank\" (Nothing, $100,000, $250,000, $500,000, $1M, $2.5M, $5M, $10M, $25M, $50M or $100M; saved with the company, default Nothing): ships won't spend that money on cargo, so it builds up for buying ships or upgrades. Fuel and repairs can still use it, so no ship is stranded. A ship waiting for a full load says so (\"... (keeping $250,000 in the bank)\"). - Totals for the last 10 minutes and all time: cargo sales, cargo bought, fuel, repairs, canal tolls, ships bought, ships sold, hub upgrades, ship upgrades, mega upgrades, farm land bought and sold, farm costs, farm store sales, and operating profit (sales − cargo bought − fuel − repairs − tolls). Smaller text and tighter columns so it all fits. - Beside the totals, a \"Delivered\" card: everything the fleet has delivered, all time, a total for each unit (Containers, Tons, Head of livestock, Vehicles) with each commodity under it when a unit has several (Toys, Clothing...; Oil, Gas, Iron ore...). - Then a card per canal the fleet has used: \"Panama Canal: 12 crossings · tolls −$48,000 · bonus XP 3,100\" (bonus XP is the extra company XP from the canal bonus). - A table of every ship: model, sales, cargo bought, fuel, repairs, tolls, lifetime profit and profit over the last 10 minutes, grouped by ship line in shop order (container ships, ore, grain, livestock, tankers, vehicles, recovery boats). Each group sits on a faint band of its line's color and starts with a total row: the line's name in its color, its number of ships, and each column summed, in slightly bigger text. Click a column to sort the ships within each group; click a ship's name to open its ship popup. Refreshes every second. - The table's money is shortened to 3 figures from $100k up (\"$23.1B\", \"-$571M\", \"$985k\") so it fits the screen however big the numbers get; hover a figure for the exact amount. The totals card shows exact amounts. - The totals and the 10-minute window are saved with the game."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"3. Shop screen","u":"screens--3-shop-screen.html","x":"- Six tabs (since 2026-10-15, a tab per company type: Cargo & Ore with the container and ore carriers; Vehicles; Agriculture with the grain and livestock carriers; Tankers; Seafood with the fishing boats, crabbing boats and seafood carriers; then Service with the recovery boats, tugs and icebreakers), opening on the company's first type; a type the company lacks has a padlock on its tab, its cards faded and \"Comes with <type>\" on their buttons, under a note saying when a type can be chosen (with a Choose a company type button while one can), each a section per ship line laid out in rows with the sections of a row side by side, each as wide as its models, cards widening (up to 175 px) to fill rows with fewer ships (up to 6 cards a row, beside the open activity log at the default window): container ships; ore and grain carriers; livestock carriers and tankers; vehicle carriers and recovery boats; icebreakers and, in a wider section to their right, tugs. A row too wide for the window stacks its sections. Cards are compact (prices of $1M and up shortened: \"$15M\"), one per ship model, showing its stats, price, and how many are owned (\"Owned 2 / 10\"). Each section title fades letter by letter through its models' map colors, smallest ship first, and each card's name is in its model's map color (dark colors brightened a little so they read on the cards). Each section sits on a faint tint of its ship line's color (the same colors as the Ships tab, Finances and the Prices menu). The Tugs section has \"Buy all tugs (14)\": it fills every free tug slot the money allows, each tug given a suggested name and based at the route port that needs one most. - Under its name each card shows the ship as it looks on the map (its hull in the model's map color, container ships, car carriers and livestock carriers fully loaded), bow to the right, in a 44 px strip. The biggest model on the map (the Mammoth) is 150 px long and the rest scale by the square root of their map length, so small ships stay a good size: a Scooter 20 is about 72 px (a quarter of a Mammoth's length on the map, half here), a Dominator about 120. - Each card shows owned / slots at the current level (and the max, while it can still grow). A full model shows \"Next slot at level N\"; a locked model shows \"Unlocks at level N\". A red dot sits on each Buy button that can be used right now (unlocked, a free slot, and affordable). - A model with a first-purchase price shows it on the card (\"$150,000 for your first, then $1,250,000\"). Buy → naming popup with a suggested random name (editable, must be unique; a Random button suggests another) → confirm. Disabled if the player can't afford it: hovering it then shows a small note straight away, \"You can't afford this ship ($1,300 short).\" Shows \"Limit reached\" at the ownership limit."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"4. Route Assignment screen","u":"screens--4-route-assignment-screen.html","x":"- Full-screen gray version of the map; no top bar or nav bar. The side panel shows the ship's current route, its range, and the port it starts from. - Click ports in order to add waypoints; a side list shows ports in order plus estimated travel time and distance for each leg and the full loop. Legs through a canal add the lock time (6 chambers × 3 s through Panama, not counting any wait) and show the toll and XP bonus: \"to Balboa · 44 nm · 30 s · Panama Canal: toll $4,200, +50% XP\". - Rules: consecutive waypoints must differ; since routes loop, the last waypoint must also differ from the first. Every leg, including the one back to the start, must be one the ship can make, on one tank or by way of refuel stops (see Refuel stops). - Ports the ship can't reach from the last waypoint even refuelling on the way (or, before any are added, from the port it's docked at or heading to) are greyed out; clicking one explains why. Legs beyond one tank list their refuel stops in light blue and are drawn through them on the map (see Refuel stops). - Buttons: Cancel (always available), Undo last waypoint, Accept (enabled once 2+ waypoints and the route is valid). Each stop in the list has an X on the right that takes it out of the route (and one of a pair of the same port left side by side). - Assigning a new route to a ship at sea: it finishes its current leg, then follows the new route. - Assigning a route also un-pauses the ship. - A ship docked at a port that isn't on its new route first sails to the route's first port (a normal, paid leg; by way of refuel stops if it's beyond one tank). - Running ships leave a port when their port stop ends (and they have fuel for the next leg); paused or route-less ships stay docked."},{"p":"Playing the game / Screens","t":"Fishing Grounds screen (2026-10-14)","u":"screens--fishing-grounds-screen-2026-10-14.html","x":"- Opened from the Ships tab's Fishing Grounds button or a fishing boat's popup; full screen like the Route Assignment screen: the gray map on the left, a side panel on the right, Done or Esc to go back. - On the map, every fishing ground some boat of the company can work (its gear and depth) from a port it can get to from its base, refuelling on the way, with a trip from there reaching the ground, is a pie of the year: twelve slices, January at the top going clockwise, each month's slice split between the colours of the species in season there then, dark when nothing is; this month's slice has a white edge; big pies have the months' letters round the rim. The pie is the ground's own size on the map (9 to 72 px). Over pies 24 px or bigger are pictures of the ground's species round its middle. Hovering a picture tells all about that species there (its family and gear, its season there, how rich the catch is and the stock left, about what a crate fetches, whether it's a trophy fish, which boat models catch it, and its fact); hovering a slice, that month's species, rough seas and the ground's quality. Clicking a pie picks the ground (and opens its boats), or opens a picked one's boats. Picked grounds are ringed in gold with their name and boats. - The side panel: \"Your grounds\" on top (the picked ones: each with Boats… and an X to drop it, its boats going back to fishing the best within reach of their ports), and below \"All grounds your boats can reach, best first\": ranked by quality, the average over the twelve months of the best a crate-a-day there is worth, each with a little pie, its depth, species count, quality (% of the best) and a bar, Pick, and its name to show it on the map. - A picked ground's boats: a row for each boat model whose gear and depth can work something there (only those), with its picture, how many the company owns and has free, what it catches there, and the count of its boats sent there: + on the left, the number, - on the right, starting at 0 (the free boats nearest the ground go first; a boat sent elsewhere is busy; + is greyed out with none free). Each model's boats there land their catch at a port chosen from the HQ and hubs (\"freezer\") and the ports by the ground (miles from it beside each), sold there or, ticking Freezer (HQ and hubs only), kept in the freezer for the seafood carriers. Under each row, how its boats get there: straight out from the port, or \"Beyond a trip from Rotterdam: its boats sail to Lerwick and fish from there, and refuels at Aberdeen on the way each way, then bring the catch back the same way to land it\", in light blue, or in red if they can't get there at all. - A boat in port with a catch away from its base heads home to land it, else heads to that port, both by way of refuel stops if they're beyond one tank; at that port it makes its trips from there and back, and the catch is landed only at its base. Up to 8 boats work a ground at once. - Freezers (the cold store) are at the company's HQ and hubs only: a catch landed anywhere else is sold, whatever its boat's Catch setting."},{"p":"Playing the game","t":"Tutorial","u":"tutorial.html","x":"- The tutorial's voices: Captain Mo B. Dick, a big white-bearded captain in his dress whites, does the welcome and the birds (birds following ships and their boosts; the sounds and the sound tracker); Sailor Sonny, strong, sharp and able, does shipping (getting around, routes, the first ship, trading, growing the fleet, hubs); HQ Marissa, slim, tanned and Italian with long brown hair, green eyes and a lovely smile, does the money and the achievements (fuel and wear, prices, company levels, the first payday, the first achievement star); Deckhand Joe does sailing and the odd things about ships (upgrades, breakdowns, storms, seasons, icebreakers, lost ships, and whether to shoo a flock that has settled on a ship: don't). Each card shows the speaker's portrait and name over the tip. At least 25 s of play passes between one tip and the next, so they come at a digestible pace. - Chosen when founding the company (\"Tutorial (recommended for new players)\", on by default) and saved with it. A save started without it never shows the tutorial or its switch. - Tips on the basics come one at a time in a card in the bottom left of the main screens (top left over the Route Assignment map, which centers on the ship), under any popup. Each shows once its moment comes, the first not yet seen in order, and goes for good when the player clicks Got it or does what it asks: - Welcome (what the game is, the HQ ring, the Tutorial switch); Getting around (pan, zoom, clicking ports and ships, game speed). - Buy your first ship (Shop; done on buying one); Give it a route (Assign Route; done when a route is set); Planning a route (over the Route Assignment map: clicking ports, range, green and red legs, Accept). - Then: Trading runs itself (once a route is set); Fuel and wear (after the first sale: Refuel and Repair, ships held in port, the Ships tab's dot); Finding better trades (the Prices picker, the Markets page, port prices); Grow your fleet (the Shop's red dot, Finances). - When they happen: Company levels (level 2), Ship upgrades (a ship has an upgrade point), Hubs (a hub can be founded), Breakdowns (the breakdowns level), Storms (storms first act on ships), Seasonal closures (they first apply), Icebreakers (seasonal closures apply and icebreakers are unlocked), Lost at sea (a ship is lost: Send Recovery and the Rescue switch). - The map's Tutorial switch hides or shows the tips (saved with the game); turning it back on carries on where it left off."},{"p":"Playing the game","t":"Popups","u":"popups.html","x":""},{"p":"Playing the game / Popups","t":"Port popup (appears above or below the clicked port)","u":"popups--port-popup-appears-above-or-below-the-clicked-port.html","x":"- Title: port name and size (\"Hong Kong · Giant 99\"), then the size's details (see Port sizes). - Below that, tabs (one showing at a time, so the popup always fits on screen; the tab picked last stays picked for the next port): - Market: each commodity's buy and sell price there, green where cheap and red where expensive against its world average, grouped by the ship line that carries it (2026-10-15), in the Shop's order: a heading per line in its colour with how many goods (\"▾ Container ships · 13\"), click to fold it away or open it; lines the company's fleet sails (or, with no fleet yet, its types') open to start with, and each line's state is kept for the next port. The makers' goods listed are only those with a factory there. - Headquarters / Hub: level, XP, the company XP bonus and the upgrade tree; \"Build a hub\" instead at a port without one while a hub is available; no tab otherwise. - Your trade: what the company has bought and sold there since it began keeping count, each commodity with how much and for how much, ranked by what an industry building for it there would have been worth a month, \"Built\" where one stands, with a note of what a building does: the commodities best worth a building at that port. - Ships (N): the player's ships docked there, each with thin maintenance / fuel / cargo bars and clickable to open its ship popup; the list scrolls past 300 px."},{"p":"Playing the game / Popups","t":"Ship popup (openable from map, Ships screen, Finances and port popups)","u":"popups--ship-popup-openable-from-map-ships-screen-finances-and-port-popups.html","x":"- Name (top) with Crew and Upgrades buttons (showing unspent points, with a red dot while there are any) and X; Model, current speed (with top speed), range, capacity, and level with XP into it (cargo ships); under them the captain's face and \"Captain Ada Holm\", with how many mates and crew it has (\"3 mates · 320 crew\"). The Crew button swaps the bars and toggles for every officer, each with their face, name and rank (the Captain \"in command\", the rest Mates), then the crew and the ship's wages. - Click the name to rename the ship: it becomes a text box (up to 24 characters); Enter saves, Esc or clicking away cancels. A name another ship already has (ignoring case) or a blank one shows in red with the reason in its tooltip. The new name shows everywhere at once (tiles, Finances, the map) and the ship's last-10-minutes finances carry over. - The Upgrades button swaps the bars and toggles for the three skill paths (10 pips each, the bonus so far, and a + button to spend a point). Paths are listed Speed, Efficiency, Durability. - Maintenance, Fuel and Cargo bars with live values (recovery boats: Tow instead of Cargo), then the Repair, Refuel, Rescue (auto-recovery) and Full (full loads) toggles. While docked: this stop's summary, e.g. \"Sold $1,990 · Fuel −$800 · Repair −$200 / Profit $990\". - Lost ships: a Send Recovery button (see Lost ships and recovery boats). - Cargo ships with a route say what they carry (\"Carrying 20 containers of Clothing, bought for $101,811 at Rome.\") and what happens at their next stop: what they sell there for about how much, then what they buy there for the stop after, as the game would pick it, about how many and for about how much (as much as the hold takes after any lightening for the leg, and the money left after the sale, less the fuel and bank reserves): \"At Barcelona: sells for about $105,449; then buys about 20 containers of Coffee for about $98,000, for Genoa.\" (or \"nothing there pays to carry on to Genoa\"). In port the next stop is that port until the ship has loaded there for the leg on. - Status: e.g. \"Docked at Rome\" (with \"(no route)\", \"(paused)\", or why it's held when relevant), \"Unloading at Tunis\" / \"Loading at Tunis\", \"En route to Tunis — 40%\", \"Stopping at Tunis\" (paused while at sea). In a canal: \"In Gatún Locks (chamber 2 of 3), rising — to Balboa\", \"In Pedro Miguel Locks, waiting for Miraflores Locks\", \"Waiting for Gatún Locks (2nd in line)\", \"Waiting for toll money at the Panama Canal\". A stop's summary includes the toll paid on the leg that brought the ship there. - Current route listed if one exists. - Buttons: Assign Route (opens Route Assignment screen), Pause/Go toggle (greyed out with no route), Sell (first click shows the price, second click sells), Follow on Map. - Pause behavior: a ship paused at sea continues to its next port and stays docked there. Go resumes the route from that port."},{"p":"Playing the game","t":"Activity log","u":"activity-log.html","x":"- Messages go into an activity log down the left of the game screen (272 px wide, beside the World, Ships, Finances and Shop screens; hidden on the Route Assignment screen). Newest first, in small text (size 8; about three-quarters of messages fit on one line, and longer ones like \"held at\" warnings wrap to two), each with the play time it happened (e.g. \"02:10\"). Text is in the color of the ship it's about (lightened if too dark to read; company-wide messages stay white). Bad news (breakdowns, lost ships, ships held in port or that won't make it) has a red highlight behind it; good news (level-ups, recoveries) a green one. It keeps the last 100 messages and starts empty each session. - Messages, e.g. \"Sea Otter sold Toys at Rotterdam: +$2,300 profit (bought $1,200, sold $3,500)\" (in red for a loss), \"Sea Otter loaded 15 containers of Toys for Rotterdam ($45,000)\", \"Sea Otter is held at Tunis: not enough fuel for Rome; turn on refuel\", \"Sea Otter broke down at sea! Maintenance now 30%\", \"Sea Otter is lost at sea (out of fuel)\", \"Big Mo carried Sea Otter to Tunis\", \"Auto-recovery: Little Mo sent for Sea Otter (about $160,000)\", \"Sea Otter won't make it to Tunis at this rate!\", \"Sold Big Blue for $10,000\", \"Sea Otter entered the Panama Canal: toll $4,200 (+50% XP on this delivery)\". - The « button at the top of the log folds it away to a thin strip (» opens it again), giving the screens the room; remembered with the game. - Each model's group on the Ships screen has a Mute button at the right of its title: a muted model's routine news (its ships loading cargo, selling at a profit, entering canals) is left out of the log; losses, breakdowns, lost ships, level-ups and other trouble still show. \"Muted\" while on; remembered with the game."},{"p":"Playing the game","t":"Look","u":"look.html","x":"- Fonts: Nautical Classic, Cinzel (bold) for the headings and titles, every button's text, the company name, the clock, the level and the money in the top bar, the port names on the map and the loading screen, and Lato for everything else (fonts/, SIL Open Font License, their licences on the Licences page); any character they lack comes from the system's fonts."},{"p":"Playing the game","t":"Settings page sliders","u":"settings-page-sliders.html","x":"- The Settings page's General tab has three-step sliders in place of the radio buttons and switches: Ships seen from afar, Routes and Day and night. Birds and Tutorial stay switches. On the Audio tab the Master slider goes to 200%; the other volumes to 100%."},{"p":"","t":"Business and trading","u":"business.html","x":""},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Company types","u":"company-types.html","x":"- Every new company starts as one of six company types (a seventh, Research, can only be taken at a pick: see Research), chosen first thing on the New Company screen: Cargo & Ore (container ships, ore carriers and breakbulk carriers, and its mines, plants and factories: see Cargo & Ore industry), Vehicles (car carriers and boat carriers, and the makers: see Vehicle makers and factories), Agriculture (grain, livestock and Fresh produce carriers, and farms: see Farms; the Shop tab once called Food), Tankers (tankers and gas carriers, and rigs and refineries: see Oil and gas), Seafood (fishing boats, crabbing boats and seafood carriers) and Cruises (Ocean and Expedition cruisers: see Cruise lines). Each line has five tiers (see Ship models). - Its type's ships unlock fast: tier 1 at level 1, tier 2 at 4, tier 3 at 9, tier 4 at 16, tier 5 at 25 (every line of the type together: a Cargo & Ore company gets the Scooter 20 and Mini Bulker at 1, the Feeder 100 and Laker at 4...). Slots grow from each model's unlock level as before. - At company level 15, and every 15 levels after (30, 45, 60, 75), it branches out into one more type of the player's choice, for good: a Choose a company type button at the top right of the World map (above Unlock a region), a Choose button on the Shop's locked tabs, the level-up card (\"a new company type to choose\"), the Shop button's red dot and a tutorial tip all point to it. The choice shows the six types as cards, the types it has greyed; Choose, then Confirm. A type taken at level L unlocks its tiers at L, L + 3, L + 8, L + 15 and L + 24 (its first ships straight away). Level 90 gives a hub only. By level 75 a company has all six. - Ships outside the types (recovery boats, tugs, icebreakers) unlock at their own levels for every company. - Company level costs follow the company: each level costs the same minutes of a full fleet's XP as before, but the full fleet is the one its types give it, so a company with fewer lines levels as fast as one with every line. Fishing and crabbing boats count by a typical trip's catch. About 74,000-144,000 XP to level 15 by type (80,000 before types). - Each type is shown as an icon with its name: beside each saved game on the title screen, in the Type column of Finances → Compare companies (every type the company has, and the level it took each at, on hover), on the Shop's tab banners and the type cards. - Recommended headquarters follow the type: each port is ranked by how much its type's first ships could earn from it with only its home market region open (the best loop for cargo ships, the catch in season for fishing and crabbing boats, the best cruise for cruise ships), and ports a season closes are never better than hard starts. The New Company map and its notes, the join page and AI rivals' headquarters all follow the chosen type. The Great Lakes ports are offered to every type. - The tutorial follows the type: Mo B. Dick's welcome says what the company does, Sonny names its first ships, a Seafood start's first boat counts (it needs no route), the fishing tip only shows to a company with Seafood, the levels tip mentions the type picks, and a \"Time to branch out\" tip comes with each pick. - The Fresh produce carriers are left out of every company's level costs (Progression), so adding Agriculture's third line moved no company's level."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Company ratings","u":"company-ratings.html","x":"- Each company type a company runs has a rating of 0 to 5 stars, worked out from a sample every minute of play over the last 120 (about 2 hours): the window's average, the minutes not yet on record counting as nothing, so a type taken new starts at 0 stars and earns them. - A cargo type's sample: a third each Condition (its ships' maintenance from 40% to 95%, the share of their upgrade points spent, the mega upgrades of its models; not their age), Reliability (ships idle: paused, held, without a route or lost; departures with an empty hold; breakdowns and losses, a loss counting three) and Scale & reach (its ships against what its slots allow at the company's level, the ports it traded at in the last 30 minutes against what the level expects, the company's hubs against its slots). Agriculture has a fourth part, a quarter of its stars, Farms, so an Agriculture company needs farms for 5 stars. Cruises: a quarter each guest experience, itinerary, reliability & safety and value & reviews (see Cruise lines). - Effects (gentle, nothing at 2.5 stars): at 5 stars the type's cargo sells for 5% more, pays 20% less in port fees and earns 10% more XP; at 0 the opposite. The Cruises rating moves its recommended prices too (x0.7 at no stars to x1.3 at 5). - Shown on the Ratings page (a star button under the trophy on the map: each type's stars, what they do now, how much of the window is on record, each part's stars with the figures behind it and how to raise it), in a Ratings column on Compare companies, beside each type's name on the Shop's and the Ships tab's tabs, and in the activity log with a card each time a type reaches a new whole star or falls to one."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Company logos","u":"company-logos.html","x":"- Every company has a logo: the mark of the type it started as on a shape (square, circle, triangle or diamond) in the company's colour and a second colour (any of the company colours but its own), laid out solid, bordered, split on the diagonal or with a stripe across; Swap colours trades the background's and the mark's. - Made on its own page when founding (New Company: types, the company, then \"Your company logo\", then the CEO), with a big preview and every choice drawn as it would look. Changing it later costs $1,000,000. - Shown beside each saved game on the title screen, beside each company's name in Compare companies, before the company's name in the top bar, on each card of the Hubs screen, as a small sign beside each company's HQ and hub rings on the map (and on its HQ building's roof or over its hub warehouses zoomed in), and on its cruise ships' funnels."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Economy & time","u":"economy-time.html","x":"- Starting money: $2,500,000. - NOT an idle game (for beta): time only passes while the game is open, at a constant speed, or 2x or 4x with fast forward (see Top bar). - On quit, the game saves and the world freezes. On Continue, everything resumes exactly where it left off. Ships do not move and no money is earned while the game is closed. - Ships trade (see Trading): they buy a commodity at one port and sell it at the next. Profit is the price difference, less fuel, repairs, canal tolls, port fees and crew wages. - Port fees: a delivery sold at a port where the company has no HQ or hub pays 10% of the trade's margin (what the cargo sold for, less what it cost; nothing on a trade at a loss). The sale's log line shows it (\"port fee $1,200\"), the route screen's leg preview allows for it, and the Finances tab totals it as \"Port fees\" (the ship table puts it with the tolls). - Crew wages: every ship has captains and crew by its tier. Every two calendar weeks (about 17 minutes of play) each ship that worked in that time (sailed, did a job or a stop in port) pays its full wages: $3,270 a captain and $1,090 a crew member (a Scooter $5,450, a Dominator $361,880); ships idle the whole time pay nothing. Calibrated on the median profitable route: about 20% of a Scooter's profit, 10% of a Feeder 100's, 5% of a Dominator's (crew-heavy livestock carriers more, big tankers and car carriers less). Each ship's officers are characters made up when it's bought: the first is its Captain, in command, the rest its Mates. The shop's cards list each model's officers (\"Captain + 2 mates\"), crew and wages; the log says each payday (\"Payday: $16,350 in crew wages for 3 ships\"); the Finances tab totals them as \"Crew wages\" and gives each ship a Wages column. - Loans: on the Finances page's Your company tab, under Keep in the bank, a Loan row shows what's owed with Borrow $1M and Pay back $1M buttons: up to $10M in $1M steps (paying back needs the money; the last step pays off what's left). Interest of 8% a year is charged on what's owed each crew payday (a period's share, $30,685 on $10M), logged and counted in Finances as \"Loan interest\" (part of operating profit). Saved with the company. - Debt: wages are paid whatever the money, so they can take it below $0 (the top bar shows it in red). A company in debt runs its ships on credit, buying their fuel, repairs, full cargo loads and canal tolls whatever the money, so nothing stops and sales pay the debt off; but it can buy nothing else (ships, upgrades, hub points) until it's back above $0. - Every delivery (when unloading finishes) adds its units to the company's delivered totals, by commodity (shown on the Finances screen). - Ships buy their own fuel at each port's local price, which follows the oil price there (cheapest in the Gulf, most expensive on remote islands); on average it's half the old $2.40. Repair costs are half what they were. - Endless sandbox — no win condition."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Trading","u":"trading.html","x":"- 53 commodities besides the seafood: Toys, Clothing and Furniture, and since 2026-10-15 Electronics, Home appliances, Machine parts, Batteries, Solar panels, Tools & hardware, Sporting goods, Footwear, Cosmetics and Medicine (container ships); Steel, Aluminium, Copper, Nickel, Zinc, Tin, Lithium, Gold bars, Silver bars, Ferromanganese, Rare earths, Fertilizer, Plastics and Timber, by the ton (breakbulk carriers, 2026-10-15: see Cargo & Ore industry); Crude oil, Diesel, Premium gasoline and Jet fuel (tankers); LNG, LPG, Ammonia and Hydrogen (gas carriers: see Oil and gas); Iron ore, Coal and Gold ore, and since 2026-10-15 Copper ore, Bauxite, Nickel ore, Zinc ore, Tin ore, Lithium ore, Silver ore, Phosphate rock, Manganese ore and Rare earth ore (ore carriers); Wheat, Corn, Soybeans, Rice, Barley, Coffee, Cocoa, Tea, Sugarcane, Pepper, Vanilla, Cinnamon, Almonds, Olives, Dates, Cotton and Rubber, by the ton (grain carriers); Apples, Pears, Cherries, Peaches, Strawberries, Blueberries, Raspberries, Blackberries, Cranberries, Oranges, Lemons, Bananas, Mangoes, Pineapples, Avocados, Grapes, Potatoes, Tomatoes, Onions, Lettuce and Peppers, by the pallet (Fresh produce carriers, added 2026-10-15 with the farms); Cattle, Sheep, Pigs and Horses; and the 44 vehicle and boat makers' goods, each its own (car carriers: 22 carmakers' cars and trucks; boat carriers: 22 boatbuilders' boats; see Vehicle makers and factories). Coffee moved from the container ships to the grain carriers with the farms, $160 a ton (it was $2,250 a container; a container ship with coffee aboard in an old save sells it as tons of the same worth). The Shop and ship popup sum up the grain carriers' and Fresh produce carriers' long lists. Every ship picks whichever of its goods pays best at each stop. - Their markets follow who really grows, raises or builds them: wheat cheap on the Canadian and US prairies' ports, the Black Sea, Argentina and Australia, expensive in the Middle East and Africa; corn from the US Gulf and Great Lakes, Brazil and Argentina; soybeans from Brazil, Argentina and the US, most expensive in China; rice from Vietnam, Thailand, Myanmar, Pakistan and India, most expensive in West Africa and the Middle East; barley from Australia, Canada and the Black Sea, most expensive in the Middle East; cattle from Argentina, Uruguay, Brazil, Queensland and the Top End; sheep from Western and South Australia, New Zealand and the Horn of Africa, most expensive in the Middle East; pigs from Denmark, the Netherlands and Spain, most expensive in East Asia; cars from Japan, Korea, Germany and Detroit; trucks from Gothenburg, Germany, Detroit, Japan and China; boats from Italy, Florida, Taiwan and the Netherlands, most expensive in the Middle East and Africa. - The farm crops' markets follow where they're really grown (cheap there, expensive where they're not: cocoa from West Africa, tea from India, Sri Lanka and Kenya, spices from the Malabar coast and Southeast Asia, vanilla from Madagascar and Mexico, almonds from California, olives from the Mediterranean, dates from the Middle East and Egypt, rubber from Southeast Asia; apples and cherries from Washington and Chile, citrus from Brazil and the Mediterranean, bananas from Central America, Ecuador and the Philippines, mangoes from India, berries from the US, Chile and Peru, potatoes from Northern Europe; horses from the Pampas, Ireland and Uruguay, most expensive in the Middle East and East Asia), and the farm ports known for a crop sell it cheapest. Its profits there are a unit of hold's. - Livestock and vehicle carriers show what they carry: brown and white cattle, cream sheep with dark faces, pink pigs or bay, chestnut, grey and black horses in the pens; Fresh produce carriers crates of their crop in its colour under slatted, ventilated hatch covers (round fruit, ovals, berries, bananas in hands, pineapples, lettuces); grain carriers' open holds show the farm crops in their own colours; cars, trucks (a cab and a long box trailer) or white boats with a coloured stripe on the decks. - Every port buys and sells everything. Prices come from 17 regions (0.5x where a good is produced to 2x where it's scarce: toys cheap in China, oil and gas in the Gulf, gold at Tema, Durban, Callao, Magadan, Port Moresby and Juneau, iron ore on the Great Lakes, in Brazil and Australia, vehicles in Japan, Korea, Germany and Detroit...), evened out between nearby ports (as competing traders would, over about 1,200 nm) so prices build up with distance, with a gentle local variation (up to 16%, varying over thousands of miles) and a coastal variation (typically up to about 3% either way, varying over about 1,600 nm, so neighbouring coasts differ a little and a Scooter can find a margin on most coasts) and a slow drift (up to 5% over about 20 minutes, neighbours drifting together). Buying costs 5% more than selling at the same port. Every commodity's base price was halved on 2026-10-08 (a container of toys is $2,000, a ton of oil $165, a car $4,500), so every buy and sell price is half what it was; fuel follows the oil price, so it halved too, while XP and market impact per load were kept the same. Your own trades nudge prices by up to 1% (for a very large load), recovering within a couple of minutes. Island groups get small local gaps on top, so inter-island hops can pay: imports land cheaper at the main port (Honolulu, Suva, Pago Pago, Ponta Delgada) and cost more on the outer islands, and local products are cheaper where they are made (Kona coffee on the Big Island, Kobe coffee and Osaka clothing, Fiji gold); each group has at least one profitable container trade and one ore trade. - Halfway through each stop a ship sells its cargo, then buys the most profitable cargo it can carry to its next stop (after the canal tolls on the way): a full hold (less what a canal makes it leave behind), or as much as the money allows, always keeping enough for the whole fleet to fill its fuel tanks. Nothing if no cargo makes a profit (it sails empty). - Canal tolls are a share of the cargo's value at the destination (none for an empty ship). The rough-seas bonus (+50%), the upper-lakes bonus (+20%) and a hub's Sale prices upgrade add to a profitable trade's profit. - Balance: a good route earns about what the same ship earned under the old flat pay, the best about twice that; short hops earn a little, long hauls between the right regions a lot. - Markets page (the Markets button at the map's top left): pick a commodity to see the cheapest ports to buy it, the most expensive to sell, and the best trades anywhere and within 800 nm (port names go to the map). The port popup shows every commodity's buy and sell price there (green cheap, red expensive). The World map's Prices picker has two columns: Price boxes, a ship line's prices in a box over each port (see the map's top right, below), and Port colors, every commodity grouped by its ship line with the line's swatch (containers orange, tankers teal, ore grey, grain gold, livestock pink, vehicles blue: the line's middle ship's map color, brightened a little), each seafood family with a little drawing of one of its species instead, on the menu and on the button: picking one colors every port by its price, green where it's cheap, white about its world average, red where it's expensive, looked at again every second as prices drift; a port grown into its marina gets a ring in its color round its jetty instead, and by night its town's glow takes the color. The menu opens lined up with the button's right edge, and the chosen line's or commodity's name is shown in its color on the button. The route screen shows what each leg would load and its expected profit, or, for a leg with nothing worth carrying, that it sails empty and about what that costs in fuel and repairs (in red: \"nothing to trade: sails empty, about -$184 in fuel and repairs\"), at the arrival port's prices less any hub discount there."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Farms","u":"farms.html","x":"- Agriculture companies farm. 36 farm ports, each with its climate (Tropical, Tropical highland, Subtropical, Mediterranean, Arid, Oceanic, Cool, Continental), what it's known for and 6 to 12 plots: New Orleans, Savannah, Oakland, Seattle-Tacoma, Halifax, Veracruz, Puerto Cortes, Santos, Paranagua, Buenos Aires, Valparaiso, Guayaquil, Callao, Valencia, Palermo, Izmir, Porto, Rotterdam, Nantes-Saint-Nazaire, Cork, Aarhus, Odesa, Alexandria, Abidjan, Durban, Cape Town, Mombasa, Kolkata, Kochi, Colombo, Laem Chabang, Ho Chi Minh City, Tianjin, Brisbane, Melbourne and Auckland. A company farms at any farm port in a region it has unlocked. - Land: each plot has its fertility (65% to 130%, fixed by its port and place) and is bought from the land at a price set by it and by how much of the port's land is taken ($1M for poor soil with the land all free to $6M for rich soil with the last of it). A company may own at most half a port's plots. It can sell a plot back to the land for half the land's price, or list it at its own asking price: another Agriculture company buys it with its crop or herd and upgrades (AI rivals buy listed plots too). The plots are shared by every company in the game, first come. - Crops: any of the 38 farm crops (the 21 fresh, 11 new dry crops, coffee and the five grains), one to a plot. A crop's yield a year at full fit is $4M at its base price, times the plot's fertility and its climate fit, delivered day by day over its harvest months into the company's farm store at the port. Harvests follow the real seasons: the months north of the equator, six months later south of it, and at tropical ports twice a year unless it's gathered all year (bananas, pineapples, rubber). An annual crop needs 90 days before its first harvest and is sown again each year (8% of a year's yield at base price); an orchard, vineyard or plantation is planted once (35%) and bears fully after 2 years. Changing crop is replanting. - Pastures: a plot can graze a herd of a breed instead (23 breeds: Angus, Hereford, Belted Galloway, Brahman, Nelore and Wagyu cattle; Merino, Suffolk, Romney, Dorper, Awassi and Valais Blacknose sheep; Large White, Duroc, Berkshire, Iberian and Meishan pigs; Thoroughbred, Arabian, Quarter Horse, Criollo, Clydesdale and Icelandic horses), each with its worth against its plain species (Wagyu x2.5, Iberian x2.2, Valais Blacknose x2...), its growth a year, the head a plot carries (scaled so each species' plain breed's young are worth about what a field yields: 64,000 Angus, 72,000 Suffolk, 36,000 Large White, 36,800 Criollo; the approved draft's numbers x160, x90, x30 and x230) and the climates it thrives in. Stocking buys the herd from local breeders at a fifth of its market value; a full herd's young (growth x fit) go into the store day by day, and a herd short of full (after an outbreak) keeps its young till it's full again. Livestock carriers load the young stock, which sells at its breed's worth; clearing a pasture drives its herd into the store. - Weather: at each new year the year after's is drawn: a few farm ports get a drought (most at arid, Mediterranean, subtropical and continental ports), a late frost (cool, continental, oceanic and Mediterranean) or a hard winter (cool and continental). The Farms screen shows this year's and next year's; the activity log warns where the company farms. A drought takes 55% of a year's crops (10% with irrigation) and 40% of a herd's young; a frost 65% of the fruit, berries and vegetables (none under greenhouses); a hard winter 40% of the young (5% with barns). A storm over the port takes 70% of that day's harvest. Herds have outbreaks now and then (8% a year, a quarter with vets), losing 10-30% of the herd (half with vets). - Upgrades, a plot each: Irrigation ($400,000: +10%, droughts take only 10%), Greenhouses ($900,000, fresh crops: the climate fits halfway better, +25% for the longer season, no frost), Machinery ($500,000: half the wages, +5%), and for pastures Barns ($500,000), Better grass ($400,000: +20% head) and Vets ($300,000). A cold store ($1.5M a farm port) slows the store's fresh crops to a quarter of their loss. - Costs: the land, planting, herds, upgrades and farm workers' wages ($23,000 a planted plot or pasture every pay period, with the crews': half with machinery), in the Finances totals as Farm land bought, Farm land sold, Farm costs and Farm store sales. - The farm store: the harvest waits there for the company's ships, loaded free at the port: grain carriers take the dry crops, Fresh produce carriers the fruit, berries and vegetables, livestock carriers the young stock, each the one that pays most where it's going, its worth there counted as its cost. Fresh crops lose their daily share of value there too (a quarter with a cold store), dry crops 0.2% a day; under 25% they're thrown away (logged). Anything can be sold on the spot at the port's price. Farms never move market prices. - The Farms screen (the Farms page over the map, from its button at the map's top left, for companies that can farm): the farm ports down the left (All farm ports, My farms or Plots for sale; the company's own first, then its unlocked regions; each with its climate, free plots, its plots, plots for sale and this year's weather), and the port picked: its region, climate, what it's known for, the weather this year and next, a card for every plot (soil, owner, price and Buy; for its own what it grows with its fit, bearing, harvest months and yield a year, a crop picker and a herd picker each listing every choice best fit first with its yield and cost, the upgrades that suit it, List for sale with an asking price or Take off the list, Sell to the land (click again to confirm), and Clear the plot), and the farm store (each item's amount, freshness and worth with Sell here, and Build a cold store). The port popup of a farm port says so (climate, free plots, the company's plots and its store's worth) with a Farms button to it. - On the map each farm port's fields lie behind its town (four across, a row per four plots, on the land): wild grass for the land's, bare earth for an owned plot with nothing in, green while growing, the crop's colour while it's harvested, bare after, snow over the fields of cool and continental ports in winter; orchards drawn as rows of trees, pastures with their herd dotted about in the breed's colour, and each company's plots edged in its colour with a red barn. - AI rivals with Agriculture (from level 3, every few thinks) buy a listed plot they can afford for up to 30% over the land's price, else the best plot for its price at a farm port in their regions, and plant the best fitting crop their ships carry (or graze the best breed for their livestock carriers); one plot plus one every 12 levels, up to 6."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Vehicle makers and factories","u":"vehicle-makers-and-factories.html","x":"- Vehicles companies deal with 44 fictional makers: 22 carmakers carried by the car carriers and 22 boatbuilders carried by the boat carriers, from budget to luxury and from small (one factory) to big (three), spread over the world. Carmakers: Hoshino Motors (Nagoya, Yokohama, Charleston), Kaminari (Osaka), Daebak Auto (Busan, Incheon, Chennai), Seonghwa (Incheon, Qingdao), Longwei Motors (Shanghai, Guangzhou, Tianjin), Jinxing EV (Shenzhen, Ningbo), Rheinwerk (Bremerhaven, Hamburg, Savannah), Adlerhof (Hamburg, Antwerp), Velloro (Genoa), Corsetti (Rome, Tanger Med), Marchand (Le Havre, Valencia), Albion Motor Company (Southampton), Lakeshore Motors (Detroit, Toledo, Veracruz), Pioneer Trucks (Chicago, Houston), Nordvik Trucks (Gothenburg, Santos), Sagar Motors (Mundra, Chennai, Kolkata), Garuda Trucks (Jakarta, Surabaya), Pampa Motores (Santos, Buenos Aires), Southern Cross Motors (Melbourne, Adelaide), Amur Trucks (Vladivostok), Desert Falcon (Jebel Ali), Savanna Motors (Durban). Boatbuilders: Lazzaro Yachts and Vanderhaven (superyachts: La Spezia and Genoa, Rotterdam), Elbwerft, Solent Marine and Gulf Pearl Yachts (luxury motor and sports yachts: Hamburg, Southampton, Jebel Ali), Mistral Voiliers, Tasman Cats, Formosa Yachts, Muskoka Classics and Cape Craft (premium sailing yachts, catamarans, trawler yachts, wooden runabouts, charter catamarans), Bluewater Boatworks, Gulfstream Marine, Wavecrest, Yamakaze Marine, Fjordline Båt, Baltic Sails and Rio Náutica (mainstream sportfishers, bowriders, trailer boats, jet skis, day cruisers, sailboats, speedboats), Lakeland Boats, Nanhai Boats, Malabar Boatyards, Aegean Caiques and Andaman Marine (budget aluminium fishing boats, dinghies, fishing boats, caiques, longtails). - Their goods: each maker's vehicles are their own market good, in the Markets page's picker under Car makers and Boat builders. One takes its hold of deck slots (a car 1, a luxury car 1.5, a truck 2.5 or 3, a jet ski or dinghy 1, a runabout 2, a sailing yacht or catamaran 4, a motor yacht 10 to 12, a superyacht 60): a ship's capacity is in slots, and a good bigger than a ship's hold isn't offered to it. Prices: half at the maker's factories, 0.75 over its home region, and elsewhere what its class sells for by the region's wealth (luxury 1.2 in the poorest regions to 2 in the richest, budget 1.75 to 1.3, mainstream 1.6), then evened out over the map like every good. The old regional pricing of plain cars, trucks and boats gave way to the makers' factories. - Likeness: every company starts at 20 with every maker (wary of a new carrier). Trading its goods raises it: selling them by 0.3 x the square root of the load's worth in millions, buying them by 0.4 of that, tapering as it gets high ((120 - likeness) / 100, at least 0.2): about 46 round trips of a full Car Carrier take it from 20 to 70, 78 to 90; 40 of a Car Hopper's from 20 to about 38. Trading a maker's goods lowers its rivals' (the makers of the same kind, cars, trucks or boats, and class) by 0.15 of the gain. A ship breaking down with its goods aboard costs 2, one lost 5. The activity log says when a perk is won or lost. - Perks: at 40 the maker's own factories sell to the company 10% cheaper; at 55 first pick (buying at its factories no longer pushes their price up); at 70 the company may build a factory for it, at 90 a second. - Factories: built at any port where the maker has none (its own or any company's for it) in a region the company has unlocked: $30M for a budget maker, $50M mainstream, $90M premium, $150M luxury, and 1% of that a calendar month to run, paid a day at a time. Each makes 2,000 deck slots of the maker's vehicles a month (2,000 cars, 800 trucks, 33 superyachts) into the company's stockpile there, up to three months' worth. The company sells the stockpile there (Factory stock sales), or its ships load it free and sell it anywhere. The maker takes its cut of what factory goods sell for: 30% at 70 likeness, falling to 10% at 100 (on a ship, paid at the sale as the cargo's cost). A factory keeps running if the likeness falls back below 70; closing one, the maker buys it back for a quarter of its price. Building, upkeep and closing are Factories in the Finances totals. - The Makers screen (the Makers page over the map, from its button at the map's top left, for Vehicles companies): the makers down the left (All makers, Car makers, Boat builders or My factories; the best liked first, each with its colour, class and kind, home country, likeness and factories), and the maker picked: what it makes, where it builds and its story; its likeness bar with the perks reached; how likeness moves, its rivals (with the company's likeness with each) and the other companies' standing; its goods (slots each, average worth, how many the smallest and biggest carriers that take it hold, the cheapest and most expensive ports); and its factories: the company's own (stockpile, what it would fetch there after the cut, made so far, upkeep, Sell and Close with a second click), other companies', and Build a factory with a port picker (the HQ and hubs first, then every port it may build at by name). - The port popup lists the makers building there (theirs, or a company's for them), the player's likeness with each and its own factory's stockpile, with a Makers button to the screen. The World map's Factories switch (on to start with for Vehicles companies) marks each factory by its port, a little works in the maker's colour, outlined in the building company's colour for a company's. Price boxes for the car and boat carriers list the five makers that matter most at each port. The ship popup says when cargo is from the company's factory and what the maker takes. - Hub industries for cars, trucks and boats serve every maker's goods of that kind; delivery achievements count each maker's under Cars, Trucks or Boats. - AI rivals with Vehicles earn likeness by their trading like a player, and every few thinks build a factory for the maker they're best liked by (at the first of their HQ and hubs they can, with twice its price to spare), and sell a factory's stockpile where it is once it's nearly full. - Boats aboard a car carrier stay plain Boats and sell at the next port. - Boat carriers: Boat Barge (tier 1, level 6, 30 slots, $120,000), Deck Coaster (tier 2, level 12, 80 slots, $400,000), Yacht Transporter (tier 3, level 18, 200 slots, $1.5M), Heavy-Lift Carrier (tier 4, level 38, 2,000 slots, $15M, Medium ports and up) and Float-On Semi-Sub (tier 5, level 58, 8,000 slots, $60M, Large ports), about 5% slower than the car carriers, the three smallest through the Seaway. Drawn with a pointed bow, an open deck with yachts in cradles, the house forward and two deck cranes. The Shop, Ships and Finances screens list them as Boat carriers (and the car carriers as Car carriers)."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Cargo & Ore industry","u":"cargo-ore-industry.html","x":"- Every good the Cargo & Ore type trades has its building, for Cargo & Ore companies to build. 40 buildings: 13 mines (Iron, Coal, Gold, Copper, Bauxite, Nickel, Zinc, Tin, Lithium, Silver, Phosphate, Manganese and Rare earth mines) and a Logging camp (Timber) at their resource ports; 13 plants (Steel mill, Aluminium smelter, Copper smelter, Nickel refinery, Zinc smelter, Tin smelter, Lithium refinery, Gold refinery, Silver refinery, Ferroalloy plant, Rare earth separation plant, Fertilizer plant, Plastics plant); and 13 factories (Toy factory, Textile mill, Furniture works, Electronics plant, Appliance factory, Machine shop, Battery plant, Solar panel plant, Tool works, Sporting goods factory, Shoe factory, Cosmetics plant, Pharmaceutical plant). - The chains: iron ore and coal make steel; bauxite aluminium; copper ore copper; nickel ore nickel; zinc ore zinc; tin ore tin; lithium ore lithium; gold ore gold bars; silver ore silver bars; manganese ore and coal ferromanganese; rare earth ore rare earths; phosphate fertilizer; crude oil plastics. Then plastics make toys, cosmetics and (with rubber) footwear; cotton clothing; timber furniture; copper, rare earths and plastics electronics; steel, copper and plastics home appliances; steel and aluminium machine parts; lithium, nickel and manganese ore batteries; aluminium and silver bars solar panels; steel and zinc tools; aluminium and plastics sporting goods; medicine takes nothing shipped. Each input is so much a unit of output (1.6 tons of iron ore and 0.8 of coal a ton of steel...). - 34 new goods: 10 container goods, 10 ores and 14 breakbulk goods. Each line's new goods are priced so its average base price stays as it was (containers $1,750, ores $100 a ton), so level costs don't move. Prices: cheapest at their makers' ports (a mine's and camp's ports at half), then their regions, else their line's default by region (manufactured goods most expensive in the rich regions, ores in the industrial ones, metals in the growing ones). - Mines and camps go on claims at their resource ports (2 to 3 a port: iron at Two Harbors, Duluth, Presque Isle, Kirkenes, Vitoria, Brisbane, Nain; copper at Antofagasta, San Antonio, Callao, Lobito, Dar es Salaam, Dudinka; bauxite at Conakry, Darwin, Vila do Conde, Kingston, Fremantle; timber at Vancouver, Seattle, Arkhangelsk, Vanino and more), shared by every company, first come first served, at most 2 of a port's per company. Plants and factories go at any port in a region the company has unlocked, one of each kind a port per company. - Each makes its good a calendar day at a time: a mine or camp 15,000 tons a month, a plant 2,500 tons, a factory 600 containers, into its stockpile (holding 3 months' output). Its inputs come out of its store: the company's ships unload them there (ships unloading at a port where their company has a site taking their cargo put what fits into its store, up to 3 months' worth, the rest sold; counted at the port's buy price, what the site would have paid); when the store runs short it buys what it needs at the port's price, so a site its own ships keep supplied (from its own mines best) earns more. The company's ships load its stockpiles free, and choosing cargo they count an input their own site at the next port takes at that port's buy price. A stockpile can also be sold where it is. - Costs: a building's month of output over its inputs at base prices x 14, to the nearest $1M within $5M-$80M (a plant or factory that takes nothing shipped 1.5x): an iron mine $12M, a gold mine $37M, a copper mine $29M, a logging camp $25M, a steel mill $5M, a gold refinery $18M, an electronics plant $16M, a pharmaceutical plant $16M. Upkeep 1% of that a calendar month, paid a day at a time; inputs bought, building and upkeep are Industry in the Finances totals, stockpiles sold Industry stock sales. A site can be listed for other Cargo & Ore companies at its price (bought with its stock and store) or closed for a quarter back. - The Industry page (a mine-cart button at the map's top left, Cargo & Ore companies): the buildings down the left (Mines & camps, Plants, Factories or My buildings, each with its good and price), and the one picked: what it makes, from what, how much a month, its cost and upkeep; its good's cheapest and most expensive ports and its inputs' cheapest; a mine's or camp's claims port by port (taken and by whom, Build, and Buy for one listed); a plant's or factory's Build with a port picker (the HQ and hubs first); and the company's own (stockpile, inputs in store, made so far, Sell, List, Close). - The port popup lists the industry at the port (each building, the claims still free, whose, and this player's stockpile) with an Industry button to the page. The map's Industry switch (it was Factories) marks every site by its port alongside the makers' factories: a mine's headframe, a camp's tree, a plant's or factory's works, in its good's colour, outlined in its company's. - AI rivals with Cargo & Ore (from level 5, every few thinks, up to 1 site plus 1 every 10 levels) build the building whose good their ships carry that makes the most for its price, at a free claim or at their HQ or a hub, and sell a site's stockpile where it is once it's nearly full. - Saved with the world (the claims are everyone's) and sent to joined games with each update; the actions are host commands. - Breakbulk carriers: Breakbulk Coaster (tier 1, 60 tons), Multipurpose (tier 2, 200 tons), Tweendecker (tier 3, 1,000 tons), Project Carrier (tier 4, 5,000 tons, Medium ports and up) and Open-Hatch Giant (tier 5, 40,000 tons, Large ports), with the ore carriers' speeds, prices and running costs, a load worth what an ore carrier's is; the three smallest through the Seaway, and the Buffalo carries them. Drawn as general cargo ships with the house aft, three holds and two deck cranes: steel coils, beams and crates in the holds, all coils for steel and logs for timber. - Ore carriers' holds show the new ores in their colours."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Market regions","u":"market-regions.html","x":"- The 17 market regions start locked, all but the home port's. On the map a locked region is shaded dark, its ports are small faint dots that can't be clicked (zoomed in, their marinas show like any others, and their names are shown dimmed), its land drawings aren't drawn, and the Markets page leaves its ports out. No route can stop at its ports and no hub can be built there, but ships may sail through it. Port growth messages for its ports aren't logged. - A region's area on the globe: every point belongs to the region of its nearest port. - One more region can be unlocked every 5 company levels. The level-up toast says \"a new region to unlock\", the World tab gets a black dot, and an \"Unlock a region\" button (with the count, if more than one) heads the map's top-right panel. It opens the choice: the regions are outlined, every locked region has a button with its name at its middle, the locked region under the mouse (or button) lights up gold, and a banner at the bottom explains (Cancel or Esc closes it). Clicking a region or its button arms it (\"Click again to unlock Southeast Asia\"); clicking again unlocks it for good, with a log message. - Saved per company (multiplayer: each player's own; joined players' unlocks go through the host)."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Headquarters and hubs","u":"headquarters-and-hubs.html","x":"- The company starts with its headquarters (HQ) at its home port. It gets one more hub every 15 company levels (15, 30, 45, 60, 75, 90: 7 locations in all). A hub is founded from any port's popup (\"Build a hub here\", click again to confirm) and is permanent. - Each HQ/hub levels from 0 to 40 on the XP of every delivery unloaded at its port (the same XP the company gets), and of every full load shipped out of it by a ship that arrived empty (a one-way route out of the hub: the hub gets the XP the load will earn where it's delivered, when it's loaded; the company and ship still get theirs on delivery). Level L to L+1 costs (5 + 0.7 x L) minutes of a quarter of a full fleet's XP at the current company level, so a hub taking about a quarter of the fleet's deliveries maxes out in about 12.5 hours. - Each level is an upgrade point, spent in the port popup on four paths of up to 10 points each. Each point also costs money: $2M for a path's first point, then $500k more for each point already in that path ($2.5M, $3M... $6.5M for the 10th; $42.5M for a full path). The buttons show the price and grey out when you can't afford it; the Finances tab totals it as \"Hub upgrades\". They boost every ship docking there: Ship XP +5% per point (ship XP only, not company or hub XP), Fuel & repairs -3% price, Port stops -5% time (unloading, repairs and refueling all speed up), Pay +2% on deliveries unloaded there. The HQ's bonuses are 1.5x a hub's. - Every delivery unloaded at an HQ or hub also earns the company extra XP, free with the hub's level: +10%, plus 10% more for every 10 hub levels (+20% at level 10... +50% at level 40). The HQ's is 1.5x (+15% up to +75%). The hub's own XP and the ship's aren't boosted by it. The Hubs cards and port popup show it, e.g. \"Company XP +30% on deliveries here (+40% at level 30)\". - Hub warehouses (2026-10-14): a hub that isn't the HQ has its company's warehouses on the HQ's half of the quay behind its basin, roofed in the company's colour so whose hub it is shows from above, 2 at first and one more every 3 hub levels up to 4, the one nearest the basin its freezer (a white roof with a band of the colour and cooling units), each with its shadow, ridge, roof sheeting and loading doors. The HQ building, the warehouses and the industry are now drawn after the port's town (its ground reached over the quay and hid them all). - On the map, the HQ and hubs have a ring in the company color around their port (heavier for the HQ; gray on the Route Assignment map). A small red dot sits just up and to the right of any HQ or hub with an upgrade you can buy now. The map's Hubs button shows a black dot while a hub has an upgrade you can buy (a point and the money) or a new hub can be founded. - New ships launch at the HQ or any hub, chosen when buying; recovery boats are then re-based across the hubs (see Lost ships and recovery boats). - Warehouses: every HQ and hub has room for 3, on the quay behind the marina's basin, on the side across the channel from the HQ building (+y), in a row of equal square plots nearest the basin. One plot opens at each of hub levels 10, 20 and 30. Each is one commodity's, and serves every good of its kind (a maker's cars count as Cars, diesel as Crude oil): a warehouse, or a stockyard for livestock, a cold store for seafood and fresh produce, a terminal for oil and gas; a hub can't have two of the same, but the same one can be built at other hubs. Each costs a flat $15M (Finances \"Hub upgrades\") and can be pulled down (confirmed) for half back, freeing the plot. At its port, for its owner's ships only, its commodity costs 5% less to buy and sells for 5% more; no XP bonus since they became warehouses (it was 50%, with 20% off and 20% more). Saves keep their buildings, as warehouses for the same goods. Built from the Hubs page (each card's Warehouses section) or by clicking a plot on the map (a popup with the same section): an open plot shows \"Build ($15M)\", which opens a picker of every commodity (ones already there greyed; not the makers' goods, which use their kind's); a built one shows its boosts and \"Pull down\". On the map an open, empty plot of this player's own hub is marked out with a dashed line; built ones are a grey warehouse with a band in the good's colour, at any company's hub. In multiplayer they're host commands. Saved with each hub."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Company and ship levels (XP)","u":"company-and-ship-levels-xp.html","x":"- Every delivery (when unloading finishes) gives XP to the company and to the ship for the work done: units delivered × leg distance × 0.01, weighted by the commodity's base price against a container of toys (a ton of oil counts for less). It doesn't depend on the profit, so a trade at a loss still earns XP. Levelling is about twice as fast as before the trading update (company level costs, hub level costs and each model's first ship level all halved), so level 100 takes about 20 hours. - The company levels from 1 to 140 (Level and an XP bar in the top bar; toast on level-up with what it unlocked). Each level's cost is set so it takes roughly 3 minutes at level 1, rising to about 47 minutes at level 99 and 65 at level 139, of what a full fleet at that level earns. So bigger ships don't make levels fly by, and level 100 takes about 40 hours and level 140 about 80 (about 1 hour to level 10, 11 hours to 50, 24 hours to 75). - Ship slots are per model: each cargo ship model can be owned 1 at a time when it unlocks, plus 1 more every 3 company levels, up to its max of 8 (so 21 levels after unlocking). The Scooter 20 and Feeder 100 gain a slot every 2 levels instead (full 14 levels after unlocking). Recovery boats start at 1 and gain 1 every 5 levels, up to 8. Level-up messages say which models got a slot (\"+1 slot: Scooter 20, Feeder 100\"). - Models unlock by company level: Scooter 20 at 1, Bunker Tanker at 4, Feeder 100 at 5, Buffalo at 6 (with breakdowns and storms), Lake Tanker at 9, Feedermax 200 at 12, Coastal at 15, Trans Atlantic and Mammoth at 25, Aframax at 35, Dominator at 50, Supertanker at 60. Ships already owned are kept even if they're above the current slots or unlocks. - Ships level from 0 to 30. - Each ship level gives a skill point, spent in the ship popup's Skills view on three paths of up to 10 levels each: Speed (+2% top speed per level), Durability (−5% wear and −5% chance of taking a random breakdown per level), and Gas efficiency (−3% fuel burn per level). Range on the route screen stays at the model's base range. - Upgrades also cost money, each skill path priced separately: a path's 1st level costs 5% of the model's shop price, each next level 2.5% more, so a Scooter 20's first is $625 and a Dominator's $2,500,000; maxing all three paths costs 4.875x the price. The Skills view's buttons show the price (\"+ $12.5M\", \"Max\" when maxed), greyed out with the reason when it can't be afforded. Upgrades bought before costs came in stay free. The Finances totals list it as \"Ship upgrades\". - Recovery boats level too, 0 to 30, with the same three skills. Each finished recovery (when the boat drops the ship off) gives the boat 1 XP per mile of the job (out to the lost ship and carrying it to port), and the company the XP the rescued ship's full hold would earn over the miles it was carried (a small bonus next to deliveries). First levels: Buffalo 400 XP, Mammoth 1,000 (a Buffalo job averages about 1,050 nm, a Mammoth one about 2,700 nm), so the first level takes about one job and level 30 about 20 hours of play. Their tiles show \"Recovery · Lv 3\"."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Achievements","u":"achievements.html","x":"- The trophy button under the field guide's at the map's top right opens the achievements: the stars earned of all there are, then lines of three stars, each with how far along it is to its next star (and a bar), then the badges. - Cargo delivered: a line for each of the 20 commodities, by units delivered: containers 2,000 / 250,000 / 5,000,000; tons 50,000 / 10 million / 100 million; head of livestock 5,000 / 1 million / 10 million; vehicles 2,000 / 250,000 / 5,000,000 (a few loads of a small ship, a good many of a middling one, and hundreds of the biggest: a long game's work). - Company: Fleet (ships owned, tugs not counted: 10 / 50 / 200), Company level (10 / 30 / 60), Money in the bank ($10M / $500M / $5B), and Field guide: animals seen, a third, two thirds and all of the guide's 187 (62 / 125 / 187, shown as counts); each of those stars takes 20% off the price of a tug (see Tugs). - Hidden gems: three gems are hidden on the map, each worth $10,000,000 the first time a company finds it. The Hidden gems line has a star for each one found (1 / 2 / 3). - Badges (milestones): Maiden voyage (deliver a cargo), Branching out (build a hub), Network (5 hubs), New horizons (a second market region), Seven seas (every region), Jack of all trades (deliver every commodity), Full house (a ship of every cargo line), Between the oceans (cross the Panama Canal), Short cut (the Suez Canal), Lock keeper (every canal), Borrowed time (take out a loan), Debt free (pay one back in full) and Mega (a mega upgrade). - Once earned, kept (selling ships or spending money doesn't take them back). Each player's game looks for new ones every second and says so in the activity log (\"Achievement: Coffee ★★ (2 of 3 stars)\", \"Achievement: Maiden voyage\") and on a card in the middle of the screen (see Cards). - Cards: the big moments get a card in the middle of the screen: a company level reached (COMPANY LEVEL UP, \"Level 12\", and what it unlocked, or a word to keep going) and an achievement earned (ACHIEVEMENT, its name and stars, \"2 of 3 stars for containers delivered\"). The game pauses while one shows and carries on at the speed it was at when Continue is pressed (Space or Enter too; a game already paused stays paused); several at once queue up, one card each. In multiplayer the host's clock runs for everyone, so the card shows without pausing and Continue just closes it. - Animals seen, pokedex style: clicking a flock of birds, a pod of sea animals or a group of land animals on the map (its popup showing them) marks each species in it seen, and the field guide shows a green check by its name and how many are seen of all of them (\"34 of 121 seen\"). - Achievements and animals seen are each player's own: a joined player's are worked out by their game and sent to the host, which keeps them in its save."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Divine Actions","u":"divine-actions.html","x":"- A game mode chosen when a company is founded, unrealistic and unfair by design. Rivals (AI and other players) have the same powers. - Powers, bought from the Divine panel (the bolt button under the trophy on the World map): each row names the power, its price now and what it does, with Unleash (greyed with why: too expensive, on cooldown, Wrath used this year, three storms out). Prices scale with company level (base x (1 + level / 50)), except Wrath, a flat $1,000,000,000. Each has a cooldown in game weeks (a week being 504 s of play). A power with a target closes the panel and has the player click it on the map (a hint at the top says what; Esc cancels): a point at sea, a port, or a rival's ship at sea. - Storms, three tiers, called down at the point clicked: Squall ($25M base; a gale, 180 nm, 2 days, cooldown 0.2 weeks), Tempest ($200M; a hurricane, typhoon or cyclone by those waters, 300 nm, 7 days, with an eye, closes ports under its core; 0.6 weeks) and Wrath ($1B; the tropical kind, 600 nm, 14 days, power 2: twice any natural storm's strength, so 95% slower and 5x wear at its core at Harsh settings, closing every port it crosses; once a calendar year). A divine storm starts at full strength, holds it most of its life and fades at the end (intensity (1 - (age/life)^4) x power), drifts slowly (5 knots) from where it was called, is named for its tier (\"Cyclone Wrath\") with \"sent by <company>\" under it on the map and a \"Sent by\" row in its popup, and acts from company level 1 even in a game founded without storms (then at Harsh settings). At most 3 of a company's storms are out at once. The sender's own ships suffer in it like anyone's. - Fair winds ($100M; no target; a week): the company's whole fleet sails 1.5x faster for a week. - Calm seas ($50M; a point): every storm within 500 nm blows out at once (natural or divine). - Plague of gulls ($30M; a port; a week): gulls mob the port (drawn wheeling over it); every other company's ships there take twice as long over their stops. - Deluge ($80M; a port; a week): rain over the port; its prices halve for everyone (buying and selling). - Sea change ($60M; a port; a month): a great flock (drawn as a golden ring of birds) settles on the port: it counts 40 more birds for the port blessing below. - The Kraken ($150M; a rival's ship at sea): the ship is lost where it is (\"taken by the Kraken\", maintenance 0) for a tug or recovery boat to fetch; its arms rise round the ship for 20 s. - Lightning strike ($120M; a port where a rival has a hub): the hub drops a level; a bolt and flash over the port for 4 s. - Blessings and omens, always on in a Divine game: birds following a ship speed it up 2% a bird (an albatross 4%), up to 50%; whales or dolphins within 50 nm shield a ship from a storm's wear; sharks within 100 nm of a lost ship hurry its rescuers 1.5x; frigatebirds following a ship make off with 2% a bird (up to 10%) of its sale (logged); a port's gulls, cormorants, seabirds and sea ducks (plus a Sea change) bless it, 80 birds for the full blessing: its stops up to 50% faster and its prices up to 20% better each way for ships there; a port's lamps by night turn ships round 20% faster; the first snow (1-7 December, ports north of 50 N) slows stops there 30%; cherry blossom (Japan in April) doubles delivery XP there; and one day a year a comet crosses the sky (drawn above the night at the top of the map, logged) lifting everyone's sale prices 10%. - Every act goes in the activity log in the actor's colour (\"Northstar Freight called down a Tempest on the waters off Lagos.\"; red when it hits this player's ships, port or hub); the panel lists the last 12 acts. AI rivals use the powers too: about once a game week (Easy every 3, Hard every 1) a rival picks on the richest other company and uses the first power it can afford twice over that it has a target for (a storm or the Kraken on one of that company's ships at sea, a plague or deluge on its busiest port, lightning on one of its hubs, fair winds for itself, calm seas where its own ship is in a storm, a great flock on its own busiest port). - Multiplayer: the host does every act and sends the divine state (effects, cooldowns, ledger) with its updates; players can target each other and the AI. Achievements and company comparison are unchanged."},{"p":"Business and trading","t":"Skin packs, Sand Dollars and the Shop","u":"skin-packs-sand-dollars-and-the-shop.html","x":"- Skins are bought for Sand Dollars, the in-game currency, one at a time or in bundles: any one skin 100 Sand Dollars ($1), every ship skin of a pack 500 ($5), every outfit of a pack 500 ($5), a whole pack 1,000 ($10); a bundle never charges for skins already owned (its price is capped at the missing skins one by one). A ship skin covers the models its art fits (the drumstick and the dinner roll come in three fits, one skin); an outfit is a human skin: the CEO and captain options it unlocks, its crew hat and its uniform colours (the catalog's outfits list what each holds; a shared option such as buckle shoes comes with any outfit that has it). Sand Dollars: one Sand Dollar is one cent, and they're bought in bundles (500 for $5.00 up to 5,000 for $50.00) with Steam money through the Steam Inventory Service. Without Steam running the Sand Dollars page says so and nothing can be bought. The balance, the packs owned and the pack worn are this computer's, not a save's, with a ledger of every grant and purchase. - The Shop is opened from the title screen, its button under Single Player and Multiplayer, not from the Esc menu. It has two tabs. Skin packs lists every pack on sale (and any with a skin owned), then the packs coming soon, shown with every skin and its picture but \"Coming soon\" in place of every Buy. For each pack: its title, how many of its skins are owned (what the ships wear is chosen in the Inventory, not here, and buying a skin doesn't put it on them), a Whole pack button, then its ship skins and its outfits, each skin its own panel, four to a row: its picture (a ship skin's art; an outfit worn by a whole-body portrait), its name, which models wear it or what the outfit unlocks and Buy with its price (Owned once bought) and an All of these button per group, every bundle button showing its price now and greyed with how much more is needed. Sand Dollars shows the balance, the bundles and what Steam says about a purchase under way. - The Inventory is opened from the title screen, its button under the Shop and above Quit: every skin the player owns, in two tabs. Ship skins: the pack worn, then By ship, each model with a skin owned for it, then every owned ship skin by pack with its picture, who wears it and \"Wear on its ships\" (sets it for each of its models; \"✓ Worn\" once it is). A model's choice wins over the pack worn, and a ship's own Skin (its popup, now \"As the Inventory\" for the default) wins over both. Outfits: every owned outfit with its whole-body portrait and what it unlocks, and where it's chosen (Found Company and the CEO editor; crew hats and colours on the Uniforms page). - A pack: ship skins by model; CEO and captain outfits; crew hats and uniform colours. - 2026 Thanksgiving Pack: (coming soon: shown in the Shop, not yet sold) roast turkey (ore carriers), corn on the cob (tankers), candied yams (grain carriers), green bean casserole (livestock carriers), cranberry sauce (container ships), stuffing (car carriers), gravy boat (tug), drumstick (fishing boats), dinner roll (crab boats), pumpkin pie (reefers), the Mayflower (Mammoth), cornucopia (Buffalo), mashed potatoes (icebreakers); Pilgrim doublet, Goodwife's gown, colonial coat, linen shirt and jerkin, farmhand's tunic, breeches, slops, hose, buckle shoes, cloth wraps, capotain, coif, Monmouth cap, turkey hat; Pilgrim black, homespun brown, linen cream and gown grey-blue shirts, breeches brown and hose grey trousers. - 2026 Christmas Pack: (coming soon: shown in the Shop, not yet sold) five wrapped presents by container model, candy cane (tankers), yule log (ore carriers), gingerbread man (grain carriers), tin of cookies (livestock carriers), nutcracker (car carriers), string of lights (reefers), red bauble (tug), frosted bauble, snowman and stocking (fishing boats), boiled sweet, gingerbread house and toboggan (crab boats), Santa's sleigh (Mammoth), Santa's sack (Buffalo), Christmas tree (icebreakers); Santa suit, Mrs Claus's gown, elf tunic, reindeer onesie, ugly Christmas sweater, nutcracker tunic, carol singer's coat, their trousers and tights, elf shoes, Santa's beard, Santa hat, elf hat, antlers, earmuffs, holly top hat; Santa red, elf green, reindeer brown, sweater green and caroler green shirts, Santa red and elf stripes trousers. - 2026 Halloween Pack (on sale): five coffins by container model (wooden, black with a silver bat, a vampire's open in red velvet, a mummy's sarcophagus, an old one chained shut), witch's broomstick (tankers), black cat (ore carriers), skeleton (grain carriers), caramel apple (livestock carriers), spellbook (car carriers), vampire bat (reefers), jack-o'-lantern (tug), witch's cauldron, sheet ghost and candy corn (fishing boats), skull, witch's hat and black widow (crab boats), ghost ship (Mammoth), haunted house (Buffalo), Frankenstein's monster (icebreakers); outfits: witch (witch's gown, witch's boots, witch hat), vampire, skeleton (skeleton suit and leggings, skull mask), mummy (mummy wraps and legs, mummy wrap over the head with an eye slit), scarecrow (scarecrow's shirt, patched jeans, scarecrow hat), Frankenstein's monster (monster's jacket, ragged trousers, platform boots, monster's flat top: green skin, stitches, neck bolts), jack-o'-lantern head (a hat over the whole head); no hair is drawn under the skull mask, the mummy wrap or the monster's flat top. Witch purple, vampire black, skeleton black, mummy linen, scarecrow plaid, monster green and pumpkin orange shirts, skeleton black and patched denim trousers. - 2026 Baseball Pack: five hot dogs by container model (mini with mustard, ketchup and mustard, Chicago, chili cheese, foot-long with everything), ash, black and royal blue bats (tankers, ore and grain carriers), peanuts (livestock carriers), popcorn (car carriers), soda cup (reefers), softball, two baseballs, pretzel (fishing boats), catcher's mitt, batting helmet, home plate (crab boats), baseball (tug), foam finger (Mammoth), scoreboard (Buffalo), cotton candy (icebreakers); home pinstripes, road greys, catcher's gear, umpire's polo, vendor's stripes, 1920s flannels, the matching pants, cleats, navy and red ball caps, batting helmet, catcher's mask, vendor's paper hat, umpire's cap, old-time ball cap; pinstripe white, road grey and vendor stripes shirts, white and grey baseball pants. - Cruise ships (2026-10-15): every pack dresses each cruise line in one skin across its five models: Thanksgiving a loaf of cornbread with a pat of butter (Ocean cruisers) and a slice of pecan pie, point first, with whipped cream (Expedition cruisers); Baseball a ballpark (stands, striped outfield, the diamond at home plate, the scoreboard at the bow) and a navy team pennant on its stick; Halloween an orange candy bar with a bat label and a torn end, and a black candle with purple drips, its flame at the bow; Christmas a Christmas cracker with a holly band and gold frilled ends, and an ice skate, its curled blade tip at the bow. - Fresh produce carriers (2026-10-15): every pack dresses all five in one skin: Thanksgiving a wicker harvest basket of apples, pears, grapes, a pumpkin and an ear of corn; Baseball a striped ballpark lemonade cup with a lemon wheel and a straw; Halloween a pumpkin vine with three pumpkins, the middle one a jack-o'-lantern; Christmas a fruitcake with candied cherries, citron and nuts, an icing drizzle and a holly sprig. - Boat carriers (2026-10-15): every pack dresses all five in one skin: Thanksgiving a butternut squash, its bulb astern and its stem and a leaf at the bow; Baseball a dugout canoe with two thwarts, an ash bat and a black bat, a baseball, a catcher's mitt and a little blue pennant aboard; Halloween a ribcage, its spine down the middle, the ribs curving out to the sides on a faint dark-purple hull and a skull at the bow; Christmas a green elf's shoe with stitching, a red pointed cuff with gold tips round a red-and-white striped stocking, and its toe curled at the bow with a gold bell (Butternut squash, Dugout canoe, Ribcage and Elf's shoe in the Shop). - Gas carriers (2026-10-15): every pack dresses all five in one skin, four round things along it like the line's tank domes: Thanksgiving a platter of deviled eggs (a blue dashed rim, yolk swirls, paprika and parsley); Baseball a stick of bubble gum, its stars-and-stripes wrapper astern and four pink bubbles blown along it; Halloween a purple platter edged in orange with four bloodshot eyeballs (green, blue, orange and purple) and green slime; Christmas a tin baking tray of four star-topped mince pies dusted with sugar, holly at the bow (Deviled eggs, Bubble gum, Eyeballs and Mince pies in the Shop). - Breakbulk carriers (2026-10-15): every pack dresses all five in one skin: Thanksgiving a stick of butter half out of its paper wrapper on a white butter dish, a curl of butter at the bow; Baseball a red-and-white striped paper boat of tortilla chips with cheese sauce and jalapeño slices; Halloween a mummy, its bandages wound round, arms crossed, glowing green eyes at the bow and a loose end at the stern; Christmas a toy train, its red and navy engine at the bow (a gold-banded boiler and a cowcatcher) pulling a green wagon and a blue one loaded with presents (Stick of butter, Nachos, Mummy and Toy train in the Shop)."},{"p":"","t":"Ships and crews","u":"ships.html","x":""},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Ship models","u":"ship-models.html","x":"- Six lines: container ships (Scooter 20, Feeder 100, Feedermax 200, Trans Atlantic, Dominator), tankers (Bunker Tanker L4, Lake Tanker L9, Coastal, Aframax, Supertanker), and new ore carriers (Laker L2, Panamax Bulker L20, Capesize L45), grain carriers (Grain Laker L8, Kamsarmax L28, Post-Panamax Bulker L52), livestock carriers (Cattle Coaster L12, Livestock Carrier L30, Ocean Shearer L55) and vehicle carriers (Car Hopper L18, Car Carrier L38, Giant Car Carrier L58), plus the recovery boats. The Scooter 20 now carries 20 containers (up from 15, with no change to its fuel use). Seaway-sized models (by length): Scooter, Feeder, Feedermax, Bunker Tanker, Lake Tanker, Coastal, Laker, Grain Laker, Cattle Coaster, Livestock Carrier, Car Hopper and the Buffalo (which can now also carry the small new models). Model Price Speed (nm/s) Capacity Range per leg Max owned --- --- --- --- --- --- Scooter 20 $12,500 5.44 20 490 nm (≈1 min 30 s) 8 Feeder 100 $125,000 4.82 100 760 nm (≈2 min 40 s) 8 Feedermax 200 $500,000 3.27 200 1,175 nm (≈6 min) 8 Trans Atlantic $5,000,000 6.35 1,250 4,080 nm (Rome→New York is 4,073 nm) 8 Dominator $50,000,000 3.99 10,000 9,000 nm 8 Buffalo $1,250,000 6.35 carries 1 lost Scooter 20, Feeder 100, Feedermax 200, Bunker Tanker, Lake Tanker or Coastal recovery jobs up to 8,000 nm 8 Bunker Tanker (gas tanker, L4) $40,000 5.60 250 tons of fuel 490 nm 8 Lake Tanker (gas tanker, L9) $250,000 5.20 1,500 tons of fuel 760 nm 8 Coastal (gas tanker) $1,500,000 6.35 5,000 tons of fuel 1,175 nm 8 Aframax (gas tanker) $15,000,000 5.40 50,000 tons of fuel 4,080 nm 8 Supertanker (gas tanker) $150,000,000 4.59 500,000 tons of fuel 9,000 nm 8 Mammoth $10,000,000 6.35 carries 1 lost ship recovery jobs up to 23,300 nm 8 Model Fuel tank (units) Burn (units/s) Wear (%/min) Repair per 1% Port stop --- --- --- --- --- --- Scooter 20 510 5.55 1 $200 5 s Feeder 100 7,950 37 1 $1,330 10 s Feedermax 200 25,000 67 1 $2,400 15 s Trans Atlantic 555,000 810 1 $29,200 30 s Dominator 10,300,000 4,070 0.5 $293,000 1 min Buffalo 645,000 450 1 $30,000 10 s Bunker Tanker 640 7 1 $280 6 s Lake Tanker 6,800 45 1 $1,900 10 s Coastal 50,500 265 1 $9,500 15 s Aframax 1,830,000 2,250 1 $81,000 30 s Supertanker 41,300,000 19,125 0.5 $1,375,000 1 min Mammoth 20,400,000 4,500 0.5 $300,000 10 s - Fresh produce carriers: Fruit Coaster (tier 1, 150 pallets, 6.0 nm/s, 490 nm, $60,000), Banana Boat (tier 2, 600, 6.2, 760 nm, $300,000), Fruit Reefer (tier 3, 2,000, 6.4, 1,175 nm, $1.3M, keeps 15% of the spoiling off), Produce Reefer (tier 4, 8,000, 6.6, 4,080 nm, Medium ports, $6M, 30%) and Ultra Reefer (tier 5, 25,000, 6.4, 9,000 nm, Large ports, $25M, 45%), unlocking with the Agriculture type's tiers; slim fast hulls from lime to deep green with the bridge aft and slatted hatch covers over their crates, crews in green shirts and baseball caps. - Five tiers in every cargo line (added 2026-10-15): fifteen new models fill the gaps, so ore, grain, livestock and vehicle carriers, fishing and crabbing boats and seafood carriers each run tier 1 to 5 like the container ships and tankers. Ore: Mini Bulker (tier 1, 250 tons, $15,000) and Handysize (tier 3, 6,000 tons, $500,000). Grain: Grain Coaster (tier 1, 800 tons, $50,000) and Handymax (tier 3, 12,000 tons, $1.2M). Livestock: Stock Barge (tier 1, 100 head, $40,000) and Stock Ship (tier 3, 1,500 head, $900,000). Vehicles: Car Barge (tier 1, 30 vehicles, $120,000) and Short Sea Ro-Ro (tier 2, 80, $400,000). Fishing: Longliner 40 (tier 2, trolled lines inshore and offshore, 6 aboard, trips of 4 to 12 days, 150 crates, $150,000) and Purse Seiner (tier 4, nets offshore, 28 aboard, 10 to 40 days, 6,000 crates, $9M). Crabbing: Pot Boat 42 (tier 2, 80 pots inshore, 6 aboard, 3 to 12 days, 150 crates, $140,000) and Crabber 130 (tier 4, 200 pots offshore and deep, 15 aboard, 14 to 60 days, 2,000 crates, $8M). Seafood carriers: Fish Tender 40 (tier 1, 40 crates, $40,000), Reefer 100 (tier 2, 120, $250,000) and Ocean Reefer (tier 4, 2,500, $10M). Ranges follow the tiers (490, 760, 1,175 and 4,080 nm; the fishing boats' and reefers' a little longer), tier 4 needs a Medium port, the tier 1 to 3 cargo ships fit the St. Lawrence Seaway and the Buffalo carries them, and fuel tanks are sized to finish a full-range leg as wear slows the ship. Each is drawn with its line's art at its own size, except five drawn new: the longliner (line drum, buoys and highflyers aft, wheelhouse forward), the purse seiner (the net piled aft with its corks, the power block on a boom, the seine skiff on the stern), the pot boat (six pots aft), the Crabber 130 (eight pots, a crane, two haulers) and the fish tender (an insulated hold, the fish pump and its hose). Every skin pack dresses them in their line's skins (the new fishing and crabbing boats in a neighbouring tier's). - Speeds keep a 30: 20: 18: 35: 22 ratio (except the Feeder 100, since sped up from 3.63 to 4.82 nm/s), scaled so a Scooter 20 sails Rome→Tunis (324.9 nm) in about 60 s. A Dominator's longest leg (9,000 nm) takes about 42 minutes with wear. - Range is the longest single leg a ship can sail on one tank at 100% maintenance. Farther ports are reached by way of refuel stops (see Refuel stops); fuel is checked again at every departure. - Container ships, car carriers and livestock carriers show their cargo: empty they have a bare deck; loaded, container stacks, rows of cars or animals in the pens fill from the stern forward in quarters (any cargo shows at least a quarter), so part loads show. Smaller ships are drawn on top of bigger ones where they overlap (and a click there picks the smaller one). Colors by family, smallest to biggest: container ships yellow to red (Scooter 20, Feeder 100, Feedermax 200, Trans Atlantic, Dominator), gas tankers light green to dark blue (Bunker Tanker, Lake Tanker, Coastal, Aframax, Supertanker), recovery boats brown, darker = bigger (Buffalo, Mammoth). Zoomed in, ports show their marinas, sized like the ships (see Docks and berths); a ship sailing near a port eases out from where it lay (its anchorage spot or the harbour mouth) and in towards the harbour mouth, facing the way it's really moving, over three times the mouth's distance from the port (the same stretch of sea at every zoom); tugs towing, or turning round by, a lost ship stay alongside it where it's drawn, so they ease into the harbour with it. - The player can own at most 8 of each cargo ship and recovery boat (4 Diesel and 2 Nuclear Icebreakers), once the company level has opened all their slots. - Ship names must be unique (case-insensitive). No renaming in beta. - Selling: a docked ship (not lost, not on a recovery job) sells for 50% of what was paid for it × its maintenance (so a first Buffalo bought for $150,000 sells for up to $75,000), from the ship popup's Sell button (click again to confirm). - Newly bought ships spawn docked with no route at the port picked in the buy popup (\"Launch at\": the HQ, the default, or any hub). - Light and shadow: every building (the HQ, the industry, the lighthouse, the towns' blocks) and every ship casts a shadow away from the sun, in the sun's direction on screen from the view's middle and longer the lower the sun (long at dawn and dusk, short at noon, a soft moonlight length by night), so shadows swing round through the day; the marinas are drawn again as the direction changes. Ships roll with the sea: two swells at the ship's own phase, their size from the sea where it is (a calm swell always, more in a wind, much more in a storm; a ripple at a berth or anchor), drawn as the ship narrowing as it heels, shifting a little to the side it leans, its superstructure and details further than the hull, and its shadow swinging with it. The ships' lights are drawn where the ships were drawn that same frame. - No two companies share a color, in single player or multiplayer: rivals pick free colors, a joining player's picker greys out the colors the game's companies have (and the host refuses one anyway, giving the first free color)."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Cruise lines","u":"cruise-lines.html","x":"- Ten cruise ships in two lines: Ocean cruisers (Coastal Cruiser 200 guests $1.2M, Island Hopper 700 $6M, Panamax Liner 2,000 $25M Medium port, Grand Liner 4,000 $90M Large, Mega Liner 6,500 $250M Giant; 2 to 10 amenity slots) and Expedition cruisers (Expedition Yacht 40 $1.5M, Polar Explorer 100 $7M, Ice Cruiser 200 $25M, Expedition Liner 300 $70M, Polar Flagship 450 $180M; ice class from the Antarctic stations and icebound Arctic ports in season up to the Arctic routes without an icebreaker; helipads on the two biggest; any port). Crews: the tier's officers plus a hand for every 5 guests, times the Crew service level. On the map each cruise ship wears its hull colour, an accent stripe down both sides, its deck colour, and its company's logo on the funnel. - Round trips: a cruise ship's route is its itinerary, the first port its home port (the HQ, a hub, or a port with the company's cruise terminal: $2.5M for the first, $5M each after, built from the Cruise planner), the rest its ports of call, sailing home after the last. At home it takes bookings till its departure rule says go: after 1, 2, 3, 5 or 7 days; at 50, 60, 75, 90 or 100% full; or both (once at the fill it books on till the days are up or it's full; at the days it sails however full). Its guests' tickets are paid as it sails; each port of call charges $6 a guest (none at the company's own hubs) and pays excursion commission (more near wonders and at polar ports); a port of call a storm has closed is sailed past; back home the guests' onboard spending comes in (more with spa, casino, shops...), its supplies, entertainment and amenity upkeep are paid by the guest-night (by the Food and Entertainment levels; \"Cruise supplies\" in the totals), it earns XP for guests x miles, and the voyage gets a review (guest experience 30%, the itinerary 25% less any ports missed, value for money 25%, how smoothly it went 20%). A breakdown on a voyage refunds 15% of its tickets, a loss 75% (40% if it's rescued). - Prices: a price per guest for the voyage for each cabin class (Interior, Ocean View, Balcony, Suite), and a monthly adjustment (-50% to +50%) for each month; a package (cruise only, onboard-inclusive, excursions-inclusive, all-inclusive) puts what guests would spend in the ticket. The recommended price is shown with every factor: the class's price a night x the nights (Expedition x4), x the Cruises rating, x the itinerary (ports of call up to 4, sights near them, market regions, polar ports for Expedition), x the season, x amenities and service, plus the package; the recommended monthly adjustments follow the season of the itinerary's port furthest from the equator (Caribbean winters, European and northern summers, the Antarctic summer, the southern hemisphere, Asia, the tropics). A ship's prices and adjustments follow the recommendations (on a new itinerary and before each voyage books) till the player sets their own; Use the recommended... puts them back. - Bookings: a home port's would-be cruisers a day are 30 x its size x its season, shared by berths among the cruise ships booking there; a class books toward its ceiling, the share of its berths that will pay its price against the recommendation (about 90% at it, 60% at +25%, 30% at +50%, almost none at double; all of them 15% under). The Cruise planner shows the pool, the ship's share and each class's ceiling. - Customization: the cabin mix as shares of the ship's cabin space in 5% steps (an Interior guest takes 1 unit, Ocean View 1.25, Balcony 1.6, Suite 3: more suites, fewer guests); amenities in its deck slots (Ocean: pool deck, theatre, spa, casino, specialty dining, kids' club, waterslide park, nightclub, shops; Expedition: zodiac fleet, lecture hall, observation lounge, spa, dining, shops, kayaks), each with its cost, upkeep, onboard spending and guest experience; looks (hull and stripe in any company colour; teak, white or blue decks). These change in a refit, at the HQ or a hub, paid at once and out of service for half a game day plus a quarter a change. Service levels (Food, Crew, Entertainment: Basic, Standard, Good, Premium, Luxury) change any time. - Guest experience (0-5): amenities for its deck slots, premium cabins (Balconies and Suites), service levels, less crowding, which only comes with a ship packed full of Interior and Ocean View guests (never with over half the space in Suites). - The Cruise planner (a cruise ship's popup): Itinerary (home port, terminal, ports of call and their sights, nights, miles, appeal, season by month; Edit itinerary opens the route screen), Prices (package, prices against recommendations with their factors, monthly adjustments against theirs, the bookings outlook), Departures (the rule; a warning when a fill can't be reached at the prices), Ship (service levels; the refit: cabins, amenities, looks, its cost and days and the experience it makes) and Reviews (its last voyages' money and reviews). A cruise ship's status says how booking is going or where it is on its voyage. - The Cruises rating's parts: guest experience (the fleet's average), itinerary (average appeal), reliability & safety (maintenance; incidents and ports missed per voyage) and value & reviews (the average of recent reviews). - AI rivals with Cruises buy the biggest cruise model they can afford twice over (one more every 10 levels), fit its deck slots, and sail it from a hub to up to 3 of the most appealing ports within reach at the recommended prices. The tutorial has \"Plan a cruise\" and \"Tickets and reviews\" tips, and a Cruises start gets its own welcome."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Fuel, maintenance and port stops","u":"fuel-maintenance-and-port-stops.html","x":"- Every ship has a fuel tank and a maintenance level (0–100%). Both start full when the ship is bought. - Fuel burns at a fixed rate per second at sea, whatever the ship's speed. - Maintenance drops 1% per minute while a running ship is at sea (the Dominator and Mammoth: 0.5% per minute). It doesn't drop while docked or while paused. - Speed = top speed × maintenance, so a ship at 0% stops dead. - Ships keep their speed at sea right up to the harbour mouth and away from it. They get going and slow down inside their marina instead (see Marinas). Near a port a ship sails a curve between its lane and the harbour: leaving, from just outside the harbour mouth (or its spot at the anchorage) round onto its lane; arriving, off its lane and round to the mouth heading in, swinging out to meet the entrance head on, then on into the marina without stopping. The curve takes 3 times the harbour's distance from the port of lane (at least 2 nm; more if the curve is longer, so a ship is never faster than at sea; the two ends share a short leg), gone along evenly, worked out once a leg. - A slower, worn ship takes longer and so burns more fuel per leg. Tanks are sized so a ship leaving at 100% can just finish a leg of its full range (plus 1 s of spare fuel) while wearing on the way. - Random breakdowns start at company level 6. Every 5 s, each ship at sea (never a recovery boat, and never inside a canal) rolls its own chance of losing 50 points of maintenance: 0.105% a roll at 100% maintenance, rising to 1.05% at 0% (worn ships break far more often). That's times the model's sturdiness, times its age (+10% for every hour it has spent at sea, up to double), less its durability skill. A new Scooter at 100% breaks down about once every 80 minutes at sea. At 0% it's lost at sea. A ship that's slowed down can also run out of fuel before it arrives. - Port stop: after arriving, a ship docks for a set time: Scooter 20 5 s, Feeder 100 10 s, Feedermax 200 15 s, Trans Atlantic 30 s, Dominator 1 min. - Cargo unloads over the first half of the stop (the cargo bar drains), and the ship is paid when unloading finishes. Cargo loads over the second half (the bar refills). - With auto-repair on, maintenance refills to 100% over the first 5 s, paid as it goes. With auto-refuel on, the tank refills over the next 5 s. The Scooter 20 does each in 2.5 s to fit its 5 s stop. - The ship can leave when the stop ends. - Docks and berths: a cargo ship's stop needs a berth. Every port has berths by its size band, 6 at a Small port, 8 at a Medium, 10 at a Large, 14 at a Giant, and 14 at a port with any company's HQ or hub, one fewer piers than berths each side (the first berth each side lies along the first pier's seaward face, open to the outer harbour; the last along the land end); every pier is as long as the longest ship the port takes, so any ship the port takes fits any of its berths (kind \"L\" in the config; a Small port's \"s\" berths take only the smaller ships, tiers 1-3, as before). Ships queue first come first served (\"2nd in line\" counts the ships waiting ahead). Berths are shared by every company's ships (in multiplayer too). - Coming in it sails steadily from where it is (its spot at the anchorage, or the mouth, arriving from sea) in through the entrance on its right-hand side, through the outer harbour and down the channel on the side across from its pier (a quarter of the channel's width off its middle, room to come round), round into its slot's mouth bow first, 0.7 of a length before it, and straight on in along the slot's middle line, slowing over its last length; pulling out, it backs straight out of its slot stern first at half its speed until its bow is clear of the pier tips, turns round there in the channel on its thrusters, then steadily out through the entrance on its right-hand side to sea (or to its spot at the anchorage, slowing to anchor there), curving onto its lane and always heading the way it moves. Turning is smooth throughout: its heading eases round every bend rather than stepping, and it slows for any bend tighter than its own length across (never below 15% of its speed; the time it takes counts this too). Weighing anchor (or a tug leaving its moorings) a ship sets off the way it faces, as a boat does, and where that isn't the way it's going comes round on a turning circle 0.8 of its length across, through poses round the circle a quarter turn apart at most; coming to anchor it does the same in reverse, arriving heading into the wind. The only sideways movement in port is the last slide onto the pier, and backing out of a slot. A lane that comes in to a port from behind or beside its jetty (along the coast) rounds the jetty's end, 1.5 of its radius out, to the mouth, so nothing sails through the marina: from behind the marina (level with the jetty's end or nearer) it first runs along past the marina's wall; from further along the coast it makes straight for the jetty's end (turning out by the wall would loop it out to sea and back, as Shanghai's lanes from the north did); and where a plain curve to the mouth clears the jetty anyway it isn't rounded at all. Without a map the old fixed times stand. - Marinas: zoomed in, each port's dot grows into its marina once that's bigger on screen than the dot. It's a rectangular basin dug into the land behind the coast facing the sea, open to the sea, with piers reaching in from its two side walls like a rib cage (half its berths each side, rounded down, the odd berth on the +y side; each as long as the longest ship the port takes, 0.6 dock units wide; the slot between one pier and the next is two ships' widths and 2.1 to spare, room for two ships side by side, each coming straight in), a channel between their tips 1.2 ship lengths wide (at least 4 widths) from the entrance to the land end, the first slot each side open to the outer harbour (one ship: a width and 1.4 to spare before the first pier), and behind the basin a stone quay 0.9 of a length deep (at least 8 units) for the HQ and industry. A ship moors alongside its pier, bow to the wall, tied to the bollards along it: the first each side along the first pier's seaward face, then two to each slot, one along the pier on its land side and one along the pier on its sea side (the last slot's land side its own pier, with turning water past it, 0.6 ship lengths, before the basin's end wall), each backing straight out along its own lane to leave. The jetty is a breakwater curving out round the sea side from where the walls meet the coast, with the entrance in its middle, lit red and green; inside it, the outer harbour where ships come round. Outside the jetty's arms, off to either side (where that's water; clear of the lanes rounding the jetty and of the approach funnel), lie the rocks the birds perch on, each a ship's width or more across, foam round it. Behind the industry's half of the quay a pad of quay reaches 3 units further inland for the port's lighthouse, a round white tower with a red band seen from above. Sizes in dock units (1/75 of a degree): a Small port's basin 14 wide and 8 deep (jetty radius 8), a Medium's 20 by 16 (11), a Large's 26 by 19 (14), a Giant's or hub's 26 by 26 (14), their quays 8 deep. Ports a canal runs through have the canal coming in through a cut in the land end, straight on from the channel. An HQ's or hub's ring shows round its port only while its marina is too small to draw. On the quay behind the basin, on the side with its name (-y), stands its HQ building, seen from above, a rectangular block (no longer curved round the basin) which grows through 10 levels, one every 10 company levels (level 1 to start, 10 from company level 90), from the inland corner towards the basin: a little wooden hut with the company's flag, a wooden house with its color on the eaves, a stone lodge with an annex, a brick office with its sign and roof gear, concrete offices with planters and then a deck with sunshades in its color, glass offices adding a lawn, solar panels and a pool, and at level 10 a tech campus filling its half of the quay, with a roof terrace and a helicopter pad. Taller buildings cast longer shadows. - Seen from above: walls with the roof inset, pitched roofs with a ridge (the sunny side lighter), flat roofs with a parapet and roof gear, East Asian roofs in tiers; a few tall buildings with long shadows (most in the Americas and East Asia); and a landmark on the waterfront in the local manner (a church tower, a minaret and dome, a pagoda, a skyscraper, onion domes, a stupa or a dome). The look is the port's market region's, or its country's where that tells. Laid out once per port by a seed of its id and drawn into its marina's item, once the map's land is sorted. Roads tie it together: a quay road behind the quay with a spine across the quay strip to the basin, a spur to the HQ and a cross road past the industry plots; an avenue inland through the middle of the town with a dashed line down it; a road along each wide street and each cross street, pale walkways along the narrow streets; and a shore road along each side of the basin. At night (and under storms) its windows light up in the style's warmth over a glow across the town, with street lamps along the quay road and the avenue (drawn as one run of dashes per town under the marina's transform, so a night costs about 2 ms at a hub, not 20; see Night and day). Neighbouring towns share the land between them: the bigger port's (by rank) is laid out first and the smaller one keeps off its ground, so no block stands on another town's. No tree or mountain stands on a town either: any land drawing whose picture would reach onto a town's ground is hidden once the town is laid out. - Arriving, a ship queues for a berth, first come first served (\"Waiting at anchor for a berth at Rome (2nd in line)\"), lying at the anchorage while it waits; with one free it comes in (\"Coming alongside at Rome\"): in through the entrance, down the channel, round into its slot and the last slide onto the pier on its thrusters, and is tied to the bollards (bow and stern lines and two springs). Only then does its stop (unloading, repairs, refuelling, loading). When it's done it pulls out (\"Pulling out of Rome for Tunis\"): backing out of its slot, turning in the channel and out to sea. A ship that can't go yet (held, paused, no route, waiting for a full load, money for fuel, a storm, ice) stays at its pier while the port has at least 2 other berths free, else pulls out to the anchorage, freeing the berth, and waits there (topping up its fuel as it can); every 2 s of play a cargo ship lying at the anchorage with nothing to do (waiting, idle, or new) comes in to a pier if the port has more than 3 free, so the anchorage is the overflow, not the rule. Tugs, icebreakers and recovery boats take no berth: tugs moor along the piers, the rest lie at the anchorage. - Points too near the port's marina as it is now are skipped. A ship dropping anchor takes the first free point out of the shipping lanes: one no lane the map draws (every company's routes, just as laid), nor any ship's way between a harbour mouth near it and such a lane (the curves in and out drawn with the lanes, worked out on the ground wherever the view is), passes within its swing of, with 3 nm to spare; only if every point is in a lane does it take the first free one. So ships at anchor wait between the lanes where they can (the anchorage is the overflow: a ship lying in port takes a slip at its moorings first, see Long-term moorings). Which points are in the lanes is worked out again whenever the routes sailed change; ships already lying there stay put. A ship at anchor lies with its bow at its anchor, swung round to face into the wind (see Weather); in a storm it swings and pitches about the anchor. - Berths, queues and the ships coming alongside or pulling out are saved; a ship from an older save that was mid-stop queues to finish it. In multiplayer the host's berths and the docking times are sent to joined games (protocol 3). - Auto-repair, auto-refuel and auto-recovery are per-ship toggles in the ship popup, all on for new ships. - Ships buy cargo only with money beyond the fleet's fuel reserve: what it would cost to top up every ship's tank where it is (or, for a cargo ship at sea, where it's going, counting the fuel it will burn getting there, at its speed now). It's worked out again every 3 s of play, since with a big fleet it's costly. A ship already full in port reserves nothing, so big idle tanks (a Buffalo's 645,000 units) don't lock up the money. - Full loads (the ship popup's Full toggle, on for new and saved cargo ships): a ship only buys a full hold (less anything a canal makes it leave behind), never a part load, and with an empty hold it waits in port until it can afford one (\"Docked at Rome — waiting for money for a full load to Malta\"). A leg where no cargo makes a profit is sailed empty straight away. Waiting isn't treated as a problem (no red dot or log message). With it off, a ship buys as much as the money allows and sails. - A ship only leaves if it has enough fuel for the whole next leg (plus 1 s spare), allowing for the wear it will pick up on the way. So only a breakdown can leave it stranded at sea. Otherwise it is held in port (red dot, a toast, and the reason in its status): refuel is off, it's waiting for money, it's too worn to make the leg on a full tank (turn on repair), or it can't reach where it's bound even by way of refuel stops (assign a new route). A leg beyond one tank is sailed by way of refuel stops (see Refuel stops). - Calling in: a cargo ship lying at the anchorage (waiting, idle, or new) loads, refuels and repairs only in port. Given a route (or able to sail again), it comes in for a stop (queuing for a berth, coming alongside, a full stop: repairs, fuel, loading) when there's a cargo to pick up for its first leg (with full loads on, once it can afford a full hold; until then it waits at anchor, saying so), or when it needs fuel for the leg or is under 95% maintenance and there's money for them. Otherwise (nothing to load, the fuel for the leg and 95% or better) it sails straight from the anchorage. A cargo already aboard stays aboard through such a stop. - Money never goes below $0 but through crew wages (see Economy & time: Debt). Refills buy what the player can afford, and held ships keep buying as money comes in, until they have enough fuel (with auto-refuel on, until the tank is full or the money runs out)."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Refuel stops","u":"refuel-stops.html","x":"- A port beyond one tank is reached by way of refuel stops, for every ship: it sails port to port, each leg one it can sail on a full tank, each stop a port it fits that its company has unlocked (the AI rivals go anywhere), and fills up (and repairs) at each. The way is the shortest in sea miles, each stop counting as 120 nm more so fewer stops win between ways about as long. - A ship bound beyond one tank sails for the next stop on the way, and a port it stops at on the way is only a refuel stop: no trade, its cargo (or a fishing boat's catch) stays aboard for where it's bound, and the stop doesn't move it along its route. Its status says so (\"En route to Valletta to refuel, then on to Piraeus\", \"Refuelling at Valletta on the way to Piraeus\"). A cargo sold where it was bound is paid, and earns XP, from the port it was loaded at, not the last refuel stop. - The Route Assignment screen greys out only the ports the ship can't reach even refuelling (clicking one says why), lists each leg's refuel stops under it in light blue (\"Beyond one tank: refuels at Valletta and Heraklion on the way (no trading there)\", its distance and time counted through them, each stop's port time too), and getting to the first port from where the ship is if that needs them; the full loop counts the refuel stops; on the map the legs are drawn through their stops, each ringed and labelled \"Refuel\". The ship popup's route line ends with its refuel stops."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Lost ships and recovery boats","u":"lost-ships-and-recovery-boats.html","x":"- A ship lost within reach of free tugs based at either end of its leg is towed in by them instead (see Tugs); otherwise a recovery boat goes, as below. - A ship at sea that runs out of fuel or hits 0% maintenance is lost. It stops where it is, gets a red \"!\" above it on the map and a red dot on the Ships screen, and a toast says so. - The Mammoth is a recovery boat ($10,000,000, at most 8). It's the biggest ship, as fast as a Trans Atlantic (6.35 nm/s), and the most expensive to run: 4,500 fuel units/s ($10,800/s) and $300,000 per 1% of repair. - The Buffalo is a smaller recovery boat ($1,250,000, but only $150,000 for the company's first ever Buffalo; at most 8), the size of a Trans Atlantic and as fast. It can only carry Scooter 20s, Feeder 100s, Feedermax 200s and Bunker, Lake and Coastal tankers, costs about a tenth as much to run (450 fuel units/s, $1,080/s, and $30,000 per 1% of repair), wears 1% per minute, and does jobs up to 8,000 nm. - A lost ship's popup has a Send Recovery button. It shows the estimated cost (the recovery boat's fuel and repairs, including its trip home), which boat would go, where it would take the ship, and about how long. You choose whether it's worth it. - The nearest free boat that can carry the ship goes (the cheaper one if two are as near): one that's docked, not refueling, and not on a job. Boats sailing to their base count as busy. A boat only sets off if it has the fuel and maintenance to finish the job. - Auto-recovery (\"Rescue\" toggle in the ship popup, on for new ships): when the ship is lost, the cheapest capable free boat is sent automatically. If none is free, lost ships wait in a queue (longest-lost first) and the next free boat takes them. - A ship at sea that no longer has the fuel or maintenance to reach port (usually after a breakdown) gets an amber \"!\" on the map, \"won't make it!\" in its status, and a toast. - The Mammoth sails the sea lanes to the lost ship's position, through whichever end of the ship's leg is closer. It keeps the heading it came in on, driving on past the ship in line and slowing to a stop clear in front of it, its stern a little past the ship's end (3 s; \"Pulling up ahead of Sea Otter\"), and takes it aboard like a real semi-submersible heavy-lift ship (4.5 s): it ballasts down until its work deck is under water (the sea and ripples drawn over the deck, the wheelhouse still above), reverses under the ship, which floats in over its open stern, then deballasts and rises with the ship centered on deck. It picks ships up facing either way: one it came up behind floats in bow first and rides facing forward, one it met head on floats in stern first and rides facing aft (\"Ballasting down to take Sea Otter aboard\", \"Floating Sea Otter onto its deck\", \"Rising with Sea Otter aboard\"). Only if the port it's carrying the ship to is back the way it came does the pair spin round on the spot, about its middle (2 s; \"Spinning round with Sea Otter aboard\"); then it heads off along the lane. At the port it floats the ship off the same way (4.5 s: ballasts down, the ship slides off over the stern, it rises) before handing it over (\"Floating Sea Otter off at Rome\"). These times count in the job's estimate. - It carries the ship to the nearer end of its leg. If the company has tugs based there, it only carries it to 90% of their longest reach out, floats it off at sea (\"Golden Pipit brought Arctic Parakeet within reach of Suape's tugs\") and sails on into port itself; the ship is lost again there on its leg, and the tugs fetch it in (at once if free, else when auto-recovery next finds them free). The popup's estimate says so (\"Golden Pipit → 135 nm off Suape for its tugs\"). The carried ship is drawn on the boat's open work deck, behind its middle, so the wheelhouse near the bow stays in view. Recovery boats are drawn longer than the ships they carry. - At the ship's destination, it unloads and is paid as usual. - Back at its origin, it gets no pay. It docks (repair and refuel as its toggles say) and tries the leg again. - The Mammoth then docks there (repair and refuel like any ship), sails home, and waits for the next job. Its bars show maintenance, fuel, and \"Tow\" (the ship it's carrying). - Each recovery boat is based at the HQ or a hub, and returns there after a job. Boats are spread evenly across the HQ and hubs, model by model (each location gets its share; locations that already have the most keep any extra one). It rebalances when a hub is founded or a recovery boat is bought or sold: boats already at a location within its share stay, and the rest are re-based to the nearest location still short that a full tank can reach. A re-based boat sails there (paying its own fuel and repairs) once it's free. The Hubs screen lists the boats based at each location. - Recovery boats have no route and no Assign Route or Pause buttons, and never break down at random. They pay their own fuel and repairs: those show on the recovery boat's own line in the Finances tab."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Tugs","u":"tugs.html","x":"- The Tug: a small black tug, drawn at sea with a fender all round, a white wheelhouse and a funnel. Docked, a tug moors along the inside of its marina's jetty, not at the anchorage: each in its own slot (the first free one, kept till it leaves), the two arms by turns, from a little over halfway round each arm (54 degrees from the entrance) towards the entrance, 0.6 dock units apart, bow towards it; with those all taken, on round towards the arms' land ends. It comes home from just inside the entrance across the outer harbour to the land end of its own arm and along the arm to its slot, and leaves along the arm to just inside the entrance and out, so it never crosses the jetty, the basin's corners or the pier tips (on the gray route map, a small black tick across the port's outline, reaching a little inside and out of it, side by side 3 px apart at the top). A service ship like recovery boats: no route or cargo, pays its own fuel and repairs, never lost, no breakdowns. - Slots come from the routes, not levels: two for each port on any cargo ship's route, e.g. one ship on Rotterdam-Hamburg allows 4. The Shop card says \"Owned 1 / 4 (2 per route port)\", and when they're full the button says \"Route to more ports for more\". With no routes yet, buying explains that tugs are based at the ports on your routes. - Each new tug launches straight at the route port with the fewest tugs (ties: the port on the most ships' routes), so they spread evenly; the buy popup shows where (the port can't be picked). When the routes change, a tug at a port no longer on any route (or over its port's two) is re-based to the nearest route port still short of tugs that a full tank (1,500 nm) can reach, and sails there once free; with nowhere to go it stays put. - Job: a ship lost within a tug's reach (150 nm along its lane, +15% per Range upgrade) of either end of its leg is fetched by the free tugs (docked at their base, with the fuel) based at the nearer end, up to two, before any recovery boat is sent (auto-recovery and the Send Recovery button alike; the button shows \"Tugger and Puller → Rotterdam, about 43 s\"). They sail out along the lane one after the other (the second leaves 1.5 s after the first, \"Following Tugger out to tow Hauler\", so they head out in line), stop short ahead of the ship's bow, turn round on a turning circle to face home (3 s), and once both have, tow it in. Both pull from ahead of its bow on tow lines (1.2 tug lengths): one tug on a single line, two side by side in a tight Y, their lines from the bow the same length. Each tows at half its speed, added up, so two bring a ship in twice as fast as one. The towed ship comes round from the way it lay to the way it's towed over its first 3 nm. At the tugs' port the ship is handed over (paid as usual if that's where it was going, else nothing), and the tugs bring it in: - With a berth free (it takes one at once, as it would next step): they pull it straight in through the mouth, through the outer harbour and down the channel to where it comes round for its slot. There they let go, easing to a stop beside it, away from its slot and inside the channel, clear of the pier tips (where the channel is too narrow for the second tug beside the first it stops behind it; \"Bringing Hauler alongside at Rotterdam\"), and lie there while it goes on in by itself at half speed, round into its slot and the last slide onto its pier; then they go out along the channel's middle and home by their arm's land end to their moorings. Its time coming alongside is all this. - With no berth free: they cast off at the harbour mouth and go in to their moorings by their arm's land end; the ship glides on to its spot at the anchorage to wait for a berth, as any ship coming in to wait does. - Tugs always go the way they face, as boats do (never sideways): along their lanes and the tow's track (the lane, the curve into the harbour, on in through the mouth), round smooth turns (0.7 of their length), backing straight off at most. Every move is worked out ahead from the ship's own way in, so each tug's track is smooth from heading out to docking again. - Upgrades: tugs level up from their jobs, and spend points on Speed, Range (reach +15%) and Efficiency (fuel), in place of Durability. - Statuses: \"Standing by at Rotterdam\", \"Heading out to tow Hauler\", \"Turning round to take Hauler in tow\", \"Waiting for Puller to take Hauler in tow\", \"Towing Hauler to Rotterdam with Puller\", \"Bringing Hauler alongside at Rotterdam with Puller\", and on the towed ship \"Being towed to Rotterdam by Tugger and Puller\". The activity log says \"Auto-recovery: Tugger and Puller sent out from Rotterdam for Hauler (about $339)\" and \"Tugger and Puller towed Hauler in to Rotterdam\". A tow under way is saved with the game. - Tugs show in the Shop's Tugs section, the Ships tab's \"Recovery boats and tugs\" filter and Finances' Tugs group; a tile reads \"Tug at Rotterdam · Lv 2\". - Tugs cost 20% less for every third of the field guide's animals the company has seen, for every tug bought from then on, Buy All Tugs too; the Shop's tug card shows \"$40,000 (20% off: animals seen)\", its price's tooltip how it works."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Tugs working a ship in","u":"tugs-working-a-ship-in.html","x":"- A ship towed in by tugs is brought to its berth (chosen as any ship's is) by a series of real tug manoeuvres, worked out for its own pier. Every tug only ever goes the way it faces (ahead, or straight astern), turns on the spot (harbour tugs can) or steers round, or lies with its fender on the hull pushing; the ship only turns about a point or goes straight along its length: nothing slips sideways, and nothing backs through a pier. 1. Towed in: the lead tug pulls on its line ahead; the other lets go inside the breakwater, waits beside the way in while the ship passes, then makes fast astern on a line of its own (drawn from its bow to the ship's stern) to steady it. The ship stops in the channel abreast of its slot; the lead eases out to the side away from the slot and stops beside or ahead of the bow, short of the basin's end. 2. Stations: each lets go and goes round to push: out to the side, along, turned square to the hull and in until its fender's on it; the lead on the bow from the side away from the slot, the other on the stern from the slot's side. 3. Turned: pushing bow and stern opposite ways they turn it about a point near its middle until it faces straight into its slot (where it couldn't lie along the channel abreast of its slot, it's towed round a quarter circle on the hip instead). 4. Pushed in: with no room astern of it yet (a long ship in a narrow channel: a tug there would be in the slot across the way), the one pushing makes fast alongside its stern quarter on the hip before it's turned (approached from astern along the channel, where there's room) and pushes it part way in made fast there; then it lets go, comes round astern, its bow on the stern, and pushes it the rest of the way in a few pushes, easing off between. The lead backs off and stands by. 5. Clear: the pusher backs straight out of the slot to just past the channel's middle and steers round for the way out; then both go to their moorings. - Which point it turns about, where the tugs push, which side the hip tug lies, whether it's turned about a point or round a curve, and whether the tugs turn on the spot or steer round are chosen for each pier, ship size and team from a list of ways, the first that keeps the ship and tugs clear of the piers, the basin's walls, the breakwater and the ships at every other berth (each taken to hold the biggest ship it can) at every moment, with no jumps, snaps or sideways slips. All 3,592 cases (every berth of the four marina sizes, every ship size and small ships sharing a big berth towards either end, one tug and two, either side of the entrance) were checked at 400 moments each and all are clear. The berthing takes the whole of the work (about 40 to 60 s of play). Tugs in port draw no wake of their own. - Marinas got turning water for it: the last slot each side has its own pier on its land side (no berth lies against the basin's end wall any more), and the basin runs on past it by 0.6 of the longest ship's length of open water, the quay, HQ, industry and town behind it moving back with it."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Icebreakers","u":"icebreakers.html","x":"- Only needed situationally, for trade where seasonal ice closes or slows things (in saves with seasonal closures); the Shop's section says so (\"Icebreakers (for seasonal ice)\"), as do each card and the Tutorial's Icebreakers tip. - Two models: the Diesel Icebreaker (level 8, $8,000,000, $3,000,000 for the company's first, at most 4, 4.5 nm/s, fits the Seaway) and the Nuclear Icebreaker (level 30, $75,000,000, at most 2, 5.5 nm/s, burns little fuel but costly to repair; too big for the Seaway; its Arctic role is to come). Service ships, like recovery boats: no route or cargo, pay their own fuel and repairs, never lost, no breakdowns or tolls, slots grow like recovery boats'. - Each is stationed at an ice area from the Station picker in its popup: the Gulf of Finland (Helsinki, St Petersburg, Ust-Luga, Tallinn), the Sea of Okhotsk (Magadan, Vanino), the White Sea (Arkhangelsk), the Great Lakes (any lake port; not the nuclear one), or, nuclear only, the Northern Sea Route or the Northwest Passage (see The Arctic). It sails to the nearest station port and waits there; \"Not stationed\" leaves it where it is. - Sea of Okhotsk and Great Lakes: while the ice is in and an icebreaker is stationed, the ice closure no longer stops ships (the Seaway, Welland and Soo locks still shut). A ship whose lane reaches the ice edge stops there, engines off (\"Waiting at the edge of the Sea of Okhotsk ice for an icebreaker\"); one leaving a port in the ice waits in port. The icebreaker (from wherever it is in its area) sails out, through whichever end of the lane is nearer, and leads the ship and up to 6 more waiting on the same lane, the same way, through the ice (8 boats with the icebreaker): 30 nm apart, so each shows on its own from the opening zoom (they overlap a little zoomed right out), at 65% (diesel) or 85% (nuclear) of the slowest of them (\"Following Krasin through the Sea of Okhotsk ice\"). Ships burn fuel and wear for the miles they make. Into an iced port, the convoy arrives and the icebreaker docks there; out of one, it lets the ships go at the ice edge and turns back. Its turns (to face the way it leads, if it came in the other way, and to head back from the ice edge) take 3 s each, sailed round a turning circle rather than pivoting. While the ice is in, an icebreaker stays wherever in its area it docked, ready for the next convoy; otherwise it goes home to its station port. First come, first served. - Gulf of Finland: its ports never shut, so there are no convoys: a stationed icebreaker keeps the fairways open, and ships in the ice sail at 80% speed and 1.1x wear instead of crawling at 45% and 1.5x. - Statuses: \"Standing by at Vanino for the Sea of Okhotsk ice\", \"Heading out to lead Hauler\", \"Leading 3 ships through the Sea of Okhotsk ice\", \"Heading back to Vanino\", \"Standing by at Tallinn (the Gulf of Finland isn't frozen now)\", \"Not stationed at Vladivostok (only needed in seasonal ice)\". A port's popup says \"Iced in until 15 Apr: your icebreaker leads ships in and out\", and the activity log notes \"Magadan and Vanino are iced in until 15 Apr, but your icebreakers will lead ships through.\" - Convoys aren't saved: one under way when the game is saved forms again after loading."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Long-term moorings","u":"long-term-moorings.html","x":"- Those runs of street are the port's long-term moorings instead: each becomes a pontoon, a floating walkway along the street's line with dark floats under it and plank seams close in, with finger piers out from both sides and a slip between each finger and the next, every finger and slip only where the water really is and clear of the marina's own ground (the quay, the basin, the harbour inside the jetty), as long as the longest ship of any kind (the Mammoth) and its beam and a gap wide (since 2026-10-13: the moorings are every ship's long-term parking, the recovery boats' too; before, the port's own longest cargo ship), for a ship to come straight in, bow to the pontoon; a cleat at each finger's root, a lamp at each pontoon's end at night. The quay road and the cross roads are laid first, then the avenue and the streets, each keeping clear of the others' fingers; and the side streets' lines along either side of the harbour just outside the quay land (one row at a Small or Medium port, two at a Large, three at a Giant or a hub), so a port with water beside its marina (Shanghai, Singapore, Hong Kong, Auckland, Honolulu, Los Angeles...) gets a mooring field flanking it. A pontoon must lie along a shore or the quay, or touch land at an end (none floats off by itself), and is worth building only for 2 slips or more. The roads keep to their runs on land. Wood at ports of 8 berths or fewer, concrete at the bigger. The side rows start beyond the way ships round the jetty's end (1.5 jetty radii out), and a row only beyond a built row nearer the quay, so no field floats off by itself; a street pontoon stays within 14 units of its own quay (not out by some other islet). With the game's 322 ports, 146 have moorings, about 850 slips in all. - No mooring over a neighbour's marina (2026-10-14): a pontoon any of whose walkway, slips or fingers lies on a neighbouring port's quay or harbour (Hong Kong's over Shenzhen's, where ports are close) isn't built. - It goes to the left or right of the entrance beyond the way ships round the jetty's end (1.5 jetty radii out), out of the way of the routes, which leave straight out to sea: of every place it fits (all in the water, clear of the marina and its neighbours', and the water off its slips reachable by water from just outside the entrance, not a lagoon behind the coast), the nearest the entrance with the shore behind it. Where it fits nowhere beside the jetty (a port at the head of a narrow bay or up a river: Melbourne's, Hamburg's) it may go anywhere from 6 units plus a beam either side of the line straight out of the entrance to 40 units beyond the jetty; and in a narrow inlet or channel (Ain Sokhna's), along the shore either side of the way in, its teeth pointing into the channel. Till then the port's ships lie at anchor and move to a slip once there is one. Lakes count as water for the moorings and their ways, so the Great Lakes ports have moorings too. Anchorage, Port Tewfik, Aqaba (their channels too narrow) and Dunedin (its marina inland as drawn) have none, and St Petersburg's street moorings have no way to them and no comb fits there. - The staging point outside a slip is pulled back along the slip to the last of the water. A mooring with no way to it by water at all (a field on a lake behind the town) is drawn but never given a ship. - A cargo ship lying in port without a berth (waiting for a load, idle, paused, or for fuel money; service ships too, if they fit) takes the first free slip it fits, or if the port has none (or they're full) its spot at the anchorage as before; it sails to it from the entrance out round the jetty's end on its side, round the back of the quay if the slip is behind it, up to the slip's mouth and straight in, and leaves the same way: backing straight out of the slip to its mouth, turning there on its thrusters, and back round by the way it came, at its own speed (its time in to a berth counts all of it, as does its time out to the moorings, so it never hurries). An idle or paused ship stays at the moorings, its long-term parking, till it has somewhere to go: it isn't brought in to a pier while the port has room (only running ships are), and one done with its stop pulls out to the moorings. Tugs still moor along the jetty's arms."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"People on deck","u":"people-on-deck.html","x":"- Zoomed right in (the map now zooms to 6,000 px a degree, from 800; people show from about 1,600), every ship has its people about its deck, seen from above: a hat (or hair) on a head, shoulders in their shirt's colour, legs in their trousers' colour showing as they walk, and a shadow. Where they can go is each ship art's layout. - Who's out: a big crew keeps three 8-hour watches, a third of the crew on deck at once, changing at 00:00, 08:00 and 16:00 by the ship's own clock (its longitude), the outgoing watch walking back in to the door as the next comes out. A crew of 8 or fewer works 14-hour days and turns in in turn round the clock, so the boat never stops and someone's always at the helm. The officer of the watch keeps to the bridge wings (the helm on a small boat); the rest wander the decks, stand looking over the side, and go to work: two at the bow and two at the stern with lines while the ship comes alongside, lies there its first and last moments and pulls out; an anchor watch at the windlass at the anchorage; and at the fishing grounds the gear: by the rods while trolling, a strike every 40 seconds or so (one fighting the fish with the rod bent and the line taut astern, one beside them with the gaff), at the net reel, at the trawl winches, or at a crabber's pot hauler with the rest carrying pots from it to the stacks and back. - Dock workers (the port's, in hard hats and hi-vis) stand at the bollards on the quay beside a ship coming alongside or pulling out, and through its stop a gang works its cargo along the quay beside it, as many as it needs (a container ship 2 more a tier, bulk carriers and livestock 1 more a tier, a tanker 2 at its manifold, car carriers 3 and up, reefers 3 and up, a fishing boat 1). The bollards are now drawn their real size beside them, and a ship's shadow is its hull's own outline. - Uniforms: every ship line dresses its captain and mates one way and its crew another, by default as it would at sea: container ships a white-shirted captain in a captain's cap and crew in hard hats and hi-vis orange; tankers crew in red coveralls; livestock carriers cowboy hats; crabbers orange oilskins and watch caps; fishing boats bucket hats; icebreakers pompom beanies; tugs and recovery boats a fisherman's cap for the skipper. The Uniforms page (the Ships screen's Uniforms button, or Uniform on a ship's Crew page, opening on its line) changes any line's captain or crew outfit: a hat from the 20 (the 12 the portraits had, and a fisherman's cap, flat cap, beret, bicorne, pompom beanie, fedora, pith helmet and conical hat, which the CEO and officers' portraits can wear too), a shirt from 18 colours and trousers from 13, each with a figure walking on the spot to show it and Default to put it back. A ship's officers' portraits wear their line's captain's hat. Saved with the company, set as a command in multiplayer."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Wakes","u":"wakes.html","x":"- Every ship under way leaves a wake on the water, under its shadow and hull: never inside a marina, so no boat of any kind wakes the harbour; two arms of foam spreading astern from its quarters at the Kelvin angle (19.5°) and the wash of its screws between them, a ribbon of foam tapering and fading away. The wake runs back along the track the stern has just sailed, straight on past the track's end, so it bends with the ship's turns. Its length and brightness go with the ship's speed now as a share of its top speed: half a length astern at a crawl, three and a half at full speed. A ship coming alongside, pulling out or gliding to its spot moves at harbour pace (a quarter); a ship lying in port, held at a lock or by the weather, lost adrift, carried or towed leaves none (the tug's shows). Ships drawn under 7 px long have none. On the loading screens each ship's wake goes with its crossing speed."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Fishing and crabbing","u":"fishing-and-crabbing.html","x":"- Three new ship lines: fishing boats, crabbing boats and seafood carriers. Fishing: the Sport Fisher 24 (tier 1, from level 1 beside the Scooter: a 24 ft centre console, 4 aboard, trolls lines, trips of 3 to 7 days, 48 crates), the Sportfisher 60 (tier 3, level 14: a 60 ft sportfisher with a flybridge and outriggers that also runs nets, 16 aboard, trips of 5 to 21 days, 400 crates) and the Factory Trawler (tier 5, level 48: nets and trawls, 80 aboard, out 20 to 90 days, 40,000 crates). Crabbing, every crab caught in pots: the Crab Skiff 24 (tier 1, level 1: an aluminium skiff with 30 small pots, trips of 2 to 5 days, 30 crates), the Crabber 98 (tier 3, level 16: 150 pots, 18 aboard, 500 crates) and the Bering Sea Crabber (tier 5, level 50: 250 big pots, out 30 to 120 days, 6,000 crates; it can stay out all winter on the king crab like the Deadliest Catch boats). Carriers: the Reefer 300 (tier 3, level 16) and the Polar Reefer (tier 5, level 54), refrigerated ships for the catch. Each boat has its own top-down art, and works only the grounds its gear and size can: small boats inshore, the tier 3 boats inshore and offshore, the tier 5 boats offshore and the deep grounds. Their crews are their own, not their tier's. - What's caught: 61 species in 16 families. Every commercially caught crab (24: red, blue, golden and southern king crab; snow, Tanner, red snow and horsehair crab; Dungeness; Atlantic blue crab; Florida stone crab; brown, Jonah, Atlantic and Pacific rock, spider, velvet and spanner crab; gazami, blue swimmer and mud crab; deep-sea red, golden and Tasmanian giant crab) and 37 fish: the game fish already swimming on the map (tuna, billfish, salmon, cod, halibut, mahi, wahoo...) and 13 new ones that swim on the map too, in 32 schools in their real waters. Each species has its gear (troll, net, trawl or pot) and a grade (its price over its family's: a bluefin 3x a tuna). A trophy fish (bluefin, the marlins, sailfish, swordfish, halibut) can be hooked by a small trolling boat, filling its hold on its own (\"Reel Deal hooked a giant bluefin tuna\"). - 95 real fishing grounds, each with its species and their real seasons (months), how thick the catch is, how much it holds (stock), how deep (inshore, offshore, deep) and its rough months: Bristol Bay's red king crab from October to January, the Pribilofs and St. Matthew's blue king crab, the Bering Sea shelf's snow crab and pollock in winter, the Aleutians' golden king crab, Dungeness off Southeast Alaska, British Columbia, Washington, Oregon and California, Chesapeake Bay and Pamlico Sound's blue crab, Florida's stone crab, the Gulf of St. Lawrence and Grand Banks snow crab, the Barents Sea's king crab and cod, Lofoten's skrei, the North Sea and Norwegian Sea herring and mackerel, the Peruvian anchoveta, the Yellow and East China Seas' gazami, the Sea of Japan's red snow crab, Hokkaido's saury and hair crab, the Kona coast's blue marlin, Cabo's striped marlin, the Florida Keys' sailfish and many more. Each ground has its own fishing routes, straight out to its middle from every port within 1,600 nm as the crow flies (and at least its four nearest within 2,500), so a boat sails straight from its base to the ground and home (a far ground, beyond its base's routes, by way of the port nearest it); routed round the land like any lane and kept with them; a ground's end works like a port for the lanes. - Crayfish on the Great Lakes (added 2026-10-13): the six lake grounds (off Thunder Bay, Duluth-Superior, Calcite, Toronto, Milwaukee and Detroit) also hold two trap-fished crayfish, the rusty crayfish (May to October) and the northern crayfish (June to September), so crabbers have a catch on the lakes; a Crayfish seafood family at $900 a crate, cheapest at Detroit, Toronto and Milwaukee and most expensive overseas, carried by every fishing boat and reefer, with its own crab-style art. - A fishing or crabbing boat is bought like any ship and based at any port in its popup (its Base: it sails there once in port). It goes after what its Target says: \"Most timely and profitable\" (chosen again for every trip from what's in season within its reach, by what a day at sea of it is worth landed at its base, the trip there and back counted and the stock as it is), or a species and ground of the player's choosing, the list showing only what its gear and size can work within reach of its base, with each one's season and value a day. A trip: out along the lanes to the ground, working it (its hold filling at its rate x the species' density there x the stock left, slowed in a storm; fuel burned at 35% of its sailing rate; wear, more in a storm and 2.2x in the ground's rough months: the Bering Sea in winter), and home again with a full hold, at the end of its time out (its most days less the sailing, or what its tank allows), when the season closes, or when it needs what it has left to get home. It can be lost at sea like a cargo ship (out of fuel, broken down, battered in the rough months) and is fetched by a recovery boat or tugs like any. Out of season it waits in port (\"At Norfolk: nothing it can catch is in season within reach\"). Its status says what it's doing (\"Crabbing for red king crab at Bristol Bay: 3,200 / 6,000 crates\"), its Catch bar how full it is, and on the map a crabber at work has its string of pot buoys bobbing beside it, a trolling boat its lines streaming astern, a netter its purse seine ringed with floats, a trawler its warps out to the net. - Inshore trolling at every port (added 2026-10-11): 40 more trolled species, each with its real season, a fact and its art: Spanish mackerel, bluefish, bonito, little tunny, skipjack, greater amberjack, sea trout, Atlantic salmon, Arctic char, pollack, saithe, European sea bass, cobia, red drum, queenfish, barramundi, largehead hairtail, lake trout, walleye, snoek, Australian salmon (kahawai), northern pike, zander, rainbow runner, dogtooth tuna, coral trout, snook, spotted seatrout, kob (meagre), leerfish, dentex, lingcod, rockfish, Japanese sea bass, pink snapper, grouper (hamour), muskellunge, smallmouth bass, garfish and mangrove jack, and the Antarctic toothfish and marbled rockcod for the Antarctic stations (101 species in all). 234 inshore grounds (\"the Japanese coast off Kobe\", ids off_<port>) lie a few miles off the ports a Sport Fisher based there couldn't otherwise fish, on each port's nearest anchor point (Punta Arenas's in the Strait of Magellan), shared with the ports near them, each stocked from its sea's list (46 seas: the Great Lakes, the Baltic, the Gulf of Mexico, the Red Sea, the East China Sea...) with the southern hemisphere's seasons its own. A Sport Fisher based at any of the 322 ports has at least 4 months a year with something in season and at least 2 species to go after; the typical port has all 12 months and 5 species, and the leanest are the Arctic ports' 4-month summer (char with saithe, salmon or pike). 329 grounds in all, with 1,315 lanes. - Fishing boats port like cargo ships: home from a trip they queue for a berth, come alongside and land the catch during their stop, then pull out (to the moorings while they wait for a season, a spot on a ground or fuel money, or straight out to sea for the next trip). A boat at its pier with a trip to make pulls out bound for its first leg's end and sets off on the trip the moment it's clear of the pier; a boat setting off from a pontoon mooring (a boat that waited there, an idle ship given a route) sails out the way it came in and out through the mouth. - Trips are planned to bring the boat home with at least 70% maintenance on a normal day, and a boat at work turns for home when what's left would take it under 70% (as it does when its fuel or its days run out); the boats' wear rates and the two biggest boats' tanks were set so their longest trips at full range fit. Fishing boats break down at random like cargo ships (a hit takes half their maintenance; at 0% they're lost at sea and fetched by tugs or a recovery boat like any ship). At most 4 boats (every company's) work a ground at once: a boat whose best choice is full takes the next best, or waits (\"Taipei has 4 boats on it already\"). Each boat on a ground has its own spot round its middle on the water and clear of every marina, its lines and loop too, sailing to it on a curve off its lane (heading the way it goes, never sliding sideways, round by the ground's middle where a plain curve would cross land, straight on into its loop) over as much of the lane's end as the curve is long (at least 12 nm; up to where its port's harbour curve starts), so it's never faster than at sea, and home the same way from just where it stopped work; trollers and trawlers work slow loops round their spot, crabbers and netters lie by their gear. Only at work is their gear out: the Sport Fisher 24's rods angled out over the sides and the Sportfisher 60's outriggers spread, and the lines and nets in the water; sailing or in port the rods stand in their holders and the outriggers are folded back by the cabin. Sailing, each fishing boat keeps its own way up to 1.6 nm off to one side of the lane (eased in from the ports, never over land), so boats on one lane don't sit on top of each other. - Stocks: each ground's species is drawn down by every boat's catch and grows back 1.6x what's missing a year, so a fished-out ground catches less (its catch x stock^0.7) and the best target moves on. - The catch lands at the boat's base as crates of its family at that port's price (its grade in): sold there, or put in the company's cold store there (2026-10-14: only at its HQ or a hub, the only ports with its freezers; anywhere else it sells) for its seafood carriers (Catch in the popup: by default stored where one of the company's carriers calls, else sold). A carrier loading at a port takes the cold store's catch first (the family paying most where it's going, free: the boat's ledger counted it as income, the carrier's as its cost), else buys seafood at the market like any cargo. The seafood families are 16 commodities, cheapest where they're landed (each family's three biggest landing ports are its sources) and most expensive where they're wanted (East Asia pays most for crab and tuna, Europe for cod and whitefish), so carriers make money taking them from Dutch Harbor to Tokyo; every hub can build a family's plant. Landed catch earns XP like a delivery over the miles out and back, and the seafood families have achievement stars in crates. - The field guide has a Catch tab: every crab and fish, family by family, with where and when it's caught, how, what a crate fetches and a fact. AI rivals don't fish (they may run seafood carriers). Deckhand Joe introduces fishing in the tutorial."},{"p":"Ships and crews","t":"Choosing a fishing base","u":"choosing-a-fishing-base.html","x":"- In a fishing or crabbing boat's popup, the Base list shows beside each port how many months of the year the boat could fish from there (any species in season on a ground its gear and size can work within its range) and how many species, with each species' months in the port's tooltip (\"Brown crab (edible crab): 8 months (Apr to Nov)\"), longest season first; \"nothing within reach\" otherwise. The names show at once and the months fill in when ready."},{"p":"","t":"The world","u":"world.html","x":""},{"p":"The world","t":"World","u":"world.html","x":"- The whole Earth as a globe, seen from space (an orthographic view, north kept up), like Google Earth. Drag to turn it (the map follows the mouse; let go while moving and it keeps spinning, slowing to a stop), and zoom with the mouse wheel (easing in and out, about the point under the mouse), or on a trackpad by pinching (about the fingers) or swiping two fingers up and down like the wheel, or with the + and - keys, or the World map's + and − buttons at its bottom right, from the whole globe (90% of the view's height across) down to a close view of a harbor. The view can turn right over the poles (Antarctica's ice and the deep Arctic Ocean reach them). Around the globe is dark space with a soft blue glow of air at its edge; the globe darkens a little and fades into haze towards its edge. - How it's drawn: the map data stays in Web Mercator map coordinates. Ships' headings and the docks turn with the globe's north where they are. The biomes are cut to the land by a land mask drawn off screen. Sizes set in projected degrees (canal channels, locks, the land drawings' spacing) shrink towards the poles as the land does; ships and docks keep their size in the world. Piers show once a port's docks are 8 px out to the mouth. - Keeping the map fast (zoomed in to the marinas, and dragging): each port's marina is drawn once into its own canvas item at the zoom it's drawn at and then only moved and turned with the view (drawn again only when the zoom changes, the port changes size or becomes a hub, or its HQ building grows). Label text sizes are measured once, and labels, ports, land drawings and lanes' points are kept on the unit sphere (they never move) and looked up by cell of the globe near the view, so off-screen ones cost nothing (a long segment, open ocean between two points far apart, is drawn in pieces along the path the ships sail, straight in the map's projection, which bends with the globe zoomed out, so a ship's dot sits on its line: before, the straight screen line between the points left dots up to 30 px off at the widest zooms); the route lines' harbour curves are worked out once a zoom (and again after the view moves a degree) and kept on the globe; the land mask under the biomes is drawn again only when the view changes; birds' shapes are a bird a unit across, scaled as they're drawn, and their colors read once; what birds are where is kept for a game minute; sea animals' wander spots and the calendar year are worked out once. None of it changes how anything plays or looks. - Map art: blue sea, sandy land with a darker coastline, thin country borders, lakes (drawn with a shoreline, each in a simplified version of its real color: clear blue for most fresh water, deeper blue for very deep clear lakes like Baikal, Tanganyika, Malawi, Titicaca and the upper Great Lakes, green for shallow plant-rich ones like Victoria, Erie and Winnipeg, muddy for Chad and Tonle Sap, turquoise for glacial and salt lakes like the Dead Sea, jade for Turkana, pink for red-algae ones like the Syvash, and salt-white for dry salt pans like Lake Eyre and inland Australia's; islands in them are land; plain gray on the route map), thin blue rivers (the biggest show zoomed out, smaller ones appear as you zoom in; visual only, ships don't sail them), and country names that appear as you zoom in (placeholder colors, all in the theme). - Taken out: Los Angeles, Indiana Harbor, Burns Harbor, Algeciras and Portland, Oregon. In older saves, anything at or bound for one (ships, routes, hubs, home ports) goes to its neighbour instead: Long Beach, Gary, Gary, Tanger Med and Seattle (a route stopping twice in a row at one port stops there once; two hubs at one port keep the HQ). Chicago sits on the downtown lakefront and Gary a little east of its old spot, turned a little left, so the two stay clear of each other even as full-sized ports (not both as hubs); Philadelphia a little south-west, facing down the Delaware; Hong Kong a little south-west and Shenzhen a little north-east; Tokyo north-east and Yokohama south-west of where they were, Yokohama facing south down Tokyo Bay; Kobe north-west and Osaka south, apart across Osaka Bay; Tanjung Pelepas a little north-west and Singapore a little east; Ningbo-Zhoushan facing east by south-east. - The world's biggest container ports (the top 100, plus the next several so that 100 are new beyond the original Mediterranean set). Ranks 1–25 follow the published 2023 figures; the rest follow recent Lloyd's List rankings as best known (the full list is paywalled), so a few borderline ports may differ from the official list. - The 18 biggest Mediterranean container ports, plus Rome (Civitavecchia) and Tunis (Radès). - Mediterranean islands: Limassol (Cyprus), Palermo (Sicily), Cagliari (Sardinia), Bastia (Corsica), Palma (Mallorca). - Also: Boston, Miami, Monterey, Anchorage, Seattle-Tacoma, Cabo San Lucas. - Added for coverage: New Orleans, Havana, Port-au-Prince, San Juan, Bridgetown, Cancun, Puerto Quetzal (Guatemala), Puntarenas (Costa Rica), Puerto Ayora (Galapagos); Honolulu, Nawiliwili (Kauai), Apia (Samoa), Pago Pago (American Samoa), Nuku'alofa (Tonga), Suva (Fiji), Papeete (French Polynesia), Auckland, Wellington, Apra Harbor (Guam), Port Moresby, Cebu, Muara (Brunei); Toamasina (Madagascar), Praia (Cabo Verde); Dublin, Douglas (Isle of Man), Reykjavik, Nuuk, Juneau, Dutch Harbor (Aleutians); Gothenburg, Oslo, Copenhagen, Aarhus, Bergen, Tallinn, Riga, Klaipeda; Durres; Constanta, Odesa, Novorossiysk. - Added to reach 200: Saint-Pierre, Portland (Maine), Nain (Labrador), Hamilton (Bermuda), Ponta Delgada and Faja Grande (Azores), Funchal (Madeira), Porto, Nantes-Saint-Nazaire; Hilo and Kawaihae (Big Island of Hawaii), Malakal (Palau), Vladivostok, Korsakov (Sakhalin), Magadan, Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky; Rio de Janeiro, Salvador, Fortaleza, Buenaventura, Antofagasta, Punta Arenas; Banjul, Freetown, Luanda, Maputo. - Added with Suez and the Great Lakes: Ain Sokhna and Port Tewfik (Egypt, south end of the Suez Canal), Eilat (Israel), Noumea (New Caledonia), Flying Fish Cove (Christmas Island), Midway Atoll; the 15 biggest Great Lakes ports (Duluth-Superior, Two Harbors, Thunder Bay, Presque Isle on Superior; Chicago, Indiana Harbor, Burns Harbor, Gary, Milwaukee on Michigan; Calcite on Huron; Detroit, Toledo, Cleveland on Erie; Toronto, Hamilton on Ontario); and six Antarctic stations. - Added to reach 300 (ranks 228-300, after the rest): a port for every coastal country of over a million people that had none: Helsinki (Finland), Ust-Luga (Russia's Baltic), Rijeka (Croatia), Varna (Bulgaria), Poti (Georgia), Latakia (Syria), Beirut (Lebanon), Algiers (Algeria), Misrata (Libya), Aqaba (Jordan), Aden (Yemen), Khalifa Bin Salman (Bahrain), Shuwaikh (Kuwait), Umm Qasr (Iraq), Port Sudan (Sudan), Massawa (Eritrea), Mogadishu (Somalia), Dar es Salaam (Tanzania), Walvis Bay (Namibia), Nouakchott (Mauritania), Dakar (Senegal), Bissau (Guinea-Bissau), Conakry (Guinea), Monrovia (Liberia), Cotonou (Benin), Douala (Cameroon), Malabo (Equatorial Guinea), Owendo (Gabon), Pointe-Noire (Republic of the Congo), Banana (DR Congo, at the Congo's mouth), Yangon (Thilawa; Myanmar), Sihanoukville (Cambodia), Dili (Timor-Leste), Nampo (North Korea), Puerto Cortes (Honduras), Acajutla (El Salvador), Corinto (Nicaragua), Puerto Cabello (Venezuela), Caucedo (Dominican Republic), Port of Spain (Trinidad and Tobago), Montevideo (Uruguay); and more in the thinner regions: Kolkata (Haldia), Visakhapatnam, Kochi, Paradip, Mongla; Belawan, Makassar, Balikpapan, Davao, Da Nang; Cape Town, Beira, Nacala, Onne, Lobito; Vostochny, Vanino; Suape, Vila do Conde, Vitoria, Itajai, Rio Grande, Bahia Blanca, Valparaiso; Baltimore, Philadelphia, Jacksonville, Halifax, Mobile; Fremantle, Adelaide, Darwin. Bosnia (Neum) and Gaza have coast but no working commercial port. Then Buffalo (New York, on Lake Erie at the Welland Canal's south end; rank 301): its clothing and coffee 2% more expensive, so a Scooter makes about $1,300-1,600 to Toronto or Hamilton and $1,000-2,700 to Toledo or Detroit. - Added later (ranks 318-326, Northern Europe & Atlantic market): Liverpool, Immingham and Teesport (England's west and east coasts), Milford Haven (Wales), Grangemouth and Greenock for Glasgow (Scotland), Las Palmas (Canary Islands), Bilbao (northern Spain) and Tórshavn (Faroe Islands). - The map opens centered on the company's home port (45 degrees of longitude across); the route screen opens centered on the ship. - The land is drawn in simplified biomes, each one flat color with sharp straight edges between them and no shading: ice, tundra (and cold high plateaus like Tibet), boreal forest, temperate forest, tropical rainforest, grassland and steppe, savanna and dry scrub, and desert. Each is worked out from Natural Earth II's land cover color and the latitude, then smoothed into simple patches. The coastline stays as sharp as the land data. The sea darkens with depth in 12 bands, from light blue over the continental shelves (0-200 m) to deep navy in the trenches (Natural Earth bathymetry at 200, 1,000, 2,000... 10,000 m). A faint line marks the equator. The route screen's gray map keeps flat gray land and sea. - Dredged passages: the Strait of Messina, the Bosporus, the Dardanelles and the St. No ship anchors in the Strait of Messina. Canals and their cuts through the quays are a little wider. - Ports moved on 2026-10-10: Tanger Med south and east into open water (35.72 N, 5.30 W, out north-east) so the strait is open; Gibraltar added in line above it (36.21 N, 5.30 W, out south-west; mediterranean market), a lane's width between their jetties; Eilat and Aqaba are one port, Eilat & Aqaba; Koper turned to face west about its old entrance (its centre moved east to 45.711 N 13.776 E); Port Tewfik faces south down the Gulf of Suez with its entrance on the water (29.92 N). A town's blocks never stand on a neighbouring port's quay, basin or harbour. - The land is covered with drawings at their size in the world, growing and shrinking with the map like the land: some 130,000 of them. Mountains along the real mountain ranges, 0.42 degrees apart so their peaks overlap into ridges, in three shapes, each with a lit face and a shaded one, ridges and scree (74-92 km across), snow-capped in cold places and all along the great snowy ranges (the Himalayas, Andes, Alps, Rockies, Karakoram, Caucasus, Southern Alps...). On the sea beyond 60 degrees north and south, waves mark the rough polar waters (the Southern Ocean wears ships twice as fast; see Rough seas). And a pizza on Albuquerque, a close-up secret (7 km). A drawing shows once it's 5 px across, and all of them from 16 px (in between only some, by a random tier, so the land thins out zoomed out rather than turning to noise: forests read as texture from mid zoom and as stands of trees close in), standing on its spot, lower ones in front, imported at 4x so they stay sharp close in. Each is drawn only while it's at least half its size from the nearest shore, so no land drawing reaches into the water. Not in locked regions; the gray route map has none. The animals live on the map as the wildlife (see Wildlife). The trees change with the seasons: by the calendar, for each drawing's hemisphere (the south's six months on), spring (March to May) brings the cherry trees' blossom, autumn (September to November) turns the round trees, oaks, birches and cherries orange, yellow and red, and winter (December to February) leaves them bare and grey, each drawing changing over at its own moment in the season's first 24 days; beyond the snow line the land lies white and the trees carry snow (the conifers, and the bare trees' snow versions). Each kind's seasonal versions are their own art, loaded after the kinds. - Ocean, sea and strait names have a thin dark edge so they read over shallow and deep water alike. Each shows from Natural Earth's own zoom, sits well inside its area (a river on the middle of its longest named stretch), and never overlaps another label: ports first, then countries, then oceans, seas, rivers, ranges, deserts, straits and lakes (so a river shows across a mountain range). Rivers are named in full (\"Mississippi River\"), unless the name already says river (\"Rio Grande\", \"Syr Darya\"). If its best spot is taken, a label (except a river's) tries a few spots just around it. Ten ocean trenches are labeled too: the Mariana, Tonga, Kermadec, Philippine, Kuril-Kamchatka, Japan, Aleutian, Peru-Chile, Puerto Rico and Java trenches, in pale teal slanted letters with the sea names' dark edge, placed after the straits. The route screen's gray map has none. - Ports are white circles with names; when names would overlap, the HQ's or a hub's name wins, then the bigger port's, and the other shows on zoom. The HQ and hubs are drawn on top of other ports (circle and company-color ring), so a big neighbouring port never covers them. - Every port has a size from 1 to 100, in four bands: Small 1–25, Medium 26–50, Large 51–75, Giant 76–100. Each save keeps its own sizes. - Starting sizes: within each market region (East Asia, the Mediterranean, the Great Lakes, Antarctica...), ports are banded by real container volume (the top ~15% Giant, the next ~25% Large, the next ~30% Medium, the rest Small; at least one of each per region: 34 Giant, 58 Large, 69 Medium, 66 Small) and spread by rank within the band (Shanghai 100, Hong Kong 63, Rome 29, Taipei 11). - Growth: the cargo value your ships unload (sales) and load (purchases) at a port grows it by value ÷ ($720,000 × its size) points, so bigger ports need more. Tuned so a mid-game company's busiest port (about $60M an hour through a size-50 port) climbs a band in about 15 hours. Growth is banked (up to 5 points) and applied at no more than 5 points an hour (a band in 5 hours), so late-game fleets grow their busy ports at that cap. - Shrinking: from company level 46, every port above 51 loses 0.83 points an hour (a band in about 30 hours), less whatever it grows, and never goes below 51 (a Large or Giant port stays at least Large). Small and Medium ports never shrink, and nothing shrinks before level 46. - Effects (Large ports play exactly as before sizes; the effect scales smoothly outside 51–75): your trades move prices up to 2x as much at size 1 and half as much at 100, and their effect takes that much longer or shorter to fade; fuel and repairs cost up to 20% more at size 1 and 20% less at 100 (on top of hub discounts). - Ship limits: tier-5 ships (Dominator, Capesize, Post-Panamax Bulker, Ocean Shearer, Supertanker, Giant Car Carrier, Float-On Semi-Sub, Open-Hatch Giant) dock only at Large and Giant ports; tier-4 ships (Trans Atlantic, Panamax Bulker, Kamsarmax, Livestock Carrier, Aframax, Car Carrier, Heavy-Lift Carrier, Project Carrier) at any port but Small; the rest and recovery boats anywhere. Both also dock at any port with an HQ or hub (any company's), whose piers are all long enough for the biggest ships, so a hub at a small port can take a Dominator (and grows fast on its traffic). The route screen greys out ports that are too small, lists each stop with its size (\"1. Taipei (Medium 26)\"), says what the ship needs (\"Docks only at Medium ports or bigger, or at an HQ or hub\") and why a click was refused (\"Muara (Small 9) is too small for a Panamax Bulker, which needs a Medium port or an HQ or hub\"). New ships launch at any HQ or hub (but not a tier-4 or 5 ship at a Great Lakes one). A ship whose next port is too small is held (\"assign a new route\"), though with the size floor this can't happen in normal play. - On the map a port's dot grows smoothly with its size: 3 px radius at 1, 6 at 63 (as before), 9 at 100; docked ships ring just outside it and hub rings hug it. - The port popup's title shows it (\"Taipei · Medium 26\"), then \"Size 26.1 of 100, growing (+5.0 an hour) · next: Large at 51\" with a bar through the band, its effects (\"Fuel & repairs +10% · your trades move prices 1.50x\") and \"Too small for ships needing Large ports\" (or \"Every ship can dock here\"). - The activity log says when a port grows or shrinks into a new band (\"Taipei grew to Medium\"); a shrink is only announced a point below the band, so a port on a band's edge doesn't flip back and forth. - Lanes use the Suez and Panama canals and the main straits and river approaches (Gibraltar, Messina, Bonifacio, the Dardanelles, Singapore, the Great Belt, the Elbe to Hamburg, the Scheldt to Antwerp, the Golden Gate, Puget Sound, the Bosphorus to the Black Sea, the Mississippi to New Orleans, Icy Strait to Juneau, the Oslofjord, the Strait of Magellan, and others). All distances are real nautical miles along those lanes. Routes cross the Pacific without a seam. Every port's lanes come in to it the same way, along its one approach from its sea gate to its harbour gate (see \"How lanes are found\"), so a ship always leaves and arrives the same way whoever it's bound for, and the way between two ports is the shortest safe way from the end of one approach to the start of the other, not to the marina itself. - Nhava Sheva and Kochi a little west; Mundra mirrored to face west by south-west into the Gulf of Kutch; Yangon (Thilawa) down to the Gulf of Martaban, out south; Sihanoukville out south-west; Muara out north; Laem Chabang a tad east; Surabaya south and east along the Madura strait; Jakarta north and west; Xiamen south and east; Manila out at the mouth of Manila Bay; Oakland down the bay clear of the Golden Gate Bridge, out north-north-west; and Guangzhou, Hong Kong and Shenzhen side by side facing south by south-east, Hong Kong in the middle and furthest out, like a podium (Guangzhou out in the estuary south-east of Macau, so its lanes clear Macau and Lantau)."},{"p":"The world","t":"Panama Canal","u":"panama-canal.html","x":"- Every lane through it follows that centerline exactly. - Three sets of locks, five chambers: Gatún (3, lifting ships from the Atlantic to Gatún Lake), Pedro Miguel (1) and Miraflores (1, lowering them to the Pacific), all out in the middle of the isthmus, clear of Colon's and Balboa's marinas (which are drawn many times real size). Chambers are drawn about 1.5 nm apart, several times real size, so they're visible at full zoom (like the ships). - Each chamber has two lanes side by side, one per direction, and holds one ship per lane. A ship sails into a chamber and sits for 3 s while the water rises or falls, then sails on. If the next chamber is close (the rest of the same flight, or Pedro Miguel to Miraflores), it stays in its chamber until that one is free. Northbound and southbound ships never block each other. - A ship coming up to a busy chamber waits in line behind it, 1.6 nm behind the ship ahead, first come first served (recovery boats too). No cap on the line. - Engines are off in a chamber, in line and at the toll: no fuel burned, no wear. Nothing breaks down anywhere between the canal's entrance and exit. Fuel checks before departure only count sailing time, so they're unchanged. - Toll: a cargo ship entering the canal pays 20% of the leg's base pay (before any hub Pay bonus). It's logged, counted in Finances (\"Canal tolls\", per ship and overall), and taken off the profit reported when the ship leaves the port at the end of that leg. A ship without the money waits at the entrance (\"Waiting for toll money\", a red dot on the Ships tab) and goes once it can pay. Recovery boats (and ships they carry) pay nothing. - XP bonus: a delivery from a leg through the canal earns +50% XP for the company, the ship and the hub where it's unloaded, added to the other bonuses (a hub's +30% company XP becomes +80%). - On the map the canal is always drawn as a wide water channel across the isthmus (as are all the canals), wider as you zoom in. From 120 px per degree its chambers appear: two lanes each, with gates at the ends, the water darkening or lightening as a ship inside is lowered or lifted. Ships in the canal keep to the right-hand lane. \"Panama Canal\" is labeled at mid zoom, and each lock's name when zoomed in close. - Clicking a lock (once its chambers are drawn) opens a popup: for each direction, each chamber's ship (\"Ever Bright, rising (2 s)\", \"coming in\", \"waiting to move on\" or \"empty\") and who's waiting in line. - Ships already partway through it carry on without paying a toll. - A leg can pass several canals (Duluth to Rotterdam goes through the Soo Locks, the Welland Canal and the St. Lawrence Seaway). Each charges its own toll as the ship enters it, and each adds its XP bonus to the delivery."},{"p":"The world","t":"Suez Canal","u":"suez-canal.html","x":"- Convoys: ships reaching the canal drop anchor in a cluster at the Port Said anchorage (southbound) or the Suez Bay anchorage (northbound), engines off. Every 90 s a convoy leaves each way at once; a ship arriving within 10 s after its convoy left still joins it. The toll (25% of the leg's base pay) is paid as the convoy leaves; a ship without the money stays at anchor for the next one (\"waiting for toll money\", red dot on the Ships tab). Recovery boats join convoys but pay nothing. - Convoy ships enter one after another, fastest first, and sail at half their own speed. In the single-lane stretches they keep 1.6 nm behind the ship ahead (no overtaking); in the doubled stretches they can pass. A ship won't enter a single-lane stretch while a ship the other way is in it, and waits in the doubled stretch before it. The two convoys normally meet in the long doubled middle. A ship held up behind a slower one burns fuel and wears only for the distance it makes, so the fuel check before leaving port (which counts the half-speed canal) always holds. - Supertankers are too deep for Suez fully loaded: they unload 25% of their cargo to pass (like the real SUMED pipeline), so the leg pays 25% less, and the toll is on the reduced pay. - Deliveries through Suez get +50% XP. No breakdowns between the anchorages and the exit. - On the map: the canal is drawn as one channel in single-lane stretches and two side by side in doubled ones (ships keep right); ships at anchor sit in rings round the anchorage. Zoomed in: \"Port Said anchorage · next convoy 45 s\" and \"Suez Bay anchorage ·...\", plus Great Bitter Lake, New Suez Canal and Ismailia. Clicking the canal or an anchorage opens a popup: time to the next convoys, and each way the ships at anchor, those waiting for toll money, those entering, and the convoy under way. - Ship status: \"At Port Said anchorage, next southbound convoy in 45 s\", \"Joining the southbound Suez Canal convoy (2nd in line to enter)\", \"In the southbound Suez Canal convoy (3rd of 7), held behind Big Blue\", \"Waiting in a passing stretch for the northbound convoy to clear\". - The route screen shows each Suez leg's toll, +50% XP, \"up to 1 min 30 s waiting for a convoy\", and a Supertanker's \"unloads 25% to pass\"."},{"p":"The world","t":"Great Lakes","u":"great-lakes.html","x":"- The five Great Lakes (and Lake St. Clair) are water, reached up the St. Lawrence from its estuary, with the Welland Canal (Ontario to Erie), the Detroit and St. Clair rivers (Erie to Huron), the Straits of Mackinac (Huron to Michigan) and the St. Marys River (Huron to Superior). - Only ships that fit the St. Lawrence Seaway (about 226 m long) can sail to a Great Lakes port: the Scooter 20, Feeder 100, Feedermax 200, Laker, Grain Laker, Cattle Coaster, Livestock Carrier, Coastal, Car Hopper and the three smaller boat carriers (Boat Barge, Deck Coaster, Yacht Transporter) and breakbulk carriers (Breakbulk Coaster, Multipurpose, Tweendecker), and both recovery boats (the Buffalo and the Mammoth) so lake ships can be rescued. Bigger ships can't be given routes there or be bought at a lake hub. Recovery boats of either model can be based at a lake hub and recover ships lost on lake routes (a Buffalo only the models it carries). - Three lock systems, 3 s per lock step as at Panama: - St. Lawrence Seaway (Montreal to Lake Ontario): 7 single-chamber locks (St. Lambert, Côte-Sainte-Catherine, Lower and Upper Beauharnois, Snell, Eisenhower, Iroquois). Toll 10% of the leg's base pay, +25% XP. - Welland Canal (Lake Ontario to Lake Erie): single-chamber Locks 1–3, 7 and 8, and the twinned Flight Locks 4–6 (a lane each way). Toll 10%, +25% XP. - Soo Locks (Lake Huron to Lake Superior): one step with 3 parallel chambers, each taking a ship either way. No toll, +25% XP. - Single (shared) chambers take one ship at a time either way. A chamber is left at the level for the other way, so a ship the same way waits 3 s while it's turned around; with ships waiting both ways, a lock alternates direction. Otherwise the Panama rules apply: lines, engines off, no breakdowns in the lock systems, toll on entering (or wait at the entrance). - Legs between two ports on the upper lakes (Superior, Michigan, Huron) pay +20% (the ore and grain trade). - Container prices differ around the lakes, so a Scooter 20 can pay its way from the big lake ports: Chicago (rail-hub imports: cheap toys and clothing, expensive furniture and coffee) against Milwaukee (the reverse), about $1,700-2,000 a trip each way; and Lake Erie (Cleveland, Detroit, Toledo: toys and furniture a little cheaper, clothing and coffee a little more expensive) against Lake Ontario (Toronto, Hamilton: toys and furniture 17% more expensive, clothing and coffee 11% cheaper), about $2,200-2,800 Cleveland to Toronto and $3,700-4,100 back through the Welland Canal. Lake Ontario has the biggest differences because every way into it pays a 10% toll (Welland or St. Lawrence), so no outside port can cash in on them."},{"p":"The world","t":"The Arctic","u":"the-arctic.html","x":"- 16 Arctic ports: Murmansk (ice-free, the Atomflot base), Arkhangelsk (White Sea), Sabetta (Yamal LNG), Dudinka (Norilsk's port, up the Yenisei: carved as a channel), Tiksi and Pevek on the Northern Sea Route; Churchill (Hudson Bay grain), Iqaluit, Resolute (Northwest Passage) and Prudhoe Bay (Alaska's North Slope oil); Longyearbyen (Svalbard coal), Sisimiut and Ilulissat (west Greenland); Tromso, Hammerfest (LNG) and Kirkenes (iron ore) in northern Norway. Murmansk, Arkhangelsk and the Norwegian ports are in the Northern Europe market. - Routing runs to 80.5 N, but only lanes to or from an Arctic port may go north of 66 N; between two other ports the Arctic counts as land, so every lane that existed before is unchanged. So the Arctic routes are sailed through Arctic ports: Rotterdam to Pevek goes along the Northern Sea Route, and a summer Europe-Asia run is e.g. Rotterdam - Pevek - Yokohama. - Seasonal closures (in saves with them), on real dates: the Northern Sea Route (a stretch of sea from the Kara Sea to the Chukchi Sea: every lane through it) open 1 Jul to 15 Nov; the Northwest Passage (the Canadian archipelago) open 15 Aug to 5 Oct; and the ports Churchill (open 11 Jul to 14 Nov), Iqaluit (1 Jul to 31 Oct), Prudhoe Bay (1 Aug to 30 Sep), Ilulissat (16 May to 14 Jan) and Arkhangelsk (16 May to 30 Nov). Murmansk, northern Norway, Longyearbyen and Sisimiut stay open all year (the Gulf Stream). A ship won't start a leg through a closed route it can't finish in time; the route screen lists each leg's closures, and the map shows a closed route iced over. - Icebreakers: a nuclear icebreaker stationed on the Northern Sea Route (Murmansk, Sabetta, Tiksi, Pevek) or the Northwest Passage (Resolute, Iqaluit, Prudhoe Bay) keeps it open all winter, leading convoys through the ice like the Sea of Okhotsk ones; only a nuclear one can (\"Only a nuclear icebreaker can keep the Northern Sea Route open through the Arctic winter.\"). A diesel one can keep Arkhangelsk open (the White Sea)."},{"p":"The world","t":"Wonders of the world","u":"wonders-of-the-world.html","x":"- 49 wonders of the world stand on the map at their real places: 21 man-made (the New 7 Wonders of the World: the Great Wall of China, Petra, Christ the Redeemer, Machu Picchu, Chichén Itzá, the Colosseum and the Taj Mahal; the Great Pyramid of Giza; and the Statue of Liberty, Eiffel Tower, Sydney Opera House, Stonehenge, Angkor Wat, the Moai of Easter Island, the Parthenon, Golden Gate Bridge, Burj Khalifa, Mount Rushmore, Sagrada Família, Hagia Sophia and St Basil's Cathedral), the 6 lost Wonders of the Ancient World drawn as they were, faded, where they stood (the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, the Temple of Artemis, the Statue of Zeus at Olympia, the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, the Colossus of Rhodes and the Lighthouse of Alexandria), and 22 natural (the Seven Natural Wonders: the Grand Canyon, Great Barrier Reef, Harbor of Rio de Janeiro, Mount Everest, the Northern Lights, Parícutin and Victoria Falls; the New 7 Wonders of Nature: the Amazon Rainforest, Ha Long Bay, Iguazu Falls, Jeju Island, Komodo, the Puerto Princesa Underground River and Table Mountain; and Niagara Falls, Mount Fuji, Kilimanjaro, Uluru, Angel Falls, Yellowstone, the Salar de Uyuni and the Giant's Causeway). - Drawn on their own layer over the wildlife and under the marinas, ports and ships, each its size in the world, growing and shrinking with the map like the land, shown once 5 px across. The man-made ones are just the building or monument (no ground or water round them; the lost ones faded), and the natural ones drawn as they are. Each picture stands on its spot, moved just far enough that it never covers a port's marina (at its size now, a hub's bigger), even zoomed right in: straight away from the port, or the nearest way round that's still on land (the Colosseum beside Rome, Christ the Redeemer inland of Rio). Four follow their real course: the Great Wall of China as a strip of grey brick wall from Jiayuguan to Shanhaiguan (7 km wide), its battlements along the north side and a watchtower every 40 km, its brick courses and walkway once it's wide enough; the Grand Canyon as a layered red gorge along the Colorado from Lees Ferry to the Grand Wash Cliffs with the river blue at its bottom; the Great Barrier Reef as reefs every 45 km strung along Queensland out to sea, each a pale lagoon in a ring of coral; and the Northern Lights as curtains of green (purple at their tops) rising 260 km over Lapland, bright at night and faint by day. Names show under them (or over them, clear of the ports' names) once they're 42 px across (the ones along a course once 120 km is). The Golden Gate Bridge and Ha Long Bay are drawn in more detail than the rest, with no background of their own over the map's water (it read as a blue square at night; the other wonders that stood on blue lost theirs too): the bridge a little bigger than life (36 km), its orange towers and portal struts, cables and suspenders, the grey roadway with lanes and cars, the anchorages and the shadows on the water; the bay's limestone islands with sheer grey cliffs, forest on top and pale beaches, and junks under red battened sails among them. At night they light up. Ha Long Bay's picture stands out in the bay itself, east of Hai Phong's marina. - Click one for its popup: its picture and name, what kind of wonder it is and where (\"Man-made wonder · Egypt\", \"Ancient wonder, now lost · Greece\"), when it was built, formed or is best seen, and a fact. Clicking one checks it off in the field guide's new Wonders tab (man-made, lost ancient and natural, each with its picture, country, when and fact; the field guide's header says \"0 of 121 animals · 1 of 49 wonders seen\"), and the achievements have a line for it: Wonders of the world, wonders seen, stars at 10, 25 and all 49."},{"p":"","t":"Sea and sky","u":"sea-and-sky.html","x":""},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Weather","u":"weather.html","x":"- Storms, in saves founded with Moderate or Harsh storms. They form in their real basins and seasons by the calendar, each with a name used in turn: Atlantic and East Pacific hurricanes (Jun-Nov, peak Aug-Oct), West Pacific typhoons (all year, peak Jul-Oct), North Indian cyclones (Bay of Bengal and Arabian Sea, around the monsoon), South Indian, Australian and South Pacific cyclones (Nov-Apr), and winter gales in the North Atlantic and North Pacific (Oct-Mar) and the Southern Ocean (all year, worst in the austral winter). About 120 a year at Moderate (Harsh: half as many again), up to 8 or so at once. - Tropical storms drift west and poleward at about 10-12 knots, then curve away east (and speed up) past their recurve latitude; gales run east at about 25 knots. Each builds up over the first half of its life (3-12 calendar days, a day being 72 s of play) and fades over the rest; its strength at a point is that times how near its core it is (full within 35% of its radius, about 200-500 nm, fading to nothing at the edge). - From company level 6 (with breakdowns), a ship in a storm sails slower (by up to 40% at the core at Moderate, 60% at Harsh) and wears faster (up to 2x / 3x), and its breakdown chance rises the same way. Fuel per mile stays the same (the engines just make less way), so a storm alone never strands a ship that set off with enough fuel. A hurricane, typhoon or cyclone strong enough over a port (85% of full strength at Moderate, 70% at Harsh) closes it: ships there wait in port, and ships bound for it wait 25 nm off it with engines off, until it passes (\"Waiting off Charleston for Hurricane Ana to pass\"). These waits aren't holds: no red dot, nothing for the player to do, and no random breakdowns while waiting (nor in an icebreaker's convoy). Recovery boats go in regardless. - Warnings in the activity log, for the HQ, hubs and every port a ship is at, bound for or has on its route: a tropical storm heading for one (about 2 calendar days ahead), one closing and reopening, and each ship sailing into a storm. - Wind: everywhere, all the time, following the real prevailing winds by latitude: the north-east (south-east) trade winds in the tropics, the westerlies of the middle latitudes (strongest in the Southern Ocean's roaring forties), the polar easterlies, and near calm in the doldrums and horse latitudes, wandering in direction (up to about 45 degrees) and strength with slow gusty variation over thousands of miles and minutes of play, and wound round every storm (anticlockwise in the north, clockwise in the south), strongest at its core. The same for every player. On the World map it shows as gusts: short pale streaks blowing its way, drifting and fading, none in a calm, more, longer and brighter the stronger it blows. Ships at anchor face into it. - Clouds: drifting cloud over the globe, thicker along the equator and the middle latitudes' storm tracks and thinner over the subtropics, clearing as the map zooms in (from 7 to 24 px a degree) so ships show. - Night and day: the side of the globe turned from the sun is dark, with dusk and dawn a soft band between (a terminator, so one side of the world is in its night while the other has its day). The sun stands over the sub-solar point of the calendar's moment, tilted with the seasons (23.44 degrees); the calendar runs on Greenwich time, so the sun is over Greenwich at 12:00 GMT, the rest of the world an hour on for every 15 degrees east (the birds' nights and the deck crews' watches go by each place's own hour); the top bar shows London time, GMT in winter and BST (an hour ahead) from the last Sunday in March to the last Sunday in October, marked GMT or BST. Two darkenings: 90% of the map art, land drawings, marinas and weather is lost at the darkest, and 60% more of the lanes, ports, labels, birds and ships over them (so the names still read), and over it all the lights: ships' navigation lights (red to port, green to starboard, white at the masthead and stern, each with a halo) and the warm glow of their decks with a few deck lamps, each marina's entrance lights (red and green, haloed), lamps along its quay (a warm glow over the HQ and the industry), a lamp every 5 dock units down each pier (white at its tip), along the basin's walls and round the jetty's arms, its HQ lit (a glow in its company's colour and warm light, its windows lit, more and cooler the bigger it has grown, a few dark; red aircraft lights on its corners from level 6) and each industry building under an orange sodium floodlight with a lamp at each corner of its yard and a few lit windows (all drawn once into the marina's lights item, again when the HQ or industry changes), its town's windows lit over a glow across the town, and its lighthouse sweeping two opposite beams round once every 6 s of play, and, zoomed out past the marinas, the ports' towns as warm glows (7 to 18 px by size band). The ships' and the marinas' lights (lighthouse, entrance, quay) come on under a storm's cloud by day as well, as dark as the cloud is thick; the towns' glow is night's only. Birds already sleep by their port's night. Nothing else changes with the dark. - Ships at anchor in a storm (waiting for a berth, idle, tugs, recovery boats and other service ships) swing and pitch about their anchors, more the stronger the storm there; one over 50% of its strength at the port batters each ship there once, taking 20% of its maintenance (\"Sea Otter was battered at anchor off Rome by Storm Ciaran: maintenance down to 64%\"). - On the World map each storm is part of the sky's cloud, thick and dark (thinned a little as the map zooms in, so the ships under it show): a hurricane, typhoon or cyclone a dense core wound round with dark spiral bands (turning anticlockwise in the north and clockwise in the south, stopping when paused) and a clear eye once strong; a gale a ragged mass of dark rain cloud, longer east-west, with no spiral; lightning flickering in hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones (jagged forked bolts in the eyewall and bands, more often the stronger the storm; gales have none), and its name (\"Hurricane Ana\", \"Typhoon Yagi\", \"Storm Ciaran\") under it when big enough on screen. Before level 6 storms are drawn but harmless. Storms are saved with the game. - Each storm's track shows as a thick grey dashed line (30% opaque): where it has been since it formed (a point every 10 s of play, saved with it; storms from older saves start theirs where they were) and where it will go until it blows out (storms keep a fixed course, so this is exact). Hovering a storm (within half its radius) shows a pointer, makes its track stronger and rings its edge and core with dashed circles; clicking it opens its popup, which follows it and closes when it blows out: Strength (at the center, the core's edge, halfway out and the edge, building up or fading), what it does to ships at its center, whether it closes ports (tropical storms), its Cloud (rings, spiral bands, eye), Size (radius and across), Course (heading and speed), the ships In it now (yours named), Time (how long it has been going and has left, in calendar and play time), where it Formed, and where its basin's storms spawn and when. In multiplayer, joined games keep their own copy of each track (updates leave tracks out). - The Tutorial's Storms tip explains them the first time they act on ships."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Rough seas","u":"rough-seas.html","x":"- South of 60°S, ships wear twice as fast. The fuel check before leaving port and route ranges allow for it, so some long Antarctic legs within a ship's nominal range are refused (\"too far for one tank\"). - Legs to or from a port south of 60°S (the Antarctic stations) pay +50%, and so earn +50% XP for the ship, the company and the hub where they're unloaded. - The route screen marks those legs \"rough seas: +50% pay and XP, 2x wear\"."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Seasonal closures","u":"seasonal-closures.html","x":"- In saves founded with Seasonal closures, from company level 6 (with breakdowns and storms, so a new company, which always starts on 1 January, isn't iced in from its first minute). Real dates, every year: - St. Lawrence Seaway and Welland Canal locks: shut 31 Dec to 22 Mar. Soo Locks: 15 Jan to 25 Mar. - The Great Lakes ports: iced in 15 Jan to 20 Feb, but not in the game's first year (2000). The ice (on the map, and the icebreakers' area) covers the lakes themselves, a circle each (Superior, Michigan, Huron, Georgian Bay, Erie, Ontario), so it never reaches the Atlantic coast. - The Antarctic stations: shut by sea ice 1 Apr to 31 Oct (reachable only in the austral summer). - Nain (Labrador): 1 Dec to 30 Jun. Magadan and Vanino (Sea of Okhotsk): 1 Jan to 15 Apr. - Gulf of Finland ice (within 180 nm of the gulf: St Petersburg, Ust-Luga, Helsinki, Tallinn): 10 Jan to 31 Mar. It stays open, but ships crawl through at 45% speed and 1.5x wear (fuel per mile the same). - A ship won't start a leg it can't finish before a closure: its destination shut when it would arrive, or a canal on the way shut while it's sailing (checked about daily). It waits in port (\"Waiting for the Welland Canal to reopen on 22 Mar (its winter shutdown)\"), not a hold: no red dot. Ships already at sea finish their leg; ships can always leave a port that's closing. - Warnings in the activity log for closures touching the player's ports (HQ, hubs, where ships are or are bound, their routes) or canals their ships use: 10 calendar days ahead (\"The Welland Canal closes for its winter shutdown on 31 Dec: ships won't start legs they can't finish before then.\"), then when it shuts and reopens, and when the Gulf of Finland ice sets in and clears. - On the World map, while a closure is in force: closed ports have an icy ring, shut canals are frosted over along their path, and the frozen sea is a solid white ice sheet with a soft edge and faint cracks. That covers each closure's ice (the Great Lakes, Hudson Bay, the Labrador and Beaufort seas, the Okhotsk and White seas, the Arctic routes, the Antarctic coast) and every ice zone in season, such as the Baltic's. The sheet lies on sea and lakes, never over land. An icebreaker under way breaks a dark open-water channel through it, edged with broken ice, which freezes over again in about a minute of play. The Northern Sea Route and the Northwest Passage are labeled on the map from mid zoom. A port's popup lists its closure (\"Closes for ice from 15 Jan to 25 Mar\", or \"Closed by ice until 25 Mar\"), and the route screen notes each leg's closures. The Tutorial's Seasonal closures tip explains them once they apply. - Icebreakers (see Icebreakers) lead ships through the Great Lakes and Sea of Okhotsk ice and keep the Gulf of Finland's fairways open."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Ocean currents","u":"ocean-currents.html","x":"- 28 of the world's major surface currents: the Florida Current, Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift; the Canary, North and South Equatorial, Caribbean, North Brazil, Brazil and Benguela currents; the Agulhas, the Indian Ocean's South Equatorial, the Somali and Monsoon currents (which run the other way in the northern winter, with the monsoon), the Leeuwin and East Australian currents; the Kuroshio, North Pacific, California, Alaska, Oyashio, the Pacific's equatorial currents and its Equatorial Counter Current, the Humboldt; the Antarctic Circumpolar, East Greenland and Labrador currents. The month is the calendar's, whatever the seasonal closures setting. - Ships feel them: a lane's speed zones are split where the current's push along the lane changes, each sped up or slowed by (15 kn + the current along the way) / 15 kn, at most 35% either way. Fuel burns by the second, so a ship with the current also burns less fuel a leg, and one against it more; the fuel a ship needs for a leg counts the currents of the month it sets off in, so it never runs dry for them. - It's kept if it saves at least 2.5% of the leg's time in some months and is no more than 1.2 times as long as the stretch it leaves out; the months it's quicker in go with it. A ship setting off on a leg takes the second way round in those months, and keeps to the way it chose (and the currents of the month it chose it in) to the end of the leg, saved with it, the host's way sent to the other players. So ships from the US to Europe ride the Gulf Stream, and with the Somali Current turning with the monsoon, the way along the Horn of Africa changes over the year. Lanes through a canal, and lanes to or from the Arctic, have no second way. Its status says \"Sailing to Rotterdam on the Gulf Stream\". - On the map the currents flow: dashes drifting along three lines across each current's width the way the water runs (back the other way in the months it turns), warm currents red-orange and cold ones blue, brighter the stronger it runs this month; only the middle line far out; fading in from a mid zoom and out again close in."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Sea animals","u":"sea-animals.html","x":"- Just for looking at (they never affect ships): 28 pods of 8 species to start with (see below; 112 pods of 54 species now, with the fish, sharks, seals, sea lions and otters): bottlenose and spinner dolphins, orcas, and humpback, blue, gray, sperm and southern right whales, 2 to 5 pods each, each pod its real size (bottlenose 8-15, spinners 80-200, orcas 4-15, sperm whale families 10-14 and a bachelor group of 3, the great whales 1-4). - Each pod spends parts of the in-game year in its areas, wandering from spot to spot in each (a leg every 2 calendar days, easing out from the middle over its first 4 days there and back over its last), and migrates between them in the gaps along a sea path round the land. Real routes, for example: humpbacks from the Gulf of Alaska to the Mexican Pacific, from California to Costa Rica and Panama, from Southeast Alaska to Hawaii, from the Antarctic Peninsula to Colombia and from the Antarctic to Queensland; gray whales from the Bering Sea down the Pacific coast to the Baja lagoons; a small pod of 4 transient orcas down the Pacific coast from British Columbia to Monterey and back; spinner dolphins round Hawaii. Where a pod is follows from the calendar alone, so every player sees the same and nothing is saved or sent. - On the World map (from 10 px a degree in), each pod is a loose group, sized in the world like the ships (small: a humpback about 5 px long at the opening zoom, growing as you zoom in) swimming the way it's heading, at most 4-15 animals drawn (by species; a big dolphin pod shows 12 as a crowd). Under water each is a pale blue-grey shape (its outline, as seen through the sea), drawn only where all of it is over open water (and a leap or breach only where all of that is too); now and then a whale or orca comes up to breathe, shown in full color with a puff of spray, or breaches, rising bigger and crashing back in a splash; dolphins leap in arcs ahead of their shapes and splash down. They swim under the ice and storms. - Hovering a pod shows a pointer and its year of routes and areas dashed; clicking it opens its popup (following it): the species, how many in the pod (and how many the map shows), what it's doing now (in an area until about when, or migrating from where to where, how far and arriving about when), its year (each area, from early/mid/late one month to another), how long they grow and a fact. - More species: Fin whales (the Ligurian Sea in summer and off Lampedusa in late winter; the Gulf of St. Lawrence and the Scotian Shelf), beluga whales (the St. Lawrence estuary all year, and 30 off the Churchill River in Hudson Bay in July and August, wintering in Hudson Strait), narwhals (Lancaster Sound in summer, Baffin Bay in winter; the males' spiral tusks show), and common (superpods of 150-200 in the Bay of Biscay and off Southern California, golden hourglass on each side), dusky (120 off Kaikoura, wintering in Admiralty Bay) and Atlantic spotted dolphins (the Bahama Banks): 37 pods of 14 species of whales and dolphins. - Fish, sharks, seals, sea lions and otters (the same data, 40 more species in 75 more groups, drawn and behaving by their kind): 25 game fish in schools, the map showing up to 5-30 of each (salmon: Chinook, sockeye, coho, pink, chum and Atlantic, running in to the Columbia, Bristol Bay, the Strait of Georgia, Cook Inlet, Kamchatka and the Gulf of St. Lawrence in season and wintering at sea; tuna: Atlantic bluefin between the Gulf of Maine and the Gulf of Mexico and between the Balearics and Biscay, yellowfin, albacore and skipjack; billfish: blue, black and striped marlin, sailfish and swordfish off Kona, Bermuda, Cairns, Pinas Bay, Cabo, the Bay of Islands, Isla Mujeres, Costa Rica and the Grand Banks; and mahi-mahi, wahoo, tarpon, giant trevally, great barracuda, yellowtail kingfish, king mackerel, striped bass, Pacific halibut and Atlantic cod), leaping clear in arcs like the dolphins (not the halibut and cod); 6 sharks (great whites between Guadalupe or the Farallones and the White Shark Cafe and off Hermanus, tiger, scalloped hammerheads schooling at Cocos and the Galapagos, shortfin mako, bull and whale sharks), alone or in twos and threes, showing their fin at the surface now and then, makos and great whites breaching; 4 seals (harbour, grey, northern elephant and leopard) and 3 sea lions (California, Steller and Australian) in colonies along the coasts, bobbing up (sea lions leaping along too); and sea otters (Monterey Bay, Prince William Sound, the Kurils) and marine otters (Chiloe), floating on their backs. A pod of fish is a school, of sharks sharks, of seals or sea lions a colony and of otters a raft in their popups; the field guide's Sea animals tab groups them (whales, dolphins and orcas; seals, sea lions and otters; sharks; game fish). 112 pods of 54 species in all."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Wildlife","u":"wildlife.html","x":"- Every land animal and every bird is an individual that lives on the map for good, just for looking at, except that the birds following a ship boost it (see Bird boosts under Birds). Worked out from the data and the clocks alone, so every player sees much the same and nothing is saved. Till then there's none. About 2,500 land animals in 456 groups and some 16,500 birds in 2,500 colonies. A group's home away from its species' spot is only taken where all the ground it roams is land, so no herd wanders into the sea (southern India's elephants did). - Each is drawn from above at its size in the world (exaggerated so it reads, like the ships: an elephant 13.5 km, a penguin 4.5 km), growing and shrinking with the map, so they show once you're zoomed in (from about 3 px), on the full-color map only, not in locked regions; turned the way it's heading, stepping as it walks (a second drawing with its legs swapped, or a bird's wings), dimmed asleep. - Camels, giraffes, zebras, kangaroos, moose, reindeer and llamas just roam and bed down where they are. When its species is up (by day; lions, tigers, koalas and kiwis by night; kangaroos and moose at dusk and dawn, by the sun there) a group roams its range round its home from spot to spot at its walking pace (kmh), grazing and resting between, the young close by their mother; the rest of the time it's at its home (or bedded down where it last was), going and coming back as the day turns. Brown bears sleep the winter (December to March) in their caves, and polar bear mothers with cubs keep to their snow dens December to February: the den shows its z's. Reindeer go 4 degrees south for November to April and zebras 1.9 degrees north (the Serengeti to the Mara) for July to October, the herd walking there over 10 days. - Birds live in colonies at the ports: each port has its gulls (8, 12, 16 or 20 by its size band, 4 more at a hub, shared between up to three of the species whose ranges cover it, or the nearest), and up to 2 species of raptors (and an owl), 2 of cormorants, 3 of waders and 1 of sea ducks whose ranges cover it. Each colony's home is on the shore either side of its port (the same spots the waders use): a gull or cormorant colony at the water's edge, an eyrie, heronry, nest in the reeds or nest on the ground inland. A colony whose species' range only covers its port some months spends the rest at the nearest port its range covers then (or in its range there), flying between the two in a V (all its birds, along the shortest way round the globe) over the first 10 days of each stay; at the port it's visiting it's one of that port's birds. Seabirds nest in colonies at some of the ports in their range (30% of them; their numbers three times their follow count) and forage out to sea round them, gliding from spot to spot within 60% of their range (albatrosses 2,500 km, shearwaters 1,800, terns 350, pelicans 150, condors 300), and follow ships that pass. Each bird lives as a permanent map object: see Birds. - Movement goes by the life clock: calendar time at the game's speed, but never slower than 1x, so the wildlife carries on while the game's paused; when things happen (day and night, the seasons) goes by the calendar. - Click an animal for its group's popup: the species with its picture, its group (\"A pride of 6 with a cub\", \"A mother with 2 cubs\", \"On its own\"), what they're doing now (\"On the move\", \"3 grazing, 2 resting\", \"Asleep at the cave\", \"Hibernating in the den\"), its home and when it's there (\"A cave, back by night; they sleep the winter away in it\"), where it lives, its habits and a fact. Click a home for its popup: \"Rookery (Penguins)\", \"Colony (Herring Gull)\": who lives there and what they're doing now (home for the night, most out and about by day, away at Lisbon for now, on the wing to Lisbon), for a bird colony when it's there through the year (\"Here April to September; the rest of the year at Lisbon\", or all year) and any eggs or chicks in the season (its hemisphere's spring and early summer), and a fact. A V on the wing or a seabird out at sea opens the birds popup (\"Arctic Tern on the wing: 12 flying to Cape Town\"). Each counts as seen for the field guide."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Birds","u":"birds.html","x":"- Just for looking at (they never affect ships). Every bird is a permanent map object: one of the wildlife's colonies' birds (see Wildlife), always somewhere, doing something, never appearing or vanishing. Its day goes in spells (each class its own length: gulls 18-40 s, raptors 30-70, cormorants 25-55, waders 40-90, sea ducks 45-100, seabirds 50-130 s, each bird its own phase); at the start of each it picks what to do next by its class and the hour, then flies there from wherever it was (its last spell's end, worked out again if no one was looking), smoothly and along a gentle curve, at its class's pace (faster if it must, to arrive within 80% of the spell), and does it. Its position is worked out from the bird clock alone (real seconds, so they carry on while the game's paused, at their own pace at any game speed), so it's the same whoever looks, and nothing is saved. By night (before 5:30 and after 20:00 by the sun; night herons the other way round) birds sleep at their colony's home; sea ducks and seabirds out at sea sleep on the water. - Gulls: 15 species, each in its real range (Herring, Great Black-backed, Lesser Black-backed, Yellow-legged, Black-headed, Ring-billed, Laughing, California, Western, Glaucous-winged, Black-tailed, Heermann's, Sooty, Kelp and Silver gulls), some moving between summer and winter ranges. Every port has its gull colony (see Wildlife). By day each gull circles the port, perches on the port's rocks (each bird its own spot, the rocks' filled first, then the breakwater's arms), swims off it, follows a ship within 60 km of the port (flying behind and off to one side of it, wherever it goes, till the spell ends), or rests at the colony; by night most sleep at the colony and a few sit on the sea. - Seabirds: 16 species (Andean and California condors; Wandering, Black-browed and Laysan albatrosses; Arctic, Common, Caspian and Royal terns; Brown, Great White and Australian pelicans; Sooty and Manx shearwaters; Magnificent and Great frigatebirds, with their long angular wings, forked tails and scarlet throats), each with seasonal ranges they migrate between (Arctic Terns from pole to pole, Sooty Shearwaters round the Pacific). Each wanders out over the sea from its colony a stretch at a time (within 60% of its range, then up to 120 km about that), gliding in wide circles and sitting on the sea (paddling about now and then; a frigatebird never settles on the water and soars on through the night instead), and takes up following a ship that passes within 250 km (steadily by species and ship), till its spell ends; by night it sleeps on the water where it is. - Raptors: 15 eagles, hawks and the osprey, drawn with broad, finger-tipped wings (an osprey's bent at the wrist), a fanned tail and a hooked bill: Bald, White-tailed, Steller's Sea, African Fish, White-bellied Sea, Golden and Wedge-tailed eagles; the Osprey; Red-tailed, Rough-legged, Swainson's, Harris's and Cooper's hawks, the Northern Goshawk and the Common Buzzard; each with its seasonal ranges (Swainson's hawks migrate from the Great Plains through Central America and Panama to Argentina; Steller's sea eagles winter on Hokkaido's drift ice; Rough-legged hawks breed on the Arctic tundra; Peregrine falcons on sea cliffs the world over, Gyrfalcons round the Arctic and Brown falcons across Australia, with swept pointed wings and long narrow tails). They never follow ships. By day each perches on the land behind its port, soars high in slow circles over the land, or hunts out over the sea (circling, now and then plunging in with a splash and climbing again); by night it sleeps at its eyrie. A bird's shadow falls further off the higher it flies. - Owls (raptors of kind Owl): 8 of them, drawn with a big round head (a pale facial disc, big eyes, ear tufts on the horned owls), broad rounded wings and a short round tail: Snowy (the Arctic, south in winter to North America's coasts, Scandinavia, Russia and Hokkaido), Barn, Short-eared, Great Horned, Eurasian Eagle-, Blakiston's Fish, Pel's Fishing and Powerful owls. They go about it as the other raptors do, but by night, and sleep on their perches by day. A port has up to one owl species besides its 2 other raptors. They have no calls yet. - Cormorants: 9 species (Great, Double-crested, Pelagic, Neotropic, Cape, Japanese, Little Pied and Guanay cormorants, and the European Shag), each where it really lives. Drawn with a long neck stretched out ahead, a small head (a pale cheek or bright throat on some) and a thin bill. A colony keeps together: perched on the rocks or the breakwater (now and then holding their wings out to dry), swimming low off it and diving for fish (a dark shape under a ripple while under), or circling wide round the port together; by night they sleep at their colony. - Herons, egrets, bitterns and cranes: 22 species (Grey, Great Blue, Purple, Goliath, Striated and Black-crowned Night herons; Great, Little, Snowy, Cattle and Reddish egrets; Eurasian, American, Least and Australasian bitterns; Common, Sandhill, Red-crowned, Whooping, Grey Crowned, Sarus cranes and the Brolga), each where and when it really is (Whooping Cranes winter only at Aransas on the Texas coast; Red-crowned Cranes on Hokkaido; Common and Sandhill cranes migrate). They live along their port's shore either side of its marina: the shore is found once per port from the land as drawn (the water's edge, and a spot inland), never on a marina's quay. Drawn standing from above with a long shadow cast to the side (legs, neck and head: they're tall), the body with folded wings, neck and dagger bill; in flight with broad fingered wings and legs trailing (a crane's neck stretched out, a heron's drawn back). By day each stands at the water's edge, neck poised, or stalks slowly along it, flying between spots along the shore; a bittern always stands hidden in a clump of reeds swaying in the wind, bill to the sky; by night they sleep hunched at the heronry. - Birds that fly in Vs in life do so on their migrations (the leader at the point, the rest back along either arm by turns, 4.5 km apart): each bird flies from wherever it was into its place over the first 25 s. - Sea ducks: 9 eiders and scoters (Common, King, Spectacled and Steller's eiders; Common, Black, Surf, White-winged and Velvet scoters), each with its real migration: breeding on Arctic and northern coasts and lakes in summer and wintering on northern coasts (the whole world's Spectacled Eiders in a few holes in the Bering Sea ice; Steller's Eiders in Norway's Varangerfjord; scoters off Britain, both North American coasts, and Japan). A raft lives off its port day and night, the ducks together on one of the sea spots off it, drawn swimming from above, drakes in their pattern (eiders white-backed with black sides and green napes, King Eiders with blue-grey heads and orange bills, scoters black with bright bills, white face and wing patches) and hens brown, drifting and turning a little; by day each dives in its own time; now and then the whole raft flies round the port together and settles somewhere new. - Bird seasons: the data's months read in as floats, so a season's months are matched as numbers either way. - On the World map, each bird is drawn at its size in the world with the ships: a dot of its back's color while it's under 4 px across (from 1.6 px), then its drawing from above in its species' colors (mantle, head, wingtips, bill), flapping, gliding or folded, with its shadow on the water further off the higher it is, turned the way it's heading. Clicking one opens its colony's popup (remembering the bird clicked): the species with its picture, name, Latin name, how many and what they're doing now (\"5 circling the port, 4 perched, 3 resting at home\"), or where they're flying to on a migration. A port's or ship's birds list the same way. Each species has a Follow button at its right: the map follows one of its birds (the one clicked, if the popup was opened by clicking a bird; else one out and about if any is, one following the ship for a ship's birds), keeping it in the middle wherever it goes, zoomed in 3 times as close as when following a ship; the button reads Following while it does. Port popups say \"Gulls: Herring Gull (16), Yellow-legged Gull (8)\" (each species once, all its flocks there added up, the most first); ship popups say \"Following: Herring Gull (3), Wandering Albatross (2)\" while any are following it. - Field guide: the square paw-print button at the map's top right opens a guide to every bird (gulls, seabirds, raptors, cormorants, herons, egrets, bitterns and cranes, and sea ducks), sea animal (with the waters its pods visit) and land animal on the map (with its group size and habits), a tab each, each with its picture, where to see it and a fact. Esc, X or a click outside closes it. - Bird boosts: every species that follows ships gives the ship a little of its own, four shares: speed gained, wear saved, fuel saved, and storm shelter (the storm's slowing and wearing shrugged off in part). Gulls are small change (1-2% of one or two), terns are quick (+2-3% speed, a little fuel), pelicans save fuel and wear (2-4% each), condors keep a ship sound (3-4% less wear), frigatebirds and shearwaters shelter it (15-20%), and the albatrosses are the prize (the Wandering Albatross +4% speed, 2% fuel, 25% shelter). A species counts once it has a bird aboard, 15% more for each bird up to three more; all the species following add up, capped at +12% speed, 25% wear, 15% fuel and 60% shelter. Looked at again every 5 s of play; applied in the storm factors (speed, wear, shelter) and the ship's fuel burn; the ship's popup says what its followers give it. In a Divine Actions game the divine blessing comes on top."},{"p":"Sea and sky","t":"Sound","u":"sound.html","x":"- The World map has its ambience, every sound scaled by the zoom: near silent with the whole world in view, full volume close in, on a log scale bent so it stays quiet until the map is fairly close. Looping all the time: the sea's wash (softer over the land close in); a harbour's bustle whenever a marina is on screen (louder the bigger it is on screen); wind, a little always and strong under a storm, and rain under one. Now and then (every 4-9 s close in, longer further out, and only from a third of the zoom's range in) an animal in view calls: one is picked at random from the birds at the ports on screen (each species weighted by its numbers: gulls, terns, pelicans, cormorants, shearwaters, eiders, scoters, herons, egrets, bitterns, cranes, night herons, eagles, hawks, falcons, ospreys, condors), the pods in view (humpback whales, orcas, seals, sea otters) and the land's animals in view (bison, grizzly bears, elephants, giraffes, lemurs, lions, orangutans, tigers, penguins, kiwis, quetzals, toucans); a call starts at the beginning of its recording (seeking into one decodes its way there, tens of ms) and fades after 8 s; three can sound at once. - Birds call as their own species where a free recording of the species was found, the kind's recording otherwise; the 42 without one anywhere free (the other gulls, both frigatebirds, the albatrosses, the royal tern, two pelicans, the sooty shearwater, five cormorants, three eiders, four scoters, the Goliath heron, the reddish egret, the Australasian bittern, the red-crowned, whooping and grey crowned cranes and the brolga, the brown falcon, four eagles, the rough-legged hawk) call as their kind. Whenever a bird calls, the sound tracker at the top left of the map shows the very bird that's calling, drawn big in its mirror image as the map draws it, its species' name, and a speaker mark whose waves flicker while the call plays; it fades when the call ends. - Day and night (by how dark it is at the middle of the screen, as the map draws it; never with the night switched off): by night the birds call less (a third as often at full dark) and more quietly, all but the night birds (the species with behavior \"night\", such as the black-crowned night heron); by day the gulls call three times as often as their numbers would have it. Owls call by night (from 30% dark) where a port or the land is in view, chosen by where the middle of the screen is: the barn owl nearly everywhere, the great horned owl in the Americas, the barred and eastern screech owls in North America, the tawny, Eurasian scops and eagle owls in Europe and western Asia, the African scops owl south of the Sahara, the brown hawk-owl in South and East Asia, and the southern boobook in Australia and New Zealand. Owls aren't drawn, so the sound tracker doesn't show them. - Nothing in the game must drop that file."},{"p":"","t":"Rivals and multiplayer","u":"rivals.html","x":""},{"p":"Rivals and multiplayer","t":"AI rivals","u":"ai-rivals.html","x":"- Computer-run shipping companies, chosen when founding a game: New Company's \"AI rivals\" row (under Storms) has a drop-down for how many (None, the default, to 5) and Easy, Normal or Hard for them all (greyed with None; Normal to start with; each explained in its tooltip). They're ordinary companies in the game's list, saved with it like any other (the save keeps each one's difficulty), and carry on from where they were when it's loaded. - Each gets a name no company has, a company type at random (repeats allowed; another at random each time its level allows a pick, \"Northstar Freight branched out into Tankers\" in the log; its HQ from that type's recommended starts), a company color no company has (not black, white or gray), a CEO made up at random, the starting money, an HQ at its home port and that port's market region. Home ports come from the recommended starts by difficulty: Easy at fair or hard starts, Normal at great or fair, Hard at excellent or great; never a port a company has, and in a market region no company is in when there is one. - The host runs them (or single player), each thinking every few seconds of play (Easy about 15 s, Normal 7 s, Hard 3 s, spread out so they don't think at once). Each think, a rival: - Keeps afloat: in debt, it borrows a loan step if the bank will lend, else sells its least useful docked ship (never its last cargo ship); it never goes bankrupt or leaves the game. With money to spare it pays its loans back a step at a time. - Opens a market region when its level allows (the one with a port nearest its hubs), and founds a hub when its level allows (of the 30 biggest unlocked ports, the one its most expensive cargo model would earn most from; weighed up 4 ports a think so the game never stalls on it). - Buys ships: of the cargo models it can buy (unlocked, a slot free), the one that would pay for itself soonest on the best loop it can find from one of its hubs, within its difficulty's payback time (Easy 15 min, Normal 25, Hard 40, up to 4x that when it has plenty of money for the price; any ship that makes money while it has fewer than 3), keeping back half a load's money for each of its cargo ships (and Easy 30%, Normal 10% of the rest). A Hard rival buys up to 3 a think, and borrows for a ship that pays back in half its time (up to a loan step for every 5 company levels, and one to start). Each new ship sets off at once on its loop, with auto-recover on. A loop is 2 ports (and 3 for Normal and Hard), every leg one the model can sail between ports it has unlocked, judged by $ a second: each leg's best cargo at its own prices (sale bonuses, less the port fee away from its hubs and canal tolls), less fuel, repairs and crew wages, over sailing, port, lock and convoy time. A loop its other ships already sail counts 20% less for each, so its fleet spreads out. Easy picks one of its best 5 loops at random, Normal one of its best 2, Hard the best. - Gives any cargo ship without a route the best loop from where it is; Normal (every 5 minutes) and Hard (every 2) look at one of their ships and move it to a loop at least 25% better. - Spends (Easy every 4th think, Normal and Hard every 2nd): ships' upgrade points (Upgrade all), hub upgrade points (Sale prices, then Port stops, Fuel & repairs, Ship XP), mega upgrades it qualifies for, and industry buildings on its hubs' open plots (the commodity its ships trade most there). A hub upgrade, industry building or mega upgrade must cost no more than 10% (Easy), 15% (Normal) or 25% (Hard) of its money. - Buys service ships (every 3rd think): tugs once it has 4 cargo ships (up to 2, and one more for every 4 cargo ships), a recovery boat once it has 5 (the cheapest), and with seasonal closures an icebreaker (the cheapest that can work it) for each icebreaker area its routes' ports are in. - Saves up: when the ship it wants costs more than it has, its bank reserve keeps half its money back for it; otherwise its reserve is nothing. - Speed: what it works out is kept (its loops for 20-40 s of play, the ports a model can sail to from a port for 5 minutes, its fleet's counts for the think), at most 2 sets of loops are worked out afresh a think (the rest wait for a later one), and it looks at service ships every 3rd think: 3 Hard rivals take about 12% of the simulation's time over the first 40 minutes (about 5 s of 38). A rival's very first think in a session takes about 0.7 s while the sea lanes it looks at are first read in. - What the player sees: rivals' ships sail the map (fainter than the player's, like other players' in multiplayer; their popups are read-only, \"— Northstar Freight's ship\"), their HQ and hubs have rings in their color, and they're in Finances → Compare companies marked \"(AI · Hard)\". Their big moves go in the activity log in their color: \"Northstar Freight launched its first Feeder 100.\", opened up a region, founded a hub, built an industry building, mega-upgraded a model, bought its first recovery boat, stationed an icebreaker, and every 10 company levels. - The world's own features (storms, seasonal closures, ports shrinking late on) start by the highest level of the people playing: rivals' levels don't count. - Multiplayer: the host picks rivals when founding the game (the same New Company screen) and the host's game runs them; joined players see them like any company, and can't take one over (they're shown as taken and refused)."},{"p":"Rivals and multiplayer","t":"Multiplayer","u":"multiplayer.html","x":"- One player hosts a game from one of their Multiplayer saves and friends join it, each running their own company in the same world. The economy is shared (one set of markets that everyone's trades move, port sizes, storms, seasons and calendar). Every company has its own home port. Only the host sets the game speed (joined players' speed buttons are greyed out, showing the host's, and F does nothing for them). - Hosting: Multiplayer page → pick a slot (New Game to host, or a save) → Host Game dialog: the game's name in the games list (\"Name your game\"; the company's if left blank), an optional password, how many can play (2 to 8, the host included), and Over: Steam (the default when Steam is running) or Local network, or by address, and Filter: \"Filter bad words from ship names and chat\" (on by default). It reads through simple disguises (sh1t, f.u.c.k, f u c k, fuuuck) without catching place names (Scunthorpe, Fukuoka, Essex). The games list says \"Filtered\" for such games. The game then opens as usual and is open to join. - Over Steam: the game starts Steam as app 480, Valve's test app, until it has its own app id. Hosting opens a public Steam lobby holding the game's details, and joined games connect to the host through Steam's relay, so no addresses or port forwarding. The host's players list has Invite Friends (Steam's overlay; it only opens when Steam started the game, otherwise it says so and friends use the games list). Accepting a Steam invite, or Join Game on a friend in Steam's friends list, opens the join page and joins that game (while playing, the activity log says to leave this game first; back at the title screen it joins). Without Steam running, the Over choice is greyed out with the reason. - The game announces itself on the local network every second on port 24566. - Joining: Multiplayer page → Join a Game: your name (your Steam name to start with), then the games hosted on Steam and on your network, or join by address (an IP address and port; over the internet the host forwards port 24565). A game with a password asks for it; a wrong one, a full game or a different version of the game is turned away with the reason. Then pick a company: take back one of the game's companies no one is playing (with its level, money and home port; not an AI rival), or found a new one (name, color, its company type (a button per type in its color, its blurb on hover; Cargo & Ore to start with), and its HQ picked on a globe the same way as New Company: only that type's recommended starts and the Great Lakes ports no company has, colored by kind, with the start's note; the first free recommended start is picked to start with, and again when the type changes), then \"Next: Your CEO\": the same CEO page as a new game's, with Back or Found Company and Join; the host takes the CEO as sent, made safe. It starts with its home port's region unlocked, like a new game (saves where a joined company had none get it, and every region its fleet uses, when loaded). Founding at a taken home port, a port that isn't a starting port, or with a taken name says so and lets you pick again. - While playing: the host's game runs the world as in single player. About seven times a second it sends each joined game what changed (the clock, speed, weather, market and each company's money and XP every time; each moving ship's position, fuel, maintenance and timers; any ship whose state changed whole; and the rest of every ship, the port sizes and the companies' finances at least every 3 seconds), and the joined games move ships smoothly in between at their last speeds. A joined player's actions (buy, sell, routes, pause, the toggles, upgrades, hubs, mega upgrades, recoveries, icebreaker stations, renames, the bank reserve, their map and log settings) go to the host, which does them for that player's company (only for their own ships); what happens to their ships (sales, loads, breakdowns, losses, rescues, level-ups, canal tolls, port growth) comes back as their own activity log messages. - Other players' ships show on the map a little faded, with a flag in their company's color at the stern; their HQs and hubs have rings in their company's color (companies sharing a port ring it in turn). Clicking another player's ship shows it read-only (\"... — Host Co's ship\"), without its money or controls. Their route lanes show faintly in their company's color (see the Routes switch). The Ships, Finances, Hubs and Shop screens, the lost and at-risk markers and the alerts are your own company's. - The top bar shows \"Hosting · 3 players\" or \"Joined on Steam · 3 players\"; clicking it lists the players with Leave Game (and Invite Friends for a host on Steam) (the host saves and ends the game for everyone; a joined player leaves and their company carries on, to be taken back by joining again). The activity panel has two tabs, Activity and Chat: Chat lists the game's messages (\"Company: text\" in the speaker's company color, newest at the bottom; the last 200) with a \"Say something (Enter)\" line under them, and the tab counts messages that came while it wasn't open (\"Chat (2)\"). T opens the panel on the Chat tab ready to type (not while typing elsewhere); Esc leaves the chat line. Joins and leaves are logged in the host's Activity. If the host leaves or the connection drops, joined players are told so and go back to the title screen. - The host's save holds every company (each ship's company, and which company is the host's); joined games don't save. Storms, seasonal closures and port shrinking follow the highest company level in the game; breakdowns start at each company's own level; rescues use each company's own recovery boats and tugs. - Under the hood: Company holds everything a player owns. (that company's), while the sim pays, charges and levels each ship's own company. Net does the hosting, joining, updates, commands, events, chat and finding games, over Steam or ENet; past connecting, both are the same. The whole game a newcomer starts from goes in parts of at most 256 KB (Steam won't send a message over 512 KB)."},{"p":"","t":"About the game","u":"about.html","x":""},{"p":"About the game","t":"Explicitly NOT in beta","u":"explicitly-not-in-beta.html","x":"- Clans."},{"p":"About the game","t":"Copyright","u":"copyright.html","x":"- © 2026 Rory Ferrick, all rights reserved. A \"# Copyright (c) 2026 Rory Ferrick\" line heads every script and a comment every SVG. Both have a Licences link: a page with the game's copyright and every third-party notice in full, as the MIT and font licences require. Ship models have generic names."},{"p":"About the game","t":"Oil and gas","u":"oil-and-gas.html","x":"- Tankers companies drill and refine. The page over the map is Energy. - Goods: Oil is now Crude oil ($165 a ton) and Gas is LNG. New: Diesel $230, Premium gasoline $290, Jet fuel $380 (cheapest at the refining ports: Houston, Rotterdam, Antwerp, Singapore, Nhava Sheva, the Gulf; jet fuel most expensive at Hong Kong, New York, Yokohama and Incheon), LPG $160, Ammonia $260 (cheap at Port of Spain, the Gulf, the Black Sea and the Baltic), Hydrogen $520. The five tankers carry crude, diesel, gasoline and jet fuel (no longer gas). - Gas carriers, the Tankers type's second line: Gas Coaster (tier 1, 300 tons, $60,000) and Pressure Carrier (tier 2, 2,000 tons, $400,000) carry LPG and ammonia; Midsize Gas Carrier (tier 3, 8,000 tons, $2M), LNG Carrier (tier 4, 60,000 tons, $20M, Medium ports) and Q-Max (tier 5, 120,000 tons, $180M, Large ports) LNG and hydrogen too. Pale hulls with four spherical tank domes, crews in royal blue coveralls and hard hats. The Buffalo carries the three smallest. - Fields: 22 real offshore fields, 15 oil (Brent, Norwegian Sea, Gulf of Mexico deepwater, Bay of Campeche, Santos pre-salt, Guyana, Grand Banks, Niger Delta, Angola deepwater, Persian Gulf (Safaniya), Mumbai High, Bohai Bay, South China Sea, Bass Strait, Cook Inlet) and 7 gas (Qatar North Field, North West Shelf, Troll, Rovuma Basin, Gulf of Mexico shelf gas, Sakhalin, Leviathan), each with 4 to 10 slots and a richness (0.7 to 1.4). Slots are shared by every company, first come; a company may own half of a field's. - Rigs and platforms: an oil rig ($20M, from Tankers tier 3) pumps 120,000 tons of crude a year times its field's richness, and a tenth as much again of gas (three quarters LNG, a quarter LPG); a gas platform ($25M, tier 4) 100,000 tons of gas. Crews cost $60,000 every pay period each. What they make goes into the company's storage at the field (20,000 tons a rig, each good its share of it, so gas no one collects never stops the crude); what has no room is lost (the rating notices). Upgrades: More wells ($8M, +30%), Bigger storage ($5M, 60,000 tons), Storm hardening ($6M, half the odds of damage). A slot is sold back for half its rig's cost, or listed for other Tankers companies. - Storms: a storm over a field (strength over 0.3) shuts its rigs for the day; a strong one (over 0.6) damages one 35% of the time (half that hardened): damaged, it makes half; damaged twice, it stops (a red cross on its deck). Only a tug repairs it: Send a tug (the Energy page) sends the company's nearest free tug with the fuel to get there and back (tugs come with the ports on its routes), for $1.5M a step of damage; it sails out, works 2 days alongside and comes home. Rivals with no tug in reach now and then pay a contractor twice the fee. - Rig runs: a tanker or gas carrier sent on rig runs (a field's Rig runs card: a ship and a delivery port among the HQ, hubs, refinery ports and the field's nearest ports; it must have the fuel there and back on one tank) gets a route of just its delivery port and shuttles: out to the field, alongside the rigs loading what's stored (the good worth most at its port; a stop's time at least, waiting up to 3 days for a full hold), and back to its port, where its load sells or goes into the company's refinery there. Stop (or any new route) takes it off them once it's back. - Pipelines (tier 4): from a slot to the company's nearest HQ or hub within 300 nm by sea, $40M plus $100,000 a mile: its output goes straight into the company's store at that port (60,000 tons of room a piped rig), for its ships to load or a refinery there to run on. - Buildings, at any port in an unlocked region, one of each kind a port a company: Oil refinery ($30M, tier 2: crude to diesel, 95%, 1,500 tons a day), Gas refinery ($45M, tier 3: diesel to premium gasoline, 95%, 1,200 a day), Hydrogen & ammonia plant ($60M, tier 4: LNG to hydrogen at 60% or ammonia at 100%, set at each plant, 800 a day), Jet fuel plant ($70M, tier 5: gasoline to jet fuel, 95%, 1,000 a day). Workers cost $80,000 to $120,000 every pay period. Each has tanks of 20 days of its input and of its output; the company's store at the port holds what they hold, so at one port a refinery's diesel feeds a gas refinery. Upgrades: Bigger tanks (15% of its cost: twice the tanks), More throughput (30%: +25%), Automation (10%: half the wages). Pulled down for half its cost. Only the company's own ships bring input: unloading there, the tanks take what they have room for (no sale, but the delivery's XP all the same) and the rest sells; a ship whose next stop is a refinery port with room for at least half its hold buys that input where it is, ahead of the best trade. Its ships load what the buildings make, free (not the goods a building there takes in), and fill their own fuel free from the diesel in its store at a port, at the port's fuel price against the diesel's. - Unlocks go by the Tankers tiers, and are listed with the level-up's other unlocks. - The Tankers rating has a fourth part, Production, a quarter of its stars: rigs and refinery ports against 1 + level / 12 (up to 8), the share of the rigs working, the refineries' use lately, and the share of the output kept. - Map: each field a teal diamond (for Tankers companies), named from closer in; rigs and platforms on a ring round the field's end, sized in the world like the ships (at least 7 px): a grey deck edged in the owner's colour, a derrick or process modules, a helideck and a flickering flare (out while it's stopped or shut); pipelines dashed to their port in the owner's colour; refineries a little mark of tanks and a flare stack down and right of their port, in the owner's colour. Clicking a field opens it on the Energy page. - The Energy page: All fields, My rigs, Slots for sale, My refineries. A field: its richness, nearest ports, the company's storage there, its rig runs (and sending another), and a card a slot (Put up, Buy from, the state of the company's own, its output a year, Send a tug, upgrades, Lay a pipeline, List, Sell it off). A port: a card a building (Build; its input and output, rate, tanks, the plant's choice, upgrades, Pull down) and the tanks (each good, its tons against its room, its worth, Sell here). The port popup has a Refineries line with an Energy button; the ship popup says where a rig run goes and that its load came from the rigs. Finances rows: Rigs, refineries and pipelines built; Rigs and refineries sold; Energy costs (crews, workers, repairs); Energy tank sales. AI rivals put up rigs on the field nearest their HQ, send their ships on rig runs, build an oil refinery at their HQ, buy listed slots and send tugs. A tutorial tip (\"Our own oil\") once the refinery unlocks. - The hub industry building for crude is now the Oil terminal (it was the Oil refinery), and it and the Gas terminal cover the new grades."},{"p":"About the game","t":"Research","u":"research.html","x":"- The seventh company type, Research. With seven types the picks run to level 90. - Two lines, five tiers each: Survey vessels (category survey: Survey Launch 150 m, Hydrographic Cutter 600 m, Coastal Research Ship 1,500 m, Ocean Survey Vessel 2,500 m, Global Research Vessel 4,000 m; 2 to 5 gear slots) and Deep-sea motherships (category mothership: Dive Support Vessel 3,000 m, ROV Mothership 4,500 m, Deep Submergence Tender 6,000 m, Expedition Mothership 8,000 m, Hadal Explorer 11,000 m; 2 to 6 slots). Prices $90,000 to $220M, slower than cargo ships, reaching 700 to 9,500 nm from port. Art: white houses forward, an orange stern A-frame, a yellow ROV, crewed subs in their cradles, a crane, and a helideck on the two biggest motherships; crews in hi-vis (survey) and oilskin orange (motherships). A skin in every pack (gravy boat and cornucopia, glove and helmet, ghost ship and cauldron, sled and sleigh). - Gear (fitted in port into a ship's slots, a share of the ship's price; taken off for half back): Sonar suite (10%: needed to find wrecks and map the deeps; a fifth fewer days on station; reveals hidden sites nearby), ROV (15%), Crewed submersible (30%, motherships only: needed below 9,000 m; half again the XP), Ice hull (20%: polar sites; goes through sea ice without an icebreaker), Coring rig & wet lab (8%: vents, seeps, cold corals, sampling contracts), Dive team (5%: shallow wrecks, reefs and sunken cities to 60 m), Film crew (5%: documentaries pay half again; 10% more XP). - 215 real sites in 9 categories: Trenches & deeps (24: Challenger Deep, Horizon Deep, Brownson Deep, Molloy Deep...), Shipwrecks (25: Titanic, Endurance, Erebus and Terror, the San Jose galleon, Antikythera...), Vents & seeps (23: Lost City, the Galapagos Rift, Beebe...), Seamounts & ridges (24), Reefs & kelp forests (25), Sunken cities (23: Pavlopetri, Thonis-Heracleion, Port Royal...), Aircraft & war relics (24: Truk Lagoon, Scapa Flow, Bismarck, USS Indianapolis, Air France 447...), Canyons, caves & blue holes (23) and Wildlife & mysteries (24: the Bloop, the Baltic Sea Anomaly, the Ogasawara giant squid...). 37 famous ones are known from the start; the rest show as search areas (a faint ring and \"?\", off the real spot) till revealed: a sonar ship on station within 450 nm, a site done within 450 nm, or a contract there. Each site's end of its lanes is \"@res_SITE\". A site needs a ship deep enough with its needs fitted (trenches sonar and an ROV, or a sub below 9,000 m; wrecks and relics sonar and divers to 60 m, else an ROV; vents an ROV and the lab; polar sites, beyond 66 N or 60 S, an ice hull). - An expedition (the Research page, or the ship popup's Expeditions button): a docked research ship within its reach (and with the fuel there and back on a tank) is sent to a site; its route becomes its port, and once its stop is done it sets out. On station it burns 30% of its fuel burn and wears; a storm pauses the survey; each day of dives may fail (6% x (1.5 - maintenance): a day lost), and below 6,000 m a ship under 50% maintenance may lose its ROV or sub (12% a day; without the gear the site needs it turns home). It turns back early if short of fuel or hull to get home. Then it sails home and lies idle (\"no expedition\"). A ship can be called home, or moved to another port (its HQ, hubs or the 30 nearest within its reach) to set out from. - Personal research pays nothing; contracts pay. The Contracts board is shared by every company, first come: 14 offers, new ones every 4 days, gone after 40 untaken. Kinds: Science & mapping (sites), Industry surveys (oil and gas fields and fishing grounds: cable, pipeline, wind farm and seabed surveys, sonar and sometimes the lab, 200 m), Salvage & recovery (wrecks, relics and ruins: 1.7x pay, 20% chance only a quarter is recovered), Documentary (famous sites, half again with a film crew) and Search (hidden sites). Clients are fictional (Halvorsen Institute of Oceanography, Tidewater Cable Co., Brass Bell Salvage, Blue Depths Films...). Pay: 3% of the price of the cheapest research ship that could do it, a day, for its days on station plus 8, x the kind x difficulty (so a contract pays back about a fifth of that ship). Taken, it's due in 30 days plus twice its days; a company holds 2 plus one for every two research ships. Missed or given up, it fails: no pay, 10% of it as a penalty, and the rating notices. - Perks: each category's at 25%, 50% and 100% of its sites done: Trenches XP +5/10/20%, Shipwrecks breakdowns -10/20/35%, Vents fuel -4/8/15%, Seamounts speed +2/4/8%, Reefs catch +10/20/35%, Sunken cities port fees -10/20/35%, Relics repairs -10/20/35%, Canyons wear -5/10/20%, Wildlife sale prices +2/4/8%. Local ones: +25% catch on fishing grounds within 400 nm of a surveyed wreck, reef, seamount or relic, and every site done within 350 nm of a port counts as a sight for its Expedition cruises. - The Research rating's four parts, a quarter each: Contracts (done on time against failed, and how many), Discoveries (sites done, by difficulty and firsts twice, against 4 + 1.5 a level since taking Research), Fleet & gear (maintenance and gear slots filled), Safety & reliability (failed dives, lost gear, breakdowns and losses against dives). - Site pictures are drawn from each category's template with the site's own details, seeded by its id. The map's Research switch shows the known sites as diamonds in their category's colour (gold-edged and ticked once done, ringed where a ship is bound) and the hidden ones' search areas; clicking one opens its card. A discovery shows a card (FIRST DISCOVERY or DISCOVERY) with its picture and fact. Shop cards show depth (and how many sites it reaches) and gear slots. Finances rows: Research contracts, Research gear, Contract penalties. Three tutorial tips (Into the deep, Gear and expeditions, Paid work). - AI rivals with Research buy a research ship now and then (the deepest they can afford twice over, one plus one per 10 levels), fit gear, take contracts their idle ships can do and send them, else to the nearest known site they haven't done. Multiplayer: the board and first discoveries are the host's (sent with each update); sending, recalling, moving, fitting, taking and giving up are host commands; discoveries and mishaps go to their ship's player. - Industry surveys at oil and gas fields don't yet unlock or cheapen rig slots (planned)."}];
